valuation)*.  \n- **CSCO +1.84% (close $120.30)** — named by CNAS research as a key U.S. networking vendor embedded in sovereign AI projects alongside NVDA, AMD, INTC, DELL, HPE, ORCL, AMZN, underlining its role in national AI networks — *Benzinga (sovereign AI)*.  \n- **AVGO -0.18% (close $412.22)** — slightly red despite co‑funding **$125M UCLA Semiconductor Hub**; Broadcom’s Charlie Kawwas said it builds an ecosystem spanning “foundries, packaging, equipment, and cloud infrastructure” and helps train talent — *Benzinga (UCLA hub)*.  \n\n### AI Compute – GPUs, CPUs & Accelerators\n\n- **NVDA -2.27% (close $215.23)** — on top of fundamentals above, sentiment piece noted NVDA trading near **$5.4T** valuation, yet a WSJ column called it “cheap” relative to growth; prediction markets assign **66%** probability it is largest company at year‑end vs **19%** for GOOGL, **8%** for AAPL; article notes “marked apathy” per UBS and rotation toward INTC, which has more than tripled YTD — *Benzinga (prediction‑market/valuation)*.  \n- **AMD +4.98% (close $466.49)** — in addition to Taiwan plan, Webull CEO said ~**9%** of users now hold at least one semi/AI stock (vs 3% two years ago) and name‑checked **AMD and MU** as core AI retail holdings, with flow broadening up/down the supply chain — *Benzinga (Webull interview)*.  \n- **INTC +1.26% (close $119.57)** — Bloomberg‑sourced report via Benzinga said INTC is exploring acquisition of AI inference startup **Tenstorrent**, potentially valuing it **> $5B**, with QCOM also in talks; positioned as part of a race to secure AI tech to rival NVDA/AMD — *Benzinga (Tenstorrent report)*.  \n\n### Memory & Interconnect\n\n- **MU +0.65% (close $758.94)** — started manufacturing **1α DRAM** at its **Manassas, VA** fab, part of a **>$2B** expansion within a planned **$200B** U.S. investment; expected to **quadruple DDR4 wafer supply**, with qualified output by end‑2026; stronger note: MU trades **16.3% above 20‑day SMA, 49% above 50‑day, 138.9% above 200‑day**, near **$818.67** 52‑wk high, with Citi PT **$840**, Melius **$1,100**, Mizuho **$800**, EPS estimate **$19.11 vs $1.91 prior**, revenue **$33.60B vs $9.30B**, and quant scores Value 15.69, Growth 83.62, Quality 97.51, Momentum 99.45 — *Benzinga (DRAM/manufacturing & technicals)*.  \n- **GFS +6.28% (close $85.67)** — benefitted from Commerce Dept’s **$375M** CHIPS/quantum LOI plus **~1%** U.S. equity stake and launch of **Quantum Technology Solutions**; weekly gainer list cites **+23.70%** for May 18–22; also a co‑funder of UCLA hub — *Benzinga (large‑cap gainers, quantum funding, UCLA hub)*.  \n- **AMKR -0.42% (close $65.67)** — modestly red despite CEO comments that advanced packaging is “a major bottleneck” for AI chips and that Amkor is working with **AMD** on advanced packages and is involved with **TSMC Arizona** — *Benzinga (AMD piece)*.  \n\n### Wireless & Mobile\n\n- **QCOM +12.48% (close $236.80)** — Benzinga cited Qualcomm among sharp SMH gainers as Dow hit records; article attributed the move to expanded **Snapdragon Digital Chassis** partnership with Stellantis atop prior Q2 beat, dividend hike and buyback expansion; options recap flagged 17 contracts of **$250 strike June 18, 2026** calls (~**$26.8K** notional) trading bullishly, with **13,482** OI and **8,453** contracts traded at that strike — *Benzinga (market summary, options flow)*.  \n- **INDI +13.38% (close $4.76)** — intraday, Indie Semiconductor was listed among top IT gainers, “increased **21.53% to $5.08**,” in a high‑beta move without a fundamental catalyst — *Benzinga (IT movers)*.  \n- **MXL -1.65% (close $97.67)** — underperformed peers; no ticker‑specific news in the set — *Benzinga (Wireless & Mobile)*.  \n\n### Analog & Mixed-Signal\n\n- **TXN +4.20% (close $309.65)** — Seaport Global upgraded to **Buy** with **$400 PT**, highlighting opportunity in analog; same name cited as a driver of SMH outperformance alongside QCOM, AMD, INTC — *Benzinga (upgrade, markets Friday)*.  \n- **ADI +3.32% (close $396.43)** — Q2 print: **adj. EPS $3.09 vs $2.90 est**, revenue **$3.62B vs $3.51B est**, **+37.2% Y/Y**; used as benchmark for mixed‑signal recovery — *Benzinga (tech wrap)*.  \n- **VSH +12.97% (close $47.38)** — intraday noted as gaining **9.5% to $46.18** among “other big stocks recording gains” in a Dow +300pt session — *Benzinga (winners list)*.  \n\n### Chip Equipment\n\n- **AMAT +0.92% (close $431.48)** — co‑founded **$125M UCLA Semiconductor Hub** with AVGO, GFS, META, SNPS over 5 years; CEO Gary Dickerson said academia‑industry collaboration is key as complexity rises and that the hub targets AI‑native hardware, advanced packaging, thermal, ultra‑broadband and next‑gen computing; described as aiding commercialization of breakthrough tech — *Benzinga (UCLA hub)*.  \n- **ASML +2.85%**, **KLAC +2.34%**, **LRCX +1.85%**, **ENTG +2.64%**, **UCTT +3.69%**, **ICHR +4.69%**, **ACMR +3.00%**, **VECO +2.52%**, **ACLS +2.68%** — participated in AI‑capex‑driven bid; UCLA hub framed as positive capex signal for advanced nodes and packaging — *Benzinga (Chip Equipment)*.  \n\n### Fab & Foundry\n\n- **TSM -0.95% (close $403.86)** — underperformance contextualized above; additional backdrop includes: JASM (Japan unit) reported its first quarterly profit since mass production began end‑2024, and TSM is planning **1nm** development while 2nm enters production later this year; USTR Greer said **“no immediate tariffs coming on semiconductors”**, though the tariff probe remains — *Benzinga (tech wrap, macro events)*.  \n- **GFS +6.28% (close $85.67)**, **ASX +6.62% (close $34.81)**, **TSEM +2.11%**, **SLAB +0.16%**, **SITM +1.89%** — benefitted from CHIPS quantum funding (GFS), UCLA hub (GFS) and advanced‑packaging bottleneck commentary (ASX/TSEM read‑through from AMKR/AMD) — *Benzinga (Fab & Foundry, Memory)*.  \n\n### Power & Wide-Bandgap\n\n- **NVTS +18.84% (close $29.21)** — intraday cited as having “jumped **10.2% to $26.86**” among big movers in a risk‑on tape; SEC filing detailed May 18 settlement with Live Oak Sponsor: transfer of **726,225 sponsor earnout shares**, in addition to **421,000** previously earned; **115,775** sponsor earnout shares forfeited and both parties provided general release of claims — *Benzinga (winners list, SEC‑settlement note)*.  \n- **SMTC +6.36% (close $154.77)** — Oppenheimer reiterated **Outperform** and raised PT from **$110 to $150**; options “whale” flow in **June 18, 2026 $200 calls**, **90 contracts**, **$76.5K** notional, with **60** prior OI and **6,281** contracts traded at that strike — *Benzinga (PT hike, options recap)*.  \n- **WOLF +0.30% (close $69.77)**, **AEHR +5.67% (close $94.31)** — both featured as high‑beta gainers in the same “big stocks moving higher” list, with WOLF intraday “**+8.7% to $75.63**” — *Benzinga (winners list, Power & WBG)*.  \n\n### Test & Advanced Packaging\n\n- **LSCC +3.49% (close $142.99)** — Deutsche Bank kept **Buy** and lifted PT from **$150 to $175**; prior close noted at **$139.35**; used as read‑through for demand in FPGAs and advanced packaging/test — *Benzinga (PT note, analyst round‑up)*.  \n- **TER -0.32% (close $355.32)** — only name in group closing red despite broader semi strength; no ticker‑specific catalyst — *Benzinga (Test & Packaging)*.  \n\n### Specialty Materials & IP Licensing\n\n- **GSIT +3.39% (close $9.58)** — Benzinga listed GSI Technology among big gainers, “gained **11.7% to $10.34**” intraday during Friday’s risk‑on session — *Benzinga (winners list)*.  \n- **ATOM +9.65%**, **NVMI +1.36%**, **RMBS +1.13%**, **OLED +2.90%**, **AXTI +12.28%**, **ALMU -2.86%** — moved with strong semi/IP tape; no name‑specific articles except GSIT — *Benzinga (Specialty Materials & IP)*.  \n\n### Unassigned (no theme/sector)\n\n- **NIO -6.96% to $5.21** — post‑earnings selloff despite **Q1 revenue +112% Y/Y to $3.7B vs $3.55B est** and deliveries **+98% to 83,465**; still posted **$48M net loss**; ADRs roughly **26% below April high**; Benzinga linked pressure to CSRC crackdown on cross‑border trading (including **FUTU**) and heightened sector risk ahead of **LI** and **XPEV** prints — *Benzinga (EV/China recap)*.  \n- **ROST roughly +7% to ~$233 (52‑wk high)** — Q1 beat and FY26 EPS raise prompted PT hikes — *Benzinga (Ross Stores)*.  \n- **ZM +10.4% to $106.89** — on Q1 beat and raised FY27 guide; analysts increased P	{"asof": "2026-05-25", "tldr": [{"text": "Risk-on session with Dow at record highs as flows rotated within AI from GPUs into CPUs, servers, networking, power, and software; semis and software outperformed while some mega-cap platforms lagged.", "tickers": ["SPY", "QQQ", "SOXX", "IGV", "XLK"]}, {"text": "Nvidia sold off post-earnings despite a blowout quarter and major capital-return package, as investors took profits and rotated into secondary AI hardware and infrastructure.", "tickers": ["NVDA"]}, {"text": "AI CPUs, RF, analog, and AI networking rallied, led by AMD, QCOM, TXN, MRVL, and Nokia on AI infrastructure, auto, and networking optimism plus analyst upgrades.", "tickers": ["AMD", "QCOM", "TXN", "MRVL", "NOK"]}, {"text": "AI server and datacenter power names surged on backlog commentary, sovereign-AI demand, and trillions in forecasted AI infrastructure spend.", "tickers": ["DELL", "HPE", "SMCI", "VRT", "ETN"]}, {"text": "Cybersecurity and broader software outperformed into upcoming earnings, with ZS and CRM highlighted as key catalysts.", "tickers": ["ZS", "CRM", "PANW", "CRWD", "SNOW"]}, {"text": "Internet and platform names were mixed, with SPOT and creator platforms strong while GOOGL and several mega-caps underperformed amid antitrust and privacy headlines.", "tickers": ["GOOGL", "META", "AAPL", "SPOT", "NFLX", "UBER"]}], "topics": [{"name": "Macro & Indices", "bullets": [{"text": "Broad U.S. equities closed higher with SPY +0.39% and QQQ +0.42% as the Dow hit record levels, supported by strength in technology, PCs, and semiconductors.", "tickers": ["SPY", "QQQ"]}, {"text": "Sector leadership came from semiconductors and technology, with SOXX +2.33%, IGV +1.65%, and XLK +1.00% outperforming the broader market.", "tickers": ["SOXX", "IGV", "XLK"]}]}, {"name": "AI Rotation & Semiconductors", "bullets": [{"text": "Flows rotated within AI from GPU leaders into CPUs, servers, networking, and datacenter power as semis led the tape with strength in AI compute, RF, analog, and equipment.", "tickers": ["SOXX", "NVDA", "AMD", "INTC", "QCOM", "TXN"]}, {"text": "Nvidia fell 2.27% despite FQ1 revenue of $81.6B (+85% Y/Y), Q2 revenue guide near $91B, a 25x dividend hike to $0.25 per quarter, and an $80B buyback expansion, with commentary highlighting tight HBM supply, supply-constrained VeraRubin, and China AI-chip revenue going from ~90% share to zero under U.S. controls.", "tickers": ["NVDA"]}, {"text": "AMD gained 4.98% after Lisa Su outlined plans to invest over $10B into Taiwan’s AI ecosystem, ramp Venice CPUs on TSMC 2nm, and projected AI CPU demand growing above 35% annually over five years, while noting China is ~20% of revenue but not a driver for advanced China chip sales.", "tickers": ["AMD", "TSM"]}, {"text": "TSMC lagged at -0.95% despite being AMD’s 2nm partner, as notes highlighted Asia’s AI trade broadening beyond TSM into memory, CPUs, robotics, and infrastructure, with Street models calling for strong July earnings growth and PTs clustered around the low-to-mid $400s.", "tickers": ["TSM", "AMD"]}, {"text": "Qualcomm rallied 12.48% as one of the largest semiconductor movers, helped by an expanded Snapdragon Digital Chassis deal with Stellantis on top of a prior Q2 beat, dividend hike, and buyback, with options flow active in June 2026 $250 calls.", "tickers": ["QCOM", "STLA"]}, {"text": "Analog and power names outperformed, including TXN +4.20% on a Seaport Global upgrade to Buy with a $400 PT, and broad gains in ADI, NXPI, ON, and others tied to AI and industrial demand.", "tickers": ["TXN", "ADI", "NXPI", "ON", "MPWR", "MCHP", "ALGM", "DIOD", "POWI", "VSH", "MTSI", "STM", "AOSL"]}, {"text": "AI networking and communications semis such as MRVL, ANET, CSCO, and NOK rose on expectations for AI-driven network build-outs, with Marvell seeing multiple PT hikes up to $215 and Nokia benefitting from an AI Networking Innovation Lab launch and an FCC approval.", "tickers": ["MRVL", "ANET", "AAOI", "ERIC", "CSCO", "NOK"]}, {"text": "Foundry and packaging plays were mixed as TSM and UMC lagged while GFS and ASX advanced on CHIPS/quantum funding, UCLA hub participation, and read-through from advanced-packaging bottleneck commentary.", "tickers": ["TSM", "UMC", "GFS", "ASX", "TSEM", "SLAB", "SITM"]}]}, {"name": "AI Infrastructure: Servers, Power & Sovereign AI", "bullets": [{"text": "AI server OEMs DELL, HPE, and SMCI surged on AI server backlog commentary and sovereign-AI demand, with Dell hitting record highs ahead of earnings on expectations for $35.75B in revenue (+52% Y/Y) and positioning versus more than $725B in 2026 hyperscaler capex.", "tickers": ["DELL", "HPE", "SMCI"]}, {"text": "HPE gained 10.98% after CNAS research showed an NVDA-led U.S. stack, including HPE and DELL, is supplying the majority of sovereign AI projects globally, highlighting recurring server demand from national AI builds.", "tickers": ["HPE", "DELL", "NVDA"]}, {"text": "Datacenter power and cooling names VRT and ETN moved higher as Vertiv was highlighted with a 1,110% 5-year gain, expected revenue growth of 35% this year and 28% next year, and tailwinds from parabolic AI demand and grid constraints.", "tickers": ["VRT", "ETN"]}, {"text": "CSCO was cited as a key networking vendor embedded in sovereign AI projects alongside NVDA, AMD, INTC, DELL, HPE, ORCL, and AMZN, underscoring its role in national AI networks.", "tickers": ["CSCO", "NVDA", "AMD", "INTC", "DELL", "HPE", "ORCL", "AMZN"]}]}, {"name": "Software, Cybersecurity & SaaS", "bullets": [{"text": "Software rallied with IGV +1.65% as ZS, TEAM, SNOW, INTU, PANW, CRWD, CRM, NOW, DDOG, MDB, ORCL, and ADBE broadly finished higher while MSFT was modestly lower.", "tickers": ["IGV", "ZS", "TEAM", "SNOW", "INTU", "PANW", "CRWD", "CRM", "NOW", "DDOG", "MDB", "ESTC", "ORCL", "ADBE", "MSFT"]}, {"text": "Salesforce rose 2.94% into earnings on expectations for Q1 revenue of $11.06B (+12.48% Y/Y) and Q2 revenue of $11.36B (+11% Y/Y), with Agentforce + Data 360 ARR up 200% Y/Y to $2.9B and the stock framed as a dual Dow/Nasdaq 100 catalyst.", "tickers": ["CRM"]}, {"text": "Zscaler climbed 7.20% leading the cyber cohort into its upcoming earnings slot, with PANW and CRWD also bid and Wells Fargo lifting CRWD’s price target to $700 while flagging overbought technicals.", "tickers": ["ZS", "PANW", "CRWD"]}, {"text": "Intuit rebounded 3.61% after a prior selloff tied to earnings, guidance, and a 17% workforce reduction, and was cited as an example of downside risk even among perceived AI software winners.", "tickers": ["INTU"]}]}, {"name": "Internet, Platforms & Consumer Tech", "bullets": [{"text": "Internet and platform stocks were mixed, with AAPL, META, TTD, RBLX, SPOT, and PINS higher while GOOGL, AMZN, MSFT, NFLX, SNAP, and UBER underperformed amid regulatory, privacy, and M&A concerns.", "tickers": ["AAPL", "META", "TTD", "RBLX", "SPOT", "PINS", "GOOGL", "AMZN", "MSFT", "NFLX", "SNAP", "UBER"]}, {"text": "Alphabet fell 1.70% after appealing a 2024 U.S. antitrust ruling over default-search deals that found it illegally maintained search dominance and proposed remedies including mandated data-sharing with rivals and AI firms.", "tickers": ["GOOGL", "AAPL"]}, {"text": "Meta inched up 0.31% as it reiterated a 2026 capex plan of $125–$145B focused on AI, detailed deployment of over 1 GW of custom silicon co-developed with AVGO and others, and co-launched a $125M UCLA Semiconductor Hub while also facing a Texas lawsuit over WhatsApp encryption claims.", "tickers": ["META", "AVGO", "AMAT", "GFS", "SNPS"]}, {"text": "Apple gained 1.15% after unveiling AI-driven accessibility and Apple Intelligence features across hardware platforms and was cited as having bucked a broader smartphone slump amid weak Samsung April unit data.", "tickers": ["AAPL", "SSNLF"]}, {"text": "Spotify jumped 5.49% on multiple price target increases, including a JPMorgan hike to $650, and a Live Nation ticketing partnership called \\"Reserved by Spotify\\" that integrates SPOT into live event ticket access.", "tickers": ["SPOT", "LYV"]}]}, {"name": "Communications, Networking & Chip Equipment", "bullets": [{"text": "AI networking beneficiaries including MRVL, ANET, AAOI, ERIC, CSCO, and NOK were broadly bid on expectations for next-gen AI networks and increased data traffic.", "tickers": ["MRVL", "ANET", "AAOI", "ERIC", "CSCO", "NOK"]}, {"text": "Marvell extended a nine-week run to fresh highs as multiple brokers raised price targets up to $215, with Street estimates calling for EPS of $0.75 and revenue of $2.40B into a confirmed May 27 print, even as valuation metrics flagged elevated risk.", "tickers": ["MRVL"]}, {"text": "Nokia gained 9.27% to 52-week highs after launching an AI Networking Innovation Lab and securing an FCC approval for in-home broadband devices, despite trading at a rich P/E versus modest EPS expectations and consensus price targets.", "tickers": ["NOK"]}, {"text": "UCLA announced a $125M AI Semiconductor Hub co-funded by AVGO, AMAT, META, SNPS, and GFS to accelerate AI-native hardware, advanced packaging, thermal, and ultra-broadband communications, viewed as a positive capex signal for chip equipment and advanced nodes.", "tickers": ["AVGO", "AMAT", "META", "SNPS", "GFS"]}, {"text": "Wafer-fab tools and materials names like ASML, LRCX, AMAT, KLAC, ENTG, UCTT, ICHR, ACMR, VECO, and ACLS traded higher on AI-related capex momentum and the UCLA hub announcement.", "tickers": ["ASML", "LRCX", "AMAT", "KLAC", "ENTG", "UCTT", "ICHR", "ACMR", "VECO", "ACLS"]}, {"text": "Optical names CIEN, LITE, and COHR were softer despite broader AI networking optimism, while AVGO slightly underperformed even as it co-funded the UCLA Semiconductor Hub.", "tickers": ["CIEN", "LITE", "COHR", "AVGO"]}]}, {"name": "AI Compute: GPUs, CPUs & Accelerators", "bullets": [{"text": "Nvidia’s valuation and market leadership were debated as prediction markets assigned a 66% probability it will be the largest company by year-end versus 19% for GOOGL and 8% for AAPL, while commentary highlighted rotation toward INTC after its strong year-to-date performance.", "tickers": ["NVDA", "GOOGL", "AAPL", "INTC"]}, {"text": "Bloomberg-sourced reports indicated Intel is exploring an acquisition of AI inference startup Tenstorrent, potentially valuing it above $5B, with Qualcomm also in talks as part of a broader race to secure AI acceleration technology.", "tickers": ["INTC", "QCOM"]}, {"text": "AI and edge compute beneficiaries such as ARM, ALAB, CEVA, AMBA, SIMO, AIP, and others traded higher alongside the sector as investors broadened exposure beyond GPU leaders.", "tickers": ["ARM", "ALAB", "CEVA", "AMBA", "SIMO", "AIP"]}]}, {"name": "Memory, Packaging & Specialty Materials", "bullets": [{"text": "Micron advanced on the start of 1α DRAM manufacturing at its Manassas, VA fab as part of a >$2B expansion within a planned $200B U.S. investment, with expectations to quadruple DDR4 wafer supply and technicals showing the stock near 52-week highs with aggressive earnings and revenue growth estimates.", "tickers": ["MU"]}, {"text": "GlobalFoundries gained 6.28% on a $375M CHIPS and quantum funding LOI plus a ~1% U.S. equity stake and the launch of Quantum Technology Solutions, and was also noted as a UCLA hub co-funder and weekly gainer.", "tickers": ["GFS"]}, {"text": "Amkor was modestly lower despite its CEO emphasizing that advanced packaging remains a major bottleneck for AI chips and highlighting work with AMD and TSMC Arizona on advanced packages.", "tickers": ["AMKR", "AMD", "TSM"]}, {"text": "Memory and interconnect-related names including RMBS, GSIT, MRAM, TSEM, ASX, and others were generally strong, aided by CHIPS-related headlines and packaging bottleneck themes.", "tickers": ["RMBS", "GSIT", "MRAM", "TSEM", "ASX"]}, {"text": "Specialty materials and IP licensing stocks such as ATOM, NVMI, OLED, AXTI, and GSIT moved higher in a strong semi/IP tape, while ALMU was the notable decliner without a specific catalyst.", "tickers": ["ATOM", "NVMI", "OLED", "AXTI", "GSIT", "ALMU"]}]}, {"name": "Power & Wide-Bandgap", "bullets": [{"text": "Power and wide-bandgap beneficiaries including NVTS, SMTC, AEHR, and WOLF outperformed as investors looked further down the AI hardware stack into power conversion and testing.", "tickers": ["NVTS", "SMTC", "AEHR", "WOLF"]}, {"text": "Navitas surged 18.84% after an SEC filing detailed a May 18 settlement with Live Oak Sponsor involving the transfer of 726,225 sponsor earnout shares, forfeiture of 115,775 sponsor earnout shares, and mutual release of claims.", "tickers": ["NVTS"]}, {"text": "Semtech gained 6.36% following an Oppenheimer price target hike from $110 to $150 and bullish options flow in June 2026 $200 calls.", "tickers": ["SMTC"]}]}, {"name": "Wireless & Mobile", "bullets": [{"text": "RF, mobile, and auto-semi names QCOM, SWKS, QRVO, INDI, SYNA, and HIMX rallied strongly as part of the broader semi move, with INDI named among top IT gainers on a high-beta intraday spike without a fundamental catalyst.", "tickers": ["QCOM", "SWKS", "QRVO", "INDI", "SYNA", "HIMX"]}, {"text": "MaxLinear underperformed at -1.65% despite strength in the broader RF and connectivity complex, with no name-specific news cited.", "tickers": ["MXL"]}]}, {"name": "Test & Advanced Packaging", "bullets": [{"text": "Lattice Semiconductor gained 3.49% after Deutsche Bank reiterated Buy and raised its price target from $150 to $175, with the name used as a read-through for FPGA demand and advanced packaging and test.", "tickers": ["LSCC"]}, {"text": "Test