2026-06-02	daily	benzinga	complete	### 🗓 2026-06-02 — Pre-Market Brief\n\n**One-liner**: HPE smashes Q2 estimates (EPS $0.79 vs $0.53 est, rev $10.68B vs $9.79B) and surges 36% after hours; GOOGL announces $80B equity raise including $10B Berkshire placement; Anthropic files confidential S-1; NVDA unveils Arm-based RTX Spark PC chip at Computex sending ARM +16%, INTC -9%, QCOM -9%; oil spikes 8% as Iran suspends message exchanges with U.S.\n\n---\n\n### ⚡ Top Movers At a Glance\n\n| Ticker | Move | Cap | Catalyst | Theme |\n|--------|------|-----|----------|-------|\n| **HPE** | +11.0% (AH +36%) | $62B | Q2 EPS $0.79 vs $0.53 est; rev $10.68B vs $9.79B; raised FY26 guide | AI Infrastructure |\n| **ARM** | +16.4% | $435B | NVDA RTX Spark superchip uses Arm-based N1X CPU | AI Compute |\n| **DELL** | +11.9% | $310B | Continued momentum post Q1 beat; multiple PT raises to $500 | AI Infrastructure |\n| **ORCL** | +12.2% | $714B | Broad SaaS/AI infra rotation; CEO says demand outpaces supply | Enterprise SaaS / Cloud |\n| **NOW** | +9.9% | $140B | SaaS sector re-rating post SNOW earnings + NVDA Computex | Enterprise SaaS |\n| **CRM** | +9.3% | $172B | $5B Anthropic stake valuation; $2B France AI investment; SaaS rotation | Enterprise SaaS |\n| **MU** | +8.9% | $1.2T | Goldman sees memory shortage through 2028; Raymond James PT to $1,100 | Memory |\n| **INTC** | -9.2% | $550B | NVDA RTX Spark directly challenges x86 PC dominance | Semiconductors |\n| **QCOM** | -8.9% | $241B | NVDA Spark overshadows Snapdragon PC ambitions | Semiconductors |\n| **MDB** | +20.9% | $33B | SaaS short-covering wave post-SNOW blowout + AI data layer re-rating | SaaS / Data |\n| **TWLO** | +20.2% | $35B | SaaS sector-wide momentum; no company-specific catalyst | SaaS |\n| **CRWV** | +16.4% | $68B | First to validate NVDA Vera Rubin NVL72; Huang endorsement | AI Cloud |\n| **NBIS** | +18.6% | $64B | Huang praised Nebius at Computex; 21% short interest | AI Cloud |\n| **OKTA** | +13.6% | $23B | Continued post-Q1 beat momentum (+52% 5D) | Cybersecurity / SaaS |\n| **DDOG** | +13.0% | $99B | SaaS re-rating wave; no company-specific news | Observability / SaaS |\n| **NET** | +12.8% | $96B | SaaS rotation; no company-specific catalyst | SaaS |\n| **ZS** | +12.5% | $25B | Guggenheim upgrade to Buy, PT $214 (was Neutral) | Cybersecurity |\n| **SNOW** | +11.3% | $97B | Continued momentum; +64% 5D post Q1 blowout; Wedbush PT $280 | Data Cloud / SaaS |\n| **FLNC** | +43.8% | $5.0B | Siemens-NVDA AI data center reference design includes Fluence batteries | Energy Storage |\n| **TMHC** | +21.6% | $6.7B | Berkshire Hathaway to acquire for $72.50/share cash (~$6.8B) | Homebuilders |\n| **SAIC** | +10.7% | $5.0B | Q1 EPS $3.23 vs $2.29 est; rev $1.91B vs $1.82B; raised FY27 EPS guide | Defense IT |\n| **SPCE** | +19.8% | $469M | Glide test success; legal settlement; SpaceX IPO hype | Space |\n| **RKLB** | -11.9% | $71B | Space sector profit-taking; Deutsche Bank downgrade to Hold | Space |\n| **CRDO** | -0.8% (AH -14%) | $42B | Q4 EPS $1.16 vs $1.03 est beat; but FY27 >80% growth guide disappointed | AI Connectivity |\n| **GOOGL** | — (AH -2%) | $4.2T | $80B equity raise announced; $10B Berkshire private placement | Mega-Cap / AI Capex |\n| **META** | -5.1% | — | Subscription tier pivot; sector rotation away from comms services | Social / Advertising |\n| **PANW** | +8.1% | $205B | Pre-earnings momentum; reports FQ3 after close Tue ⚠️ | Cybersecurity |\n\n---\n\n### 🧭 Narrative Threads\n\n**NVDA Computex Reshapes the PC and AI Infrastructure Map** `AI Compute` → new\n\nJensen Huang's Computex 2026 keynote was the session's single most impactful event, radiating across semiconductors, software, cloud, and PC OEMs.\n\n- **NVDA** +6.3% regular session. Unveiled **RTX Spark** superchip (Blackwell GPU + Arm-based N1X CPU co-designed with MediaTek), calling it "the most efficient PC chip ever." 30+ laptop models, 10 desktop models this fall from Dell, HP, ASUS, Lenovo, Microsoft Surface.\n- **Vera CPU** introduced: Arm-based, designed for agentic AI inference; customers exploring include OpenAI, Anthropic, SpaceXAI, CoreWeave, Oracle Cloud.\n- Huang: "Nvidia has really become an infrastructure company."\n- **ARM** +16.4% to $410 — royalty on every N1X CPU shipped. Barclays raised PT to $360 Overweight; Mizuho to $425 Outperform; Wells Fargo to $410 Overweight. Jim Cramer: "obviously additive" for ARM.\n- **INTC** -9.2% — RTX Spark directly challenges x86 dominance in consumer PCs. Intel separately revealed Crescent Island GPU targeting AI inference with LPDDR5 memory/air cooling (lower cost than HBM/liquid). Barclays PT raised to $100 Equal-Weight; Mizuho to $128 Neutral.\n- **QCOM** -8.9% — Dragonfly AI data center launch overshadowed by NVDA Spark's 100+ TOPS performance on Windows-on-Arm.\n- **AMD** +0.9% but -3.4% premarket. Extended AM5 support through 2029 at Computex. Cathie Wood sold $56.2M AMD, bought $67.3M NVDA on Monday. Barclays PT raised to $665 OW; Mizuho to $615 OP; TD Cowen to $600 Buy.\n- **CDNS** +8.5% — Unveiled autonomous AI chip design agent with NVDA at Computex.\n\n**Enterprise SaaS — From "AI Kills Software" to Record Rally** `SaaS` → new\n\nThe sector staged its best three-day run on record after **SNOW**'s blowout quarter and Huang's endorsement that agentic AI makes this "one of the best moments in history to be a software company."\n\n- **iShares Expanded Tech-Software ETF (IGV)** +4% on Monday; now +3.3% YTD after being -25% at Q1 trough.\n- **NOW** +9.9% — 40% rally in four sessions, strongest in company history.\n- **CRM** +9.3% — Bloomberg reported Anthropic stake now valued at ~$5B. Announced $2B France AI investment through 2030. Citigroup lowered PT to $187 Neutral; Bernstein $173 Underperform.\n- **SNOW** +11.3% (+64% 5D, +87% in May — best month in company history). Wedbush raised PT to $280 Outperform. HSBC upgraded. Product revenue $1.33B, +34% YoY; 7,100+ accounts adopted Cortex Code (CoCo) in one quarter.\n- **MDB** +20.9%, **TWLO** +20.2%, **DDOG** +13.0%, **NET** +12.8%, **IOT** +11.7% — all moving on sector rotation / short-covering, not company-specific news.\n- **ZS** +12.5% — Guggenheim upgraded to Buy, PT $214 (from Neutral). Stock still -14% 5D after Q3 guidance miss last week.\n- **OKTA** +13.6% (+52% 5D) — post-Q1 beat continuation.\n- Wedbush's Dan Ives: "The data layer is now the monetization. It's not just chips."\n\n**HPE Blowout Validates AI Server Demand Durability** `AI Infrastructure` → ongoing\n\n**HPE** +11% regular session, +36% after hours to ~$64 after Q2 results demolished expectations.\n\n- Q2 revenue $10.68B vs $9.79B est (+40% YoY). EPS $0.79 vs $0.53 est (+108% YoY).\n- Networking revenue $2.7B (+148% YoY, Juniper integration). Cloud & AI revenue $7.7B (+23% YoY).\n- Orders more than doubled YoY; record company backlog. Cumulative "networks for AI" order target raised to ≥$2B by FY26 end.\n- **Raised FY26**: EPS $3.35–$3.45 (from $2.30–$2.50 vs $2.43 est). Revenue growth 29–33%. FCF ≥$3.5B (from ≥$2B). "Two years ahead of plan."\n- **FY27 framework**: Revenue +8–12%. EPS growth +12–16%. FCF ≥$4.5B. Net leverage target 2x by FY26 end (one year early).\n- **DELL** +11.9% to $468, new all-time high. Morgan Stanley upgraded to Equal-Weight from UW, PT $170→$448. Bernstein PT $500 OP. Goldman PT $500 Buy. Mizuho PT $500 OP. Susquehanna PT $700 (street high), upgraded to Positive.\n- **SMCI** +6% after hours on HPE read-through.\n- **MCHP** +10% after hours after disclosing Data Center Solutions BU revenue $303M in CY2025, guiding ~65% growth in CY2026 (~$500M).\n\n**Memory Supercycle: Goldman Calls Tightest Shortage on Record** `Memory` → ongoing\n\n- Goldman Sachs Sunday note: DRAM undersupply 5.0% in 2026, 5.9% in 2027. NAND shortages 4.4% and 4.6%. HBM market forecast raised to $116B in 2027 (from $75B), $168B by 2028.\n- Average DRAM prices expected +300% YoY in 2026; NAND +250%.\n- **MU** +8.9% to $1,032 ($1.2T market cap). Now 12th most valuable company globally, surpassing Berkshire, Walmart, Eli Lilly. Raymond James PT raised to $1,100 OP. Trades at ~10x forward earnings despite 903% 12-month gain. Reports June 24 ⚠️.\n- **SNDK** +6.0%. Goldman Buy, PT $1,200. Barclays upgraded to OW, PT $2,300. Mizuho PT $1,825 OP. 18 of covering analysts rate Buy.\n- **STX** +5.2%. Wells Fargo PT raised to $900 Equal-Weight.\n\n**Alphabet's $80B Capital Raise + Berkshire** `Mega-Cap / AI Capex` → new\n\n**GOOGL** fell ~2% after hours following the capital raise announcement.\n\n- $30B underwritten public offering + $40B ATM program (Q3 start) + $10B private placement to Berkshire Hathaway.\n- Berkshire: $5B Class A at $351.81/share + $5B Class C at $348.20/share. Adds to position built since Q3 2025.\n- ~$30B of ATM proceeds earmarked for 2026 tax obligations. Remainder for AI compute infrastructure.\n- Alphabet also holds estimated $122.5B in SpaceX (7% stake) and $135.1B in Anthropic (14% stake) ahead of both IPOs.\n- Jim Cramer questioned whether "enough money" exists for OpenAI, Anthropic, SpaceX, and Google simultaneously.\n\n**Anthropic Files S-1; AI IPO Race Accelerates** `AI / IPOs` → new\n\n- Anthropic confidentially filed draft Form S-1 with SEC on Monday. No share count or pricing disclosed.\n- Valued at $965B after $65B Series H round. Annual revenue run rate $47B as of May 28. Claude Opus 4.8 launched.\n- Wedbush's Dan Ives: "This represents an opening of the floodgates for the IPO market."\n- SpaceX filed S-1 on May 20; roadshow this week. OpenAI preparing its own filing.\n- Anthropic spends $1.25B/month with SpaceX for GPU access (Colossus 1 & 2). Secured 5 GW capacity with Amazon + 5 GW next-gen TPU capacity with Google/Broadcom.\n\n**Berkshire Hathaway Buys Taylor Morrison for $6.8B** `Homebuilders / Macro` → new\n\n- **TMHC** +21.6% to $71.60. Berkshire to acquire at $72.50/share cash, 24% premium to Friday close of $58.50.\n- First major deal for CEO Greg Abel (succeeded Buffett in January).\n- Jim Cramer: "shows that there may be value in the hated homebuilders."\n- RBC maintains OP, PT raised to $72.50. Citizens downgraded to Market Perform.\n\n**Credo Technology — Beat But Disappointed on Forward Optics Timeline** `AI Connectivity` → ongoing\n\n**CRDO** -0.8% regular session, -14% after hours to ~$195.\n\n- Q4 revenue $437M vs $432M est (+157% YoY). EPS $1.16 vs $1.03 est. Gross margin 68.3%.\n- Q1 guide: $465–$475M vs $462M est.\n- FY27: >80% YoY revenue growth. Optical portfolio (ZF Optics, SiFo PICs, optical DSPs) each >$100M, totaling >$600M — but back-half weighted.\n- Acquired DustPhotonics for $750M (closed last week). Cash $1.4B.\n- Sell-off likely reflects concern that optical ramp is H2-loaded and 200G per lane volume won't meaningfully contribute until FY28.\n- Mizuho PT raised to $260 OP.\n\n**Iran Tensions Spike Oil, Pressure Futures Late Monday** `Macro / Geopolitics` → ongoing\n\n- WTI crude +7.5% to ~$94; Brent +8% to ~$97 — best session since April 2.\n- Iran's Tasnim news agency: Tehran suspending message exchanges with U.S. over Lebanon attacks. Separately reported Iran could "completely block Strait of Hormuz."\n- Trump told CNBC "I couldn't care less" if Iran negotiations collapse. Also claimed Netanyahu agreed to pull troops from Beirut.\n- Dow futures -234 pts (~-0.46%) late Monday. KOSPI -1.9%, Nikkei -1.3% overnight.\n- National gas average $4.322/gal (AAA), down slightly WoW. GasBuddy: prices at 1-month low but "could spike" without Iran deal.\n- ISM Manufacturing PMI: 54.0 vs 53.3 est (strongest in 4 years). Prices paid: 82.1 vs 85.3 est.\n\n**Space Sector Rotation: Profit-Taking Hits Recent Winners** `Space / Defense` → ongoing\n\n- **RKLB** -11.9% despite Q1 revenue beat ($200.4M) and $2.2B backlog. Blue Origin New Glenn explosion + sector-wide profit-taking ahead of SpaceX IPO.\n- **SPCE** +19.8% (+137% 5D) — glide test success, legal settlement, SpaceX hype. RSI 86.78.\n- **RDW** (Redwire) -16.5% after Jefferies downgraded to Hold, PT $24.\n- BlackSky (BKSY) -11.4% after Jefferies downgraded to Hold, PT $50.\n\n**📡 Undercurrents** *(developing, not yet dominant)*\n- **MCHP** +10% after hours after disclosing Data Center Solutions BU on pace for $500M in CY2026 (+65% YoY) and announcing selective price increases across product portfolio.\n- **META** -5.1% — subscription tiers ($3.99/mo Instagram/Facebook, $2.99/mo WhatsApp) estimated at $13.5B opportunity by 2028, but stock sold on sector rotation.\n- **FULC** -50% after hours: discontinued pociredir (sickle cell) after FDA raised malignancy concerns; initiated strategic review. Cash $333M.\n- **Penguin Solutions (PENG)** +7% after hours: reaffirmed FY26 guidance, expects high end on "very strong agentic AI demand." Reports July 7 ⚠️. CFO departing.\n- **The Trade Desk (TTD)** appointed Nate Olmstead (departing Penguin CFO) as its new CFO effective July 9.\n- Andrew Left (Citron Research) convicted of securities fraud on 13 of 17 counts. Sentencing August 31.\n- SoftBank CEO Masayoshi Son: AI revolution is "50x bigger" than dot-com boom; corrections could be "the best investment opportunity."\n\n---\n\n### 📰 Channel Pulse\n\n| Channel | Material fact |\n|---------|--------------|\n| Earnings | **HPE** Q2 EPS $0.79 vs $0.53 est, rev $10.68B vs $9.79B; raised FY26 EPS guide to $3.35–$3.45 from $2.30–$2.50. **CRDO** Q4 EPS $1.16 vs $1.03, but stock -14% AH on H2-weighted optical ramp. **SAIC** Q1 EPS $3.23 vs $2.29, raised FY27 EPS guide to $9.90–$10.10. |\n| Macro | ISM Manufacturing PMI 54.0 vs 53.3 est (4-year high). Iran suspends message exchanges with U.S.; Tasnim reports possible full Strait of Hormuz closure. WTI +7.5% to ~$94. 10Y yield +6bps to 4.51%. |\n| Tech | NVDA unveiled RTX Spark (Arm-based PC chip) and Vera CPU at Computex. Cadence announced autonomous AI chip design agent with NVDA. U.S. Commerce Dept closed loophole allowing NVDA/AMD chip exports to Chinese subsidiaries overseas. |\n| IPOs | Anthropic filed 