klog | Semis / Test Equipment |\n| **OPTX** | +22.5% | Small | Selected for Russell 3000 Index inclusion effective June 26 | Idiosyncratic |\n| **PENG** | +19.8% | Small | FY26 outlook reaffirmed at high end; agentic AI demand cited | AI Infrastructure / Memory |\n| **FCEL** | +19.4% | Small | Clean energy / data center power rally continuation | Clean Energy / Power |\n| **ARM** | -1.8% | Mega | Consolidation after +26% 5D on Computex RTX Spark unveil | AI Compute |\n| **INTC** | -0.8% | Mega | Pullback; 5D -13.2% on Nvidia PC chip competitive threat | Semiconductors |\n| **PANW** | -1.1%→+11% AH | Mega | Q3 rev $3.0B vs $2.94B est; adj EPS $0.85 vs $0.80; FY raised ⚠️ | Cybersecurity |\n| **CRWD** | -1.1%→+2% AH | Mega | Sympathy with PANW beat; reports Wed after close ⚠️ | Cybersecurity |\n| **AVGO** | +3.5% | Mega | Pre-earnings positioning; reports Wed after close ⚠️ | AI Compute / Custom Silicon |\n| **GOOGL** | -2.6% | Mega | $80B equity raise announced (incl $10B Berkshire); dilution concern | Macro / AI Capex |\n| **MSFT** | -3.7% | Mega | Profit-taking; Anthropic IPO filing shifts attention | SaaS / AI |\n| **GME** | -2.1%→+7% AH | Large | Q1 EPS $0.30 vs $0.16 est; rev $835M vs $767M; $2B buyback | Idiosyncratic |\n| **ULTA** | -1.2%→+5% AH | Large | Q1 EPS $7.74 vs $6.86 est; rev $3.16B vs $3.09B; EPS guide raised | Retail |\n| **GTLB** | -5.8%→+7% AH | Large | Q1 EPS $0.23 vs $0.21 est; rev $264M vs $255M; 14% RIF announced | DevOps / SaaS |\n| **INTU** | -8.5% | Mega | Goldman downgrades to Sell, PT cut to $276 from $519; AI competition | SaaS |\n| **SHAK** | -10.9% | Large | Q2 guide cut: rev $415-420M from $424-428M; same-store trimmed | Consumer / Idiosyncratic |\n\n---\n\n### 🧭 Narrative Threads\n\n**AI Networking & Optical Interconnects — The Huang Effect** `AI Compute / Optical Networking` → new\n\nJensen Huang's Computex appearance catalyzed the session's dominant move: calling **MRVL** "the next trillion-dollar company" and reiterating Nvidia's $2B strategic investment in Marvell's optical interconnects, silicon photonics, and custom ASIC business. The endorsement drove **MRVL** +28.8% (intraday high $291.30), with 2x leveraged ETFs MVLL and MRVU each surging ~65%. The ripple spread across the entire optical connectivity complex.\n\n- **MRVL** data center segment = 76% of revenue; FY27 revenue guide raised to >80% YoY growth; Q2 FY27 guide $2.7B midpoint vs $2.60B est\n- **COHR** +16.6% to new ATH $427; 52-week range $76.88–$427; avg analyst PT $247.58 (consensus lagging)\n- **GLW** +13.3%, **LITE** +13.0%, **CIEN** +9.7%, **FN** +10.8% — all riding the same data-center optical bandwidth thesis with no company-specific catalysts\n- Nvidia said connectivity is the new bottleneck: "This is why Matt is performing so well, and why Marvell is so vital"\n- Congressional stock tracker flagged Reps. Donalds and Salazar sitting on ~150% gains from early-April MRVL purchases\n- Barclays raised MRVL to Overweight, PT to $275; HSBC street-high $300; Needham $275\n- **CRDO** +0.7% (5D +4.9%); Q4 rev $437M (+157% YoY) beat $432M est; but stock fell ~12% after hours Monday on execution concerns; guided Q1 $465-475M vs $462M est; optical portfolio expected >$600M in FY27\n\n**AI Server Infrastructure — HPE Blowout Validates Cycle** `AI Servers` → ongoing\n\n**HPE** delivered its biggest single-day move in history (+17.9%, hit intraday high $64.25 before fading to ~$56) after fiscal Q2 results demolished expectations and management pulled forward FY28 margin and revenue targets to the current fiscal year.\n\n- Q2 EPS $0.79 vs $0.53 est (+49% beat); revenue $10.68B vs $9.79B est (+40% YoY)\n- AI server backlog $6.3B; 61% from government and enterprise customers; $1.8B in new AI orders in Q2\n- Non-GAAP operating margin expanded to 13.3% from ~8% YoY (+530 bps)\n- FY26 adj EPS guide raised to $3.35-$3.45 from $2.30-$2.50 (vs $2.43 est)\n- FY26 rev guide raised to $44.2-45.6B from $40.1-41.8B; FY27 rev growth guided 8-12%\n- Memory component shortage persists through 2027 per management\n- BofA raised PT from $38 to $80 (street-high); Loop upgraded to Buy PT $75; 14 analysts raised PTs\n- Sympathy: **DELL** pulled back -6.4% (profit-taking after +101% in May), **SMCI** +5.5% intraday\n- **DELL** Morgan Stanley upgraded from Underweight to Equal-Weight, PT $170→$448; Goldman, Mizuho, Bernstein all raised to $500\n\n**Custom AI Silicon — Broadcom Earnings Wednesday** `AI Compute` → ongoing\n\n**AVGO** +3.5% to new ATH $488.82 ahead of Q2 FY26 earnings Wednesday after close. The stock is being positioned as a co-beneficiary of the custom ASIC theme Huang amplified.\n\n- Analysts expect Q2 rev $22.1B (up from $15B YoY); EPS $2.32 vs $1.58 YoY\n- JPMorgan PT $500 Overweight; Morgan Stanley raised to $485; UBS $490; Susquehanna $490\n- Gary Black: "AVGO and MRVL seem to be the big winners in Wall Street's new appreciation for custom AI chips"\n- AI revenue was +106% YoY in Q1; custom silicon market share est. ~70% (AVGO) + ~25% (MRVL) = 95% duopoly\n- Freedom Capital's Jay Woods: "may as well be" a Magnificent Seven stock; "clear path to $500"\n- Next catalyst: Q2 FY26 earnings Wed Jun 3 after close ⚠️\n\n**Cybersecurity — PANW Beat Sets Up CRWD** `Cybersecurity` → ongoing\n\n**PANW** reported after Tuesday's close and surged +11% in after-hours to $330 as AI-driven cybersecurity demand accelerated.\n\n- Q3 FY26 rev $3.0B vs $2.94B est (+31% YoY); adj EPS $0.85 vs $0.80 est\n- NGS ARR +60% YoY to $8.1B; RPO +36% to $18.4B; adj FCF $910M\n- FY26 rev guide raised to $11.42-11.43B from $11.28-11.31B; adj EPS raised to $3.77-3.79 from $3.65-3.70\n- CEO Arora: "accelerating organic bookings growth as customers turn to us to secure their AI deployments at scale"\n- **CRWD** +2.15% after hours in sympathy; reports Q1 Wed Jun 4 after close ⚠️\n- Analysts expect CRWD Q1 rev $1.36B, EPS $0.88; Wedbush PT $700; JPMorgan raised to $800; Jefferies $775\n- Wedbush's Ives: "AI has dramatically lowered the cost, skill and time required to execute sophisticated attacks"\n\n**After-Hours Earnings — GME, ULTA, GTLB** `Retail / DevOps` → new\n\nThree names reported after Tuesday's close with material beats:\n\n- **GME** Q1 EPS $0.30 vs $0.16 est; rev $835M vs $767M (+14% YoY); record quarterly net income $389.6M; $9.7B cash/securities/digital assets; $2B new buyback through Jun 2029; stock +7.4% AH\n- **ULTA** Q1 EPS $7.74 vs $6.86 est; rev $3.16B vs $3.09B (+11.1% YoY); comp sales +5.3%; raised FY26 EPS guide to $28.36-28.80 from prior; affirmed rev $13.14-13.26B; stock +5.4% AH\n- **GTLB** Q1 EPS $0.23 vs $0.21 est; rev $264M vs $255M (+23% YoY); raised FY27 rev guide to $1.112-1.118B; announced 14% workforce reduction (350 people), exiting 22 countries; stock +7% AH initially, then reversed -5.6% AH\n\n**SaaS Rotation — Monday's Winners Become Tuesday's Losers** `SaaS` → ongoing\n\nThe iShares Expanded Tech-Software Sector ETF (IGV) fell -3.2% Tuesday after gaining +15% over the prior four sessions. Capital rotated from software into semiconductors/optical.\n\n- **INTU** -8.5% after Goldman downgrade to Sell, PT slashed from $519 to $276; analyst warned AI-driven tax platforms (Prime Meridian, Perplexity Tax) can process returns for ~$0.12 vs TurboTax's $162 avg; Mailchimp growth stalled\n- **NOW** -5.7% after record +40% four-session rally; **CRM** -4.6%; **ZS** -8.5%; **HUBS** -10.4%\n- **MSFT** -3.7% as Anthropic confidential IPO filing shifted attention; analysts still bullish (Wells Fargo PT $650)\n\n**Macro / Geopolitics — Iran Standoff, Oil Elevated, JOLTS Strong** `Macro` → ongoing\n\nIran-U.S. tensions remain elevated but showed mixed signals. Trump posted on Truth Social that talks have been "going on continuously." Sec. Rubio set conditions: Iran must open Strait of Hormuz and commit to enriched uranium negotiations.\n\n- WTI crude -1.4% to ~$90.84 after Monday's +5.9% spike; Brent ~$93.68\n- National gas avg $4.29/gal; Moody's Zandi estimates cumulative war