et; iShares SOXX -4%+\n- **MRVL** +7% bucking the trend, extending +53% 5D rally after Huang's "next trillion-dollar company" endorsement; RSI 86.89\n- Next catalyst: AVGO Q3 earnings ~Sep 2; **MU** earnings Jun 24 ⚠️; **MRVL** earnings ~Aug 27 ⚠️\n\n**CrowdStrike Beat-and-Raise Meets Sell-the-News** `Cybersecurity` → new\n\n**CRWD** -10% premarket despite exceeding on every metric and announcing a 4-for-1 stock split.\n\n- Q1 revenue $1.39B (+26% YoY) beat $1.36B; adj EPS $1.10 beat $1.07\n- ARR $5.51B (+24% YoY); net new ARR $255.8M (+32% YoY) — record Q1\n- Record cash from ops $591M; free cash flow $468.5M; $4.55B cash\n- FY27 revenue guide raised to $5.92B-$5.96B from $5.87B-$5.93B vs $5.89B est\n- FY27 adj EPS guide raised to $4.88-$4.96 from $4.78-$4.90 vs $4.86 est\n- 4-for-1 stock split: record date Jun 25, distribution Jul 1, split-adjusted trading Jul 2\n- CEO Kurtz: AI adoption "existential imperative"; AIDR pipeline up 250% sequentially\n- Analyst PT raises: Scotiabank $475→$805; Citizens $550→$780; Susquehanna $475→$800; Needham $475→$780; Benchmark $700→$780; BMO $685→$745; Cantor $700→$725; Barclays $650→$675; Mizuho $630→$700\n- Stock had rallied +16% in prior 5 days, setting up profit-taking\n- **NTSK** (Netskope) -19%: Q1 rev $202M beat $198M, but CFO Drew Del Matto announced retirement; Q2 rev guide $213M-$215M (~25% growth); Baird lowers PT to $16 from $20\n\n**AI Memory & Storage Demand Cycle** `Memory / Storage` → ongoing\n\n**SNDK** +4%, **WDC** +4%, **MU** +0.1% (5D +15.5%) — memory stocks consolidating near highs despite AVGO-driven semi weakness.\n\n- Morgan Stanley raised MU PT to $1,050; Raymond James to $1,100; Susquehanna to $1,750\n- MU earnings Jun 24 ⚠️ — street expects EPS $19.29 (vs $1.91 YoY), rev $33.88B (vs $9.30B YoY)\n- Citigroup raised WDC PT to $685; Morgan Stanley raised SNDK PT to $1,750\n- Automakers, retailers warn memory chip shortages could lead to "significant near-term price increases"\n- Chinese DRAM maker CXMT cleared Shanghai listing review for ~$4.4B IPO; Q1 rev 50.8B yuan (+8x YoY); global DRAM share ~8%\n- YMTC (NAND) share climbed to ~11% globally; cleared listing tutoring stage May 19\n\n**Macro: Labor Data Softer, Fed Hawkish Voices Persist** `Macro` → ongoing\n\nWeekly jobless claims jumped to 225K vs 214K est (prior revised to 212K). Q1 nonfarm productivity revised down to +0.3% from +0.8%; unit labor costs +1.8% vs +2.3% est.\n\n- Dallas Fed's Logan: Fed "may need to hike interest rates this year"; "monetary policy is not restraining the economy"\n- Fed's Barkin: not leaning toward either inflation or employment risks; employers outside software not yet reducing headcount due to AI\n- Fed's Williams: "a lot of inflation is due to tariffs"; upside risks to inflation have increased; does not expect long-running energy price increases\n- Beige Book: 10/12 districts slight-to-moderate growth; employment little-to-no change; prices increased at moderate-to-strong pace with most districts reporting higher inflation than prior report\n- CME FedWatch: 96.2% probability rates unchanged at June meeting\n- 10Y yield 4.49%; 2Y yield 4.07%; 30Y ~5.00%\n- Challenger job cuts (May): 97K vs 83K prior\n- ISM Services (Wed): 54.5 vs 53.8 est — strongest expansion in 3 months; prices subindex 71.3\n- ADP (Wed): 122K vs 118K est — strongest since Jan 2025\n- WTI crude ~$95.27/bbl (-0.8%); gold $4,462/oz (+0.6%); Bitcoin $63,506 (-5.1%)\n\n**Geopolitical: Iran Hostilities Continue, House War Powers Vote** `Macro / Energy` → ongoing\n\n- House passed war powers resolution 215-208 requiring Trump to obtain congressional authorization for military action against Iran; 4 Republicans joined Democrats\n- Iran's FM: armed forces conducting "self-defense strikes"; any "hostile act" will be met with "immediate, decisive response"\n- Iran outlined 4-stage deal plan: ceasefire, lifting blockades, formal dialogue on sanctions/nuclear dossier, supervisory committee\n- Israel-Lebanon ceasefire deal 