2026-06-06	daily	benzinga	complete	### 🗓 June 6, 2026 — Pre-Market Brief\n\n**One-liner**: May nonfarm payrolls crushed estimates at 172K (vs. 85K), pushing rate-hike odds to near certainty by December, while AVGO's unchanged AI guide triggered a two-day semiconductor wipeout; Bitcoin broke below $60,000 for the first time since Oct 2024; MRVL added to S&P 500 effective June 22; LULU cut FY guidance citing social media backlash; and defensive staples/healthcare led a violent sector rotation.\n\n---\n\n### ⚡ Top Movers At a Glance\n\n| Ticker | Move | Cap | Catalyst | Theme |\n|--------|------|-----|----------|-------|\n| **MU** | -14.2% | Mega | AVGO AI guide disappointment; profit-taking after 837% 12M run | Memory / AI Compute |\n| **MRVL** | -13.2% | Mega | Profit-taking post-NVDA endorsement; added to S&P 500 June 22 | AI Networking |\n| **ARM** | -12.9% | Mega | Sympathy sell with semis; Mizuho PT $500 | AI Compute |\n| **SNDK** | -12.5% | Mega | Broad semi rotation; Goldman maintains Buy | Memory |\n| **GLW** | -11.5% | Mega | Gap down Jun 2; UBS raises PT to $228 | AI Infrastructure |\n| **AMD** | -10.9% | Mega | Semi selloff; Barclays raises PT to $665 Overweight | AI Compute |\n| **DELL** | -8.1% | Mega | AI server pricing uncertainty; profit-taking | AI Infrastructure |\n| **NOW** | -6.9% | Mega | Rate-hike fear hits high-multiple SaaS | Enterprise SaaS |\n| **CRWD** | -6.1% | Mega | Post-Q1 fade; 4-for-1 split announced; 20+ PT raises | Cybersecurity |\n| **PG** | +4.2% | Mega | Defensive rotation on hot jobs/rate-hike fears | Consumer Staples |\n| **KO** | +3.5% | Mega | Defensive rotation | Consumer Staples |\n| **WELL** | +4.7% | Mega | Defensive rotation into healthcare REITs | Healthcare REIT |\n| **PGR** | +4.6% | Mega | Insurance sector strength; defensive bid | Insurance |\n| **BRK-B** | +2.6% | Mega | Defensive rotation; 2.2× volume | Conglomerate |\n| **NBIS** | -13.9% | Large | Rate-hike fears; SpaceX compute deal competitive threat; NVDA director sold $221M | AI Infrastructure |\n| **ALAB** | -11.4% | Large | Broad semi selloff continuation | AI Networking |\n| **STRL** | -12.6% | Large | Profit-taking after infrastructure rally | Infrastructure |\n| **COHR** | -10.6% | Large | Semi/optics rotation | Optical Networking |\n| **HPE** | -9.0% | Large | Profit-taking after 197% 12M rally | AI Infrastructure |\n| **STM** | -9.8% | Large | Semi sector contagion | Semiconductors |\n| **IONQ** | -13.2% | Large | Quantinuum IPO rotation; quantum sector repricing | Quantum |\n| **CRWV** | -9.1% | Large | SpaceX neocloud competition; private credit pressure | AI Infrastructure |\n| **COO** | +8.1% | Mid | Q2 beat: rev $1.08B (+8%), EPS $1.21 vs $1.10 est | Medical Devices |\n| **BBCP** | +30.5% | Small | Q2 beat: rev $107M vs $97M est; raised FY guide | Infrastructure |\n| **MRLN** | +18.7% | Small | Completed CDR for C-130J autonomy with USSOCOM | Defense Tech |\n| **ABM** | +8.2% | Mid | Q2 beat: EPS $0.90 vs $0.87; 6.1% organic growth | Facility Services |\n| **TTAN** | +3.3% | Mid | Q1 beat: EPS $0.37 vs $0.27; raised FY sales guide to $1.13-1.14B | Vertical SaaS |\n| **GIII** | +4.5% | Small | Q1 beat: loss $(0.21) vs $(0.30); raised FY EPS to $2.15-2.25 | Apparel |\n| **LULU** | — | Large | Q1 rev beat but EPS miss $1.69 vs $1.70; cut FY EPS to $10.95-11.15 from $12.10-12.30 ⚠️ | Retail / Apparel |\n| **RDW** | -16.9% | Small | SpaceX IPO fatigue; S&P kills fast-track index inclusion | Space |\n| **TSAT** | -18.4% | Small | Space sector selloff | Space |\n| **BRUN** | -21.3% | Small | Profit-taking post-earnings run | Idiosyncratic |\n| **PENG** | -17.6% | Small | Post-gap continuation selling | Idiosyncratic |\n| **VSH** | -13.0% | Mid | Passive component selloff | Semiconductors |\n\n---\n\n### 🧭 Narrative Threads\n\n**Macro: Rate-Hike Regime Change** `Macro` → new\n\nThe May jobs report shattered the rate-cut narrative. Nonfarm payrolls printed 172K vs. 85K consensus, with March and April revised up by a combined 93K. Unemployment held at 4.3%. With April CPI at 3.8% YoY (highest since May 2023), money markets now price a 96% probability of at least one rate hike by December and 59% by October 28.\n\n- May NFP 172K vs. 85K est.; March revised to 214K from 185K; April revised to 179K from 115K\n- Average hourly earnings +0.3% MoM, +3.4% YoY — in line\n- 2-year Treasury yield jumped ~10 bps to 4.15%; 10-year to 4.54%\n- CME FedWatch: 96.4% chance rates unchanged at June 17 FOMC; ~59% chance of hike by Oct 28\n- Fed's Hammack (LinkedIn post): "reasonable to keep rates steady for now" but "if recent trends continue it may soon be appropriate to act against high inflation"\n- White House advisor Hassett: "the Fed shouldn't hike, and will have room to cut"\n- **Trump** said he'd leave rate-cut decisions to Warsh during October meeting\n- Ed Yardeni became first major strategist to call for a hike as soon as July\n- S&P 500 snapped 9-week winning streak; Nasdaq 100 -3.4% (worst since Oct 2025)\n- VIX +21%; gold -3.1%; silver -7%; dollar index +~1%\n\n**Semiconductor Selloff: AVGO Guide Triggers Sector De-Rating** `AI Compute / Memory` → ongoing\n\n**Broadcom** kept its FY2027 AI semiconductor revenue target at "in excess of $100 billion" rather than raising it, despite Q2 revenue of $22.19B (+48% YoY) beating estimates. The stock fell 12.6% Thursday, then another ~7% Friday, dragging the entire AI hardware complex.\n\n- **AVGO** Q2 adj EPS $2.44 vs $2.40 est; revenue $22.19B; Q3 guide ~$29.4B vs $28.5B est\n- AI semiconductor revenue $10.8B in Q2 (+143% YoY); FY26 AI semi guide $56B; FY27 reiterated >$100B\n- Goldman Sachs reiterated Buy, raised PT to $525; BofA raised PT to $530 Buy\n- **iShares Semiconductor ETF (SOXX)** fell >10% over Thursday-Friday combined\n- **MU** -14.2% Friday; CEO Mehrotra sold $36M in stock May 29 via 10b5-1 plan; earnings June 24 ⚠️\n- **AMD** -10.9%; Barclays raised PT to $665 Overweight; earnings est Aug 4 ⚠️\n- **ARM** -12.9%; Mizuho raised PT to $500 Outperform; short interest rose to 18.79M shares (13.76% of float)\n- **SNDK** -12.5%; Goldman maintains Buy on memory supply tightness thesis\n- **MRVL** -13.2% regular session but +3.5% after-hours on S&P 500 inclusion announcement (effective June 22, replacing POOL); also **FLEX** added to S&P 500 same date\n- **MRVL** Q1 EPS $0.80 vs $0.79 est; rev $2.42B; Q2 guide rev $2.57-2.84B vs $2.6B est\n- **NVDA** CFO Kress at BofA conference: content per GW could rise from ~$40B (Blackwell) to $100B+ (Feynman); BofA reiterates Buy, $350 PT, "top sector pick"\n- Dan Niles: "Semiconductor bulls should be thrilled at the resiliency" — SOXX only -2.1% vs AVGO -12.6% on Thursday\n\n**Defensive Rotation Accelerates** `Consumer Staples / Insurance / Healthcare` → new\n\nDefensive and rate-shielded groups rallied sharply as capital rotated out of tech growth. Consumer Staples sector +2.2%, Healthcare +1.5%, Utilities +1.2%.\n\n- **PG** +4.2%, **KO** +3.5%, **KMB** +5.4%, **KVUE** +5.3%, **CL** +4.7%, **CHD** +3.5%\n- **BTI** +3.4%, **UL** +3.3%, **MO** +2.5%\n- **PGR** +4.6%, **ALL** +4.6%, **CB** +3.7%, **WRB** +3.6%, **HIG** +3.6%\n- **WELL** +4.7%, **VTR** +4.8% (healthcare REITs)\n- **BRK-B** +2.6% on 2.2× avg volume; Congress members Gottheimer and Menefee disclosed BRK-B sales in recent weeks\n- **COST** held flat during Friday selloff — membership model cited as defensive cushion\n\n**LULU Guidance Cut Anchors Consumer Weakness** `Retail / Apparel` → ongoing\n\n**Lululemon** cut its full-year outlook and blamed negative social media commentary for traffic declines in North America.\n\n- Q1 revenue $2.47B (+4% YoY) beat $2.43B est; adj EPS $1.69 missed $1.70 est\n- Comparable sales +1% reported, -2% constant currency\n- Gross margin fell 410 bps to 54.2% on tariffs, markdowns, fixed-cost deleverage\n- Q2 guide: rev $2.45-2.48B vs $2.60B est; EPS $1.76-1.81 vs $2.71 est\n- FY26 rev cut to $11.0-11.15B from $11.35-11.5B; EPS cut to $10.95-11.15 from $12.10-12.30\n- Stock hit 8-year low; BTIG downgraded to Neutral from Buy\n- New CEO Heidi O'Neill not joining until September\n- Mainland China revenue +30% YoY; Americas -3%\n\n**Neocloud Competition Intensifies With SpaceX Entry** `AI Infrastructure` → new\n\nSpaceX signed a $920M/month compute-leasing deal with Google (110K NVIDIA GPUs through mid-2029), directly competing with neoclouds **CRWV** and **NBIS**.\n\n- SpaceX AI division: $818M revenue, $2.5B operating loss last quarter; $10.1B total capex\n- **CRWV** -9.1%, **NBIS** -13.9% on competitive threat + rate-hike pressure\n- NVIDIA Director Mark Stevens sold $221M in NVDA shares June 2-4\n- SpaceX IPO set for June 12 on Nasdaq as SPCX at $135/share (~$1.77T valuation)\n- S&P Dow Jones declined to create fast-track index inclusion rules for SpaceX\n- SpaceX barred Hong Kong/mainland China investors from IPO citing ITAR\n- Morgan Stanley projects SpaceX revenue could reach $3.4T by 2040\n- NYU's Damodaran values SpaceX at ~$1.3T, calls AI TAM projections "fantasy"\n- Apollo wrapped up $35B debt facility to buy AI chips for Anthropic\n\n**CrowdStrike: Beat-and-Raise Masked by Valuation Reset** `Cybersecurity` → ongoing\n\n**CRWD** Q1 topped estimates and announced a 4-for-1 stock split, but shares fell ~6% as the beat magnitude disappointed relative to the stock's ~97% pre-earnings rally.\n\n- Q1 revenue $1.39B (+26% YoY) vs $1.36B est; adj EPS $1.10 vs $1.07 est\n- ARR grew 24% to $5.51B; net new ARR $255.8M (+32% YoY) — record but beat was only ~$6M vs $15-29M in prior quarters\n- AIDR (AI Detection & Response) ending ARR +250% sequentially; Q2 pipeline >$50M\n- Next-gen SIEM ARR >$600M; Cloud + Identity + SIEM combined >$2B ARR\n- 4-for-1 stock split: record date June 25, split-adjusted trading July 2\n- FY27 NNARR growth guidance raised to 27.7% at midpoint; total ARR guided $6.53-6.56B\n- 20+ analyst PT raises; Rosenblatt $825 (street high); UBS $790; Stifel $790\n- CEO Kurtz: AI-enabled cyberattacks represent "cybersecurity's Y2K moment"\n\n**Bitcoin Crash Deepens to FTX-Era Levels** `Crypto` → ongoing\n\nBitcoin plunged below $60,000 for the first time since Oct 2024, on track for its worst week since the Nov 2022 FTX collapse.\n\n- BTC ~$59,668 at session low; down ~17% on the week, ~26% over past month\n- 310,873 traders liquidated in 24h for $1.56B (Coinglass)\n- Strategy (MSTR) sold 32 BTC for $2.5M — first sale since Dec 2022; unrealized loss now $10.8B\n- STRC preferred stock trading at $92.50 vs $100 par target\n- Grayscale's Pandl: Strategy's ability to accumulate more BTC "effectively gone" at current share prices\n- Bitcoin ETFs: $3.05M net inflow on Jun 4 after weeks of bleeding; total ETF net assets fell from $109B to $80.4B\n- Bitcoin sitting on 200-week moving average — only broken twice in history (2022 FTX/Terra collapses)\n- CLARITY Act advanced to Senate Legislative Calendar; potential vote before July recess\n\n**Earnings Beats With Sector Tailwinds** `Mixed Sectors` → new\n\nSeveral mid/small-cap names posted strong quarters driven by infrastructure, defense, and medical device demand.\n\n- **BBCP** +30.5%: Q2 rev $106.8M vs $96.7M est (+14% YoY); EPS $0.04 vs $0.01 est; raised FY rev guide to $410-425M from $390-410M; data center/infrastructure projects driving growth\n- **TTAN** +3.3% (up 15% pre-market): Q1 rev $268.8M (+25% YoY) vs $256.7M est; adj EPS $0.37 vs $0.27 est; raised FY sales guide to $1.13-1.14B; Morgan Stanley PT $124, BTIG $110, Piper $115\n- **ABM** +8.2%: Q2 rev $2.29B vs $2.21B est (+8.4% YoY); adj EPS $0.90 vs $0.87 est; organic growth 6.1% (strongest since Q3 2022); AI data center demand highlighted; reaffirmed FY adj EPS $3.85-4.15\n- **COO** +8.1%: Q2 rev $1.08B (+8% YoY) vs $1.054B est; non-GAAP EPS $1.21 (+26% YoY) vs $1.10 est; strategic review of Cooper Surgical advancing with "significant interest"; embryo culture media litigation settled at $271.6M net charge\n- **GIII** +4.5%: Q1 adj loss $(0.21) vs $(0.30) est; rev $536M vs $530M est; raised FY adj EPS to $2.15-2.25 from $2.00-2.10; gross margin +350 bps; Marc Jacobs acquisition pending\n- **MRLN** +18.7%: Completed Critical Design Review for C-130J autonomy program with USSOCOM; TD Cowen initiated Buy $11 PT\n\n**Space Sector Deflates as SpaceX IPO Approaches** `Space / Defense` → ongoing\n\nSpace stocks fell broadly as S&P Global declined to fast-track SpaceX into the S&P 500 and SpaceX IPO valuation conversations tempered expectations.\n\n- **RDW** -16.9% (5D: -24.8%); **TSAT** -18.4%; ASTS -10.4%; RKLB -6.2%; LUNR -11%\n- S&P Dow Jones: no changes to profitability/seasoning rules for index eligibility\n- Planet Labs (PL) posted record Q1 revenue $94.2M (+42% YoY) but fell 25% on $1.5B ATM offering\n- ARK Space ETF (ARKX) and Procure Space ETF (UFO) both lower\n\n**Meta AI Capex Concerns Resurface** `Big Tech / AI Capex` → ongoing\n\nReports that **META** is considering raising tens of billions via stock sale to fund AI infrastructure triggered a 5.5% decline. Hightower's Stephanie Link sold half her META position, calling the company "tone-deaf."\n\n- META capex guided $125-135B for 2026; now reportedly considering increase to $145B\n- Reality Labs has spent $82B since 2021, generating $80B in operating losses\n- **Alphabet** raised $85B in equity this week; Google signed $920M/month AI deal with SpaceX\n- Tom Lee (Fundstrat): AI companies "building huge real estate infrastructures that aren't fully funded today"\n\n**📡 Undercurrents** *(developing, not yet dominant)*\n- Private credit redemption wave continues: Monroe Capital capped redemptions at 5%; Blackstone, Cliffwater, Partners Group, BlackRock, Ares all previously gated funds. Apollo's Zelter: "We are not through the turbulence yet."\n- USMCA renegotiation delay: U.S., Mexico, Canada to miss July deadline; U.S. demands tougher rules for Chinese auto parts in Mexican trade talks (FT).\n- U.S. strikes on Iranian coastal sites near Strait of Hormuz; oil near $93/bbl; House voted 215-208 war powers resolution to restrict Trump's Iran military authority.\n- Trump signed AI national security directive: voluntary 30-day pre-release government access to frontier models; OpenAI confirmed participation.\n- Honeywell set June 15 record date for aerospace spin-off; "HONA" expected to begin trading June 29.\n- Apple WWDC starts Monday June 8; Wedbush's Ives expects "fireworks" with Siri AI standalone app powered by Google Gemini. ⚠️\n- Qualcomm Investor Day June 24: JPMorgan raised PT to $265, expects >$3B data center revenue in FY27. ⚠️\n\n---\n\n### 📰 Channel Pulse\n\n| Channel | Material fact |\n|---------|--------------|\n| Macro | May NFP 172K vs 85K est; Mar/Apr revised up 93K combined; CME FedWatch prices 96% chance of rate hike by Dec 9 |\n| Earnings | BBCP Q2 rev $106.8M vs $96.7M (+14% YoY), raised FY guide; LULU cut FY EPS guide to $10.95-11.15 from $12.10-12.30; TTAN Q1 EPS $0.37 vs $0.27 |\n| Tech | MRVL and FLEX added to S&P 500 effective June 22; SpaceX IPO priced at $135/share for June 12 debut; Apple WWDC begins June 8 |\n| Crypto | Bitcoin fell below $60,000 for first time since Oct 2024; Strategy's STRC preferred trading at $92.50 vs $100 par |\n| Geopolitics | U.S. struck Iranian radar sites near Strait of Hormuz after intercepting 4 drones; House passed war powers resolution 215-208 |\n| Private Markets | Apollo closed $35B debt to buy AI chips for Anthropic; Monroe Capital gated redemptions at 5% |\n\n---\n\n### 👁 On The Radar\n\n- **June 8-12**: Apple WWDC — Siri AI standalone app, Gemini integration, iOS/macOS updates expected ⚠️\n- **June 10**: May CPI report — April was 3.8% YoY (highest since May 2023); critical for June 16-17 FOMC ⚠️\n- **June 12**: SpaceX IPO on Nasdaq as SPCX at $135/share; ~$75B offering\n- **June 16-17**: First FOMC meeting chaired by Kevin Warsh; no rate change expected (98.2% probability)\n- **June 22**: MRVL and FLEX join S&P 500 (replacing POOL and another name)\n- **June 24**: Micron (MU) Q3 