2026-06-11	daily	benzinga	complete	### 🗓 2026-06-11 — Pre-Market Brief\n\n**One-liner**: ORCL drops ~9% pre-market despite a Q4 beat after guiding to lower gross margins and announcing $40B in financing; U.S.-Iran strikes escalate with Brent above $95; May CPI hit 4.2% YoY (highest since April 2023); and SpaceX IPO prices tomorrow with $250B+ in orders against a $75B offering.\n\n---\n\n### ⚡ Top Movers At a Glance\n\n| Ticker | Move | Cap | Catalyst | Theme |\n|--------|------|-----|----------|-------|\n| ORCL | -9% PM | Mega | Q4 beat but lower FY27 gross margin guide; $40B financing | Cloud / AI Infra |\n| ARM | -3.9% | Mega | Sector profit-taking; no company-specific news | AI Compute |\n| AMD | -3.8% | Mega | Broad semi selloff; profit-taking after +286% 12mo | AI Compute |\n| STX | -3.8% | Mega | Semi/storage sector rotation | Memory / Storage |\n| MU | -1.1% | Mega | Semi rotation; Goldman PT raised to $900 (Neutral) | Memory |\n| MRVL | -1.8% | Mega | Profit-taking post-Huang "next trillion" call; S&P 500 add 6/22 | AI Compute |\n| GLW | -1.9% | Mega | Continued slide post 6/8 gap-down | Optical / Specialty |\n| KO | +3.5% | Mega | Defensive rotation; World Cup tailwind (BofA) | Consumer Staples |\n| PM | +3.1% | Mega | Defensive rotation; €7M Italian fine (immaterial) | Consumer Staples |\n| COP | +3.9% | Mega | Oil rally on Iran escalation; WTI ~$89 | Energy |\n| CVX | +2.7% | Mega | Oil rally; Vaca Muerta NGL JV (Bloomberg) | Energy |\n| TMUS | +3.4% | Mega | Defensive telecom bid | Telecom |\n| VZ | +2.5% | Mega | Defensive telecom bid | Telecom |\n| CASY | **+18.8%** | Large | Q4 EPS $4.37 vs $3.32 est; rev $4.57B vs $4.35B; FY27 EBITDA +8-10% | Retail / C-Store |\n| DVN | +6.8% | Large | Post-Coterra merger guide; $8B buyback; Evercore upgrade to Outperform $54 PT | Energy E&P |\n| LITE | +6.8% | Large | Bounce; optical connectivity / AI data center narrative | Optical |\n| HOOD | +4.0% | Large | May data: 27.7M funded accounts, assets $377B (+48% YoY); IPO underwriter approval | Fintech |\n| HPE | -5.2% | Large | Continued post-earnings slide; bearish $55 call sweep $301K | AI Servers |\n| STRL | -7.8% | Large | Continued decline; no fresh catalyst | Construction |\n| FLEX | -6.3% | Large | Continued decline; no fresh catalyst | EMS |\n| STM | -2.4% | Large | Semi weakness; EU analog/auto exposure | Semis — Analog |\n| CBRL | **+27.2%** | Small | Q3 adj EPS $0.29 vs -$0.45 est; rev $797M vs $777M; raised FY26 guide | Restaurants |\n| FRMI | **+22.8%** | Small | Proxy campaign for board changes/strategic review; AI data center asset | AI Infra / Idiosyncratic |\n| QH | **+22.9%** | Small | After-hours short squeeze; no fundamental catalyst | Idiosyncratic |\n| VELO | **+14.5%** | Small | Aurelia gas-turbine partnership; additive mfg narrative | Industrial Tech |\n| MUSA | **+11.5%** | Small | Oil/gas price spike lifts fuel margins; KeyBanc PT $600 OW | Retail / C-Store |\n| OCC | **+25.2%** | Small | +83% 5D; no identifiable catalyst | Idiosyncratic |\n| FCEL | -8.9% | Small | Continued selloff post-Q2 miss; $42.6M impairment | Clean Energy |\n| ADUR | -7.3% | Small | Priced $15.6M public offering + $7.2M private placement at $15.20/sh | Cleantech |\n\n---\n\n### 🧭 Narrative Threads\n\n**U.S.-Iran Escalation Drives Oil & Defensive Rotation** `Energy / Macro` → ongoing\n\nThe dominant cross-asset force today. U.S. CENTCOM carried out additional "self-defense strikes" against Iran late Wednesday at Trump's direction; Iran's IRGC retaliated against U.S. bases in Jordan, Kuwait, and Bahrain. Trump warned "more strikes likely" if Iran doesn't make a deal. Brent crude above $95; WTI ~$89.\n\n- WTI +3.3% Wednesday to ~$91.07/bbl; Brent +2.9% to ~$94.10.\n- U.S. crude inventories drew 7.228M bbl (week ended June 5) vs 4M est.\n- **COP** +3.9%, **CVX** +2.7%, **PBR** +3.1% — broad energy bid. **DVN** +6.8% with post-Coterra merger guidance ($4.9B capex, 1.38M boe/d), $8B buyback, 33% dividend hike; Evercore upgraded to Outperform with $54 PT.\n- **KO** +3.5%, **PM** +3.1%, **MO** +2.5% — consumer staples outperforming as defensives draw inflows. Energy and staples were only S&P sectors positive Wednesday.\n- XLE +2.6% Wednesday; XOP +3.7%. JETS -3.8% (jet fuel costs). TAN -4.1% (rate sensitivity).\n- Trump says Iran deal could come in "two or three days"; Iran closed Strait of Hormuz to commercial vessels. Gasoline up 41% YoY per BLS.\n- Gold fell 3.1% to ~$4,128/oz (down ~13% this month); risk-off but rotation into energy/USD, not gold.\n- Illinois froze automatic gas tax hike through Jan 2027.\n\n**Inflation Prints & Fed Path** `Macro` → ongoing\n\nMay CPI came in at 4.2% YoY (highest since April 2023), matching estimates. Core CPI 0.2% MoM (vs 0.3% est), 2.9% YoY (vs 2.8% prior). Money markets now fully price a 25bp Fed hike by year-end. CME FedWatch: 98.3% probability of no change at June 17 meeting.\n\n- May PPI due today at 8:30 ET; economists expect +0.7% MoM (down from April's 1.4%).\n- Initial jobless claims also due 8:30 ET.\n- 10Y Treasury yield 4.53%; 2Y at 4.12%.\n- Mortgage rates above 7%; USD Index at 100.05.\n- BlackRock underweight long U.S. Treasuries; overweight U.S. equities, favoring AI beneficiaries.\n- Professor Siegel: "economy is not slowing"; market at 21-22x forward earnings "not cheap" but justifiable.\n- CNN Fear & Greed Index fell to 27.6 ("Fear") from 32.5.\n\n**Semiconductor Selloff / Profit-Taking** `AI Compute / Memory` → ongoing\n\nBroad semi weakness for a second day. SMH -2.9% Wednesday. Broadcom's maintained (not raised) $100B AI semi revenue forecast last week started the rotation.\n\n- **ARM** -3.9% (5D -25.2%); CEO Haas said CPU export controls to China would be hard to enforce. Named Oracle and ByteDance as new AGI CPU customers.\n- **AMD** -3.8% (5D -16.4%); KKM Financial trimmed position pre-pullback, called semis "frothy."\n- **MU** -1.1% (5D -16.4%); Goldman raised PT to $900 from $400 (Neutral maintained). Selected Bechtel for New York memory fab construction. ⚠️ Earnings June 24.\n- **MRVL** -1.8% (5D -14.5%); S&P 500 inclusion effective June 22. Liz Ann Sonders (Schwab) warned leveraged single-stock MU ETFs could "wipe out" retail traders.\n- **STX** -3.8%, **SNDK** +0.6% — mixed storage tape.\n- **STM** -2.4% (5D -11.5%); **UMC** -4.6% (5D -11.3%) — analog/foundry weakness.\n- **INTC** +0.5% — held better after 174% YTD run; Google 3M+ AI chip order for 2028 and Nvidia testing 18A as backup foundry provided fundamental support. U.S. government equity stake in INTC (433.3M shares at $20.47) established precedent for AI equity discussions.\n- TSM May revenue NT$417B (+30.1% YoY).\n\n**Oracle Q4: Beat on Numbers, Sell on Margin Guide & $40B Financing** `Cloud / AI Infra` → new\n\n**ORCL** dropped ~9% after hours despite a Q4 beat. CEO Sicilia said SaaS apocalypse fears have "quickly moved on."\n\n- Q4 revenue $19.18B vs $19.10B est (+21% YoY). Adj EPS $2.11 vs $1.96 est (+24% YoY).\n- Cloud revenue +47% YoY. Q1 guide: revenue +27-29%, cloud revenue +57-63%.\n- Q1 adj EPS guide $1.72-$1.76 vs $1.68 est.\n- CFO predicted lower gross margins for FY2027 — primary selloff catalyst.\n- Announced plans to raise ~$40B via debt and equity financing.\n- Piper Sandler maintained Overweight, raised PT to $225 from $210.\n- OpenAI partnership: OCI customers can access OpenAI frontier models and Codex via Oracle Universal Credits.\n- Jensen Huang bullish on software; Bill Ackman cautious on per-seat SaaS pricing (named Salesforce as at-risk).\n\n**Convenience Store Blowouts: CASY & CBRL** `Retail` → new\n\nTwo turnaround/execution stories delivered massive beats on the same day.\n\n- **CASY** +18.8% (3.9× avg volume). Q4 EPS $4.37 vs $3.32 est (+66% YoY). Revenue $4.57B vs $4.35B. Inside same-store sales +5.5%; inside margin 42.4%. Fuel margin 46.9 cpg (+9.3 cpg YoY). FY26 diluted EPS $19.16, net income $714M (both +31% YoY). FY27 guide: EBITDA +8-10%, 120 new stores. Dividend +14% to $0.65/qtr; buyback expanded to $1B. RBC maintained Sector Perform, raised PT to $794. Wings rollout showing +30% prepared food order frequency; pizza volumes +10% where wings sold.\n- **CBRL** +27.2% (14.9× avg volume). Q3 adj EPS $0.29 vs -$0.45 est. Revenue $797.4M vs $776.7M est. Raised FY26 revenue guide to $3.27-3.30B (from $3.24-3.27B) vs $3.25B est. Raised adj EBITDA guide to $120-125M. Wells Fargo upgraded to Overweight, PT raised to $50 from $35. Citi maintained Sell, raised PT to $34.\n- **MUSA** +11.5% — pump-price volatility from Iran conflict expected to boost fuel margins.\n\n**SpaceX IPO Eve** `Macro / IPO` → new\n\nSpaceX (SPCX) IPO prices tomorrow, June 12. Goldman Sachs and Morgan Stanley lead.\n\n- $250B+ in orders for a ~$75B offering (~3.5× oversubscribed). Valuation ~$1.75T.\n- Price-to-sales ~93.6× on 2025 revenue of $18.7B (company posted $4.9B loss in 2025).\n- Morningstar fair value est $780B — ~55% below IPO valuation.\n- Ron Baron: could reach $30T. Gary Black: retail TSLA holders will lighten to fund SPCX. Ross Gerber: "People hitting me up like it's free money."\n- Sen. Warren called for delay citing governance/valuation concerns. Damodaran passing on IPO.\n- Dual-class share structure: Musk's Class B = 10× voting.\n- Michael Burry's short bets on NVDA, PLTR, ORCL paying off amid selloff. Polymarket odds of AI bubble bursting by year-end in low 20s (up from 10% in April).\n\n**SaaS "Fab Five" vs. the Rest** `SaaS / Cloud` → ongoing\n\nBofA's Ikeda named **DDOG**, FROG, MDB, **SNOW**, TWLO as "Fab Five" — up 30% avg in 2026 vs IGV -12%.\n\n- **DDOG** +0.8%; massive PT raise wave post-earnings: Piper Sandler $275, Barclays $260, Wedbush $260, Evercore $265, CIBC $280, Canaccord $250. AI-related business >100% growth, >10% of revenue.\n- **SNOW** +1.2%; product revenue growth accelerated to 34% YoY. BofA Buy maintained.\n- Anthropic growth causing enterprise cost concerns: Uber reportedly burned full-year AI budget in first months of 2026. Palantir CEO Karp says customers "screaming" about AI token costs.\n- Congresswoman Salazar bought $15K-$50K DDOG twice in May.\n\n**AI Infrastructure Ecosystem** `AI Infra` → ongoing\n\n- **CRWV** (CoreWeave) +1.4% PM; announced $3.5B senior notes due 2032. Jim Chanos flagged $2.3B insider selling since IPO.\n- **FRMI** +22.8%; co-founder Neugebauer launched proxy campaign seeking strategic review (potential sale/merger) of Project Matador AI power/data center platform in Texas. Unconfirmed social media speculation re: OpenAI interest. Options activity: Fri $7.5 calls swept.\n- KKR launched $10B data center group led by former AWS chief (FT).\n- OpenAI eyes 10-GW Ohio mega campus with Nvidia backing. Filed confidential S-1 June 8; targeting Sept 2026 listing.\n- Anthropic filed confidential S-1 June 1; valued at ~$965B after $65B Series H.\n- OpenAI considering "drastic price cuts" to counter Anthropic (WSJ).\n- Meta severed Manus data access after China ordered buyout unwound (Bloomberg).\n\n**Cybersecurity Steady** `Cybersecurity` → ongoing\n\n- **CRWD** +0.4% — outperformed Nasdaq -2% on Wednesday. Q1 beat-and-raise: rev $1.39B, adj EPS $1.10; FY27 guide $5.92-5.96B rev. 4-for-1 split effective July 2. ⚠️ Next earnings ~Aug 26.\n- **PANW** +1.3%; IBD SwingTrader reportedly bought stock.\n- **FTNT** +0.8%.\n- CrowdStrike report: China behind 58% of state-backed cyberattacks on tech firms; North Korea infiltrating IT workforces.\n\n**📡 Undercurrents** *(developing, not yet dominant)*\n- **HUM** +0.3% (5D +12.2%): agreed to divest Gentiva minority stake for ~$900M; proceeds for general corporate purposes. Closing expected Q3 2026.\n- **HOOD** received approval to serve as IPO underwriter — positions Robinhood for SpaceX/OpenAI/Anthropic wave. Cathie Wood's ARKK sold 89,915 HOOD shares (~$7.8M) on Wednesday.\n- U.S. government equity stake in AI companies moving from concept to active negotiation. Trump said he plans to meet AI executives "very shortly." OpenAI proposed 1-5% equity donation