2026-06-28	daily	benzinga	complete	### 🗓 2026-06-28 — Pre-Market Brief\n\n**One-liner**: Memory/chip stocks reversed sharply Friday (STX −11%, WDC −12%, MU −5%) after Thursday's Micron blowout, while software names surged (NOW +9%, WDAY +7%, IGV +3.2%), oil cratered 4.7% on Hormuz ceasefire violations, and MSFT posted its worst month since 2000 amid $190B capex fears.\n\n---\n\n### ⚡ Top Movers At a Glance\n\n| Ticker | Move | Cap | Catalyst | Theme |\n|--------|------|-----|----------|-------|\n| **NOW** | +9.1% | Mega | Dip-buying; software rotation from chips into IGV | SaaS |\n| **APP** | +7.6% | Mega | Software sector rotation; IGV +3.2% on day | AdTech / SaaS |\n| **LLY** | +6.6% | Mega | EMA CHMP positive opinion Jaypirca CLL; Medicare GLP-1 bridge July 1 | Biopharma ⚠️ |\n| **PLTR** | +5.2% | Mega | Oversold bounce from 52-week low; Ark bought 30,528 shares Thu | AI Software |\n| **MSFT** | +5.0% | Mega | Bounce from 52-week low; June MTD −21.6%, worst month since Dec 2000 | Hyperscaler / AI Infra |\n| **IBM** | +4.7% | Mega | Unveiled world's first sub-1nm (0.7nm) "nanostack" chip technology | Semis / Foundry |\n| **STX** | −11.3% | Mega | Profit-taking post-MU earnings; memory sector reversal | Memory / Storage |\n| **WDC** | −12.0% | Mega | Profit-taking post-MU earnings; memory sector reversal | Memory / Storage |\n| **SNDK** | −7.4% | Mega | Gave back portion of Thu +22% rally on MU earnings | Memory / Storage |\n| **MU** | −4.8% | Mega | Profit-taking after Thu +15.7%; Q3 rev $41.46B vs $35.59B est | Memory / AI Infra |\n| **ARM** | −5.0% | Mega | Ongoing semi selloff; 5D −25% | AI Compute |\n| **MRVL** | −5.1% | Mega | Semi selloff; CFO sold $632K of shares June 23 | AI Networking |\n| **DELL** | −4.2% | Mega | GF Securities downgrade to Hold; terminated Arrow distribution deal | AI Servers |\n| **AMAT** | −1.7% | Mega | Reversal after Thu +9.7% on new chipmaking systems launch | Semi Equipment |\n| **ON** | −21%* | Large | Acquiring Synaptics in $7B all-stock deal; SYNA +5% | Semis / M&A |\n| **CBRS** | +8.3% | Large | Bounce; Ark bought 111,989 shares Thu across ARKK/ARKW | AI Compute |\n| **ASTS** | +8.2% | Large | Continued satellite momentum; Japan regulatory clearance | Space / Telecom |\n| **WDAY** | +7.1% | Large | Software rotation; IGV +3.2% vs SMH −3.4% | SaaS |\n| **VEEV** | +7.1% | Large | Software rotation; 5D +10.8% | SaaS / Healthcare IT |\n| **ZS** | +6.0% | Large | Aston Martin F1 cybersecurity partnership announced | Cybersecurity |\n| **INTU** | +5.6% | Large | Oversold mean-reversion near 52-week low; YTD −57% | SaaS / Fintech |\n| **DLTR** | +5.6% | Large | Mantle Ridge selling 12.8M shares; DLTR buying back $500M | Retail |\n| **SNOW** | +5.5% | Large | Software sector rotation | Cloud / Data |\n| **MDB** | +5.5% | Large | Software sector rotation | Database / SaaS |\n| **UMC** | −7.5% | Large | South Korea/Asia semi selloff spillover | Foundry |\n| **NBIS** | −6.4% | Large | Ongoing pressure; 5D −16.9% | AI Infra |\n| **HPE** | −5.6% | Large | Semi/AI infra rotation; memory cost pass-through fears | AI Servers |\n| **FCEL** | +22.3% | Small | Jefferies upgrade Hold→Buy, PT $16→$24; Fit Energy 380MW deal | Clean Energy |\n| **FUBO** | +18.9% | Small | Momentum; no specific catalyst identified | Streaming |\n| **APOG** | +16.2% | Small | Q1 adj EPS $0.57 vs $0.45 est; rev $342.7M vs $333.8M est | Industrials / Building |\n| **CHYM** | +12.2% | Small | Continued momentum; no specific catalyst identified | Biopharma |\n| **ARQQ** | −11.2% | Small | Sell-off; no specific catalyst identified | Quantum |\n\n*ON not in screener but largest Russell 1000 decliner at −21%+; included for context.\n\n---\n\n### 🧭 Narrative Threads\n\n**AI Infrastructure Cost Pass-Through** `AI Infra / Memory` → ongoing\n\nThe Micron blowout exposed a two-sided trade: memory suppliers win, but their customers — the hyperscalers and device makers — absorb the cost. **AAPL** fell 6.15% Thursday after raising Mac/iPad prices, citing memory costs up 2.5×; CEO Cook called it a "hundred-year flood." **MSFT** raised Xbox prices Friday citing the same memory crunch and warning costs could double again by fall 2027. The reversal hit memory names hard Friday: **STX** −11.3%, **WDC** −12.0%, **SNDK** −7.4%, **MU** −4.8%.\n\n- **MU** Q3: revenue $41.46B vs $35.59B est; adj EPS $25.11 vs $20.63 est; gross margin 84.6%; Q4 guide rev ~$50B vs $42.92B est, EPS ~$31 vs ~$25 est.\n- MU disclosed 16 multi-year "Strategic Customer Agreements" worth minimum $100B in contracted revenue with pricing floors.\n- Micron CEO Mehrotra: tight supply to persist beyond calendar 2027; HBM supply meeting ~50% of demand.\n- BofA analyst Arya raised MU PT to $1,550; Barclays to $2,000; Goldman neutral at $1,100.\n- **AAPL** raised MacBook/iPad prices; Tim Cook: memory situation is a "hundred-year flood."\n- **MSFT** raised Xbox prices effective Aug 1; expects memory costs to double by fall 2027. MSFT −21.6% MTD, worst month since Dec 2000. Stifel maintained Hold, cut PT to $400; warned Street FY27 EPS ~$1 too high.\n- **DELL** −4.2%; GF Securities downgraded to Hold from Buy on valuation; also terminated Arrow distribution deal.\n- Dan Ives (Wedbush): MSFT and META being treated "like bear market names"; calls it "Year 3 of a 10-year AI buildout."\n- MAGS ETF (Magnificent Seven equal-weight) −13% in June, >$1B in outflows MTD — worst month since inception.\n- Memory now absorbs ~30% of hyperscaler data-center spending in 2026, ~4× its 2023 share.\n- Upcoming: **NKE** earnings Tue June 30 ⚠️; **STZ** earnings Tue June 30 ⚠️.\n\n**Software Rotation — The Anti-Chip Trade** `SaaS / Enterprise Software` → new\n\nFriday's session saw the sharpest software-vs-semiconductor divergence of the year: **IGV** (iShares Software) +3.2% while **SMH** (VanEck Semis) −3.4%, a ~6.5 pp gap. Beaten-down SaaS names rallied hard as capital rotated from AI hardware into software.\n\n- **NOW** +9.1% — no specific catalyst; dip-buying near oversold levels.\n- **WDAY** +7.1%, **VEEV** +7.1%, **SNOW** +5.5%, **MDB** +5.5%, **CRM** +4.4%, **SAP** +3.7% — all moved on sector rotation.\n- **INTU** +5.6% — bouncing near 52-week low after −57% YTD; Goldman downgraded to Sell in June (PT $276).\n- **ZS** +6.0% — announced Aston Martin F1 cybersecurity partnership; KeyBanc Overweight, PT lowered to $176.\n- **PLTR** +5.2% — snapped 7-day losing streak from 52-week low $106.37; Ark bought 30,528 shares Thu; still −33% in June, worst month since 2021.\n- **APP** +7.6% — benefiting from software rotation.\n- **OKTA** +3.9% — BTIG maintained Buy, raised PT to $136.\n\n**Hormuz Tensions Re-Escalate; Oil Posts Worst Month Since March 2020** `Macro / Geopolitical` → ongoing\n\nWTI crude fell 4.7% to ~$68.55, down ~23% for June — worst monthly performance since the COVID crash. Despite US-Iran ceasefire violations, the market no longer prices a war premium.\n\n- Trump accused Iran of firing 4 one-way attack drones at Hormuz shipping; US struck Iranian missile/drone storage and coastal radar sites Friday.\n- Iran's IRGC said it struck US military positions in retaliation; Bahrain reported Iranian drone attack on its territory.\n- UKMTO raised Hormuz security threat level to "substantial"; published mine warning area.\n- Gulf exports recovered to ~63% of pre-war levels (Goldman); Dubai prompt timespreads flipped to contango.\n- US Treasury issued 60-day waiver authorizing Iranian oil sales; Goldman estimates ~60M bbl of Iranian crude on water could unlock.\n- GasBuddy: US gas prices declining faster from peak than during 2022's record $5/gal episode.\n- National avg gas $3.90/gal (AAA); California still >$5.48/gal.\n- US-Israel-Lebanon signed trilateral framework agreement Friday.\n- Baker Hughes: oil rig count +7 to 440; natgas +3 to 125.\n\n**Macro Data — Inflation Sticky, GDP Revised Up** `Macro` → ongoing\n\n- May PCE headline +4.1% YoY (highest since Apr 2023), core +3.4% YoY (highest since Oct 2023) — both in line with estimates.\n- Q1 GDP third estimate revised up to 2.1% annualized from 1.6% prior.\n- Initial jobless claims 215K vs 225K est (−12K WoW).\n- Durable goods orders −4.5% vs −3.2% est.\n- US goods trade deficit widened to $105.8B in May from $83B prior (widest in >1 year; est $85B).\n- UMich consumer sentiment final June 49.5 vs 48.9 est; expectations 50.7 vs 49.3 est.\n- S&P affirmed US 'AA+/A-1+' sovereign ratings; outlook stable.\n- BofA now expects 3 Fed rate hikes in 2026 (Sept, Oct, Dec) to 4.25-4.50%.\n- CME FedWatch: 72.2% probability Fed holds rates in July.\n- Trump "eased pressure" on Fed Chair Warsh as inflation tops 4% (CNBC).\n\n**SpaceX Post-IPO: Nasdaq-100 Entry, $25B Bond Sale, Paper Losses** `Space / IPO` → ongoing\n\n**SPCX** closed at $153.23 Friday, −32% from its $225.64 peak and still above $135 IPO price. Nasdaq confirmed SPCX joins Nasdaq-100 on July 7. JP Morgan estimates ~$4.3B in passive inflows from index rebalance.\n\n- SpaceX issued $25B in senior unsecured notes across 5 maturities (2031-2056) via Bank of NY Mellon; coupons 5.35%-6.65%.\n- Bloomberg: paper losses on the bond offering reached ~$305M since trading began; 30-year tranche widened 0.28 pp past issue spread — worst recent IG deal performance recalled by credit traders.\n- Polymarket: 35% chance SPCX ends June above IPO price (down from ~80% a week ago).\n- Short interest rose to 13% of free float from 8% (Ortex).\n- Russell reconstitution Friday added SPCX to Russell 1000; ~$12.2T benchmarked to Russell US indexes.\n- Jeremy Grantham: Buffett Indicator at 235% of GDP — "most expensive market in US history"; called SPCX IPO "one of the defining peaks of all time."\n- Triller Group (ILLR) +296.6% Thu after announcing acquisition of $411M SpaceX economic exposure at $105/share.\n- Quantum Cyber (QUCY) +24.5% Fri after board approved SpaceX equity stake acquisition.\n\n**AI Frontier Models — Government Gatekeeping Intensifies** `AI Policy / Regulation` → ongoing\n\nThe US government is asserting control over frontier AI model distribution. Anthropic and OpenAI both face deployment restrictions.\n\n- Commerce Dept granted Anthropic permission to release Claude Mythos 5 to ~100 companies and federal agencies; Fable 5 access still blocked.\n- OpenAI launched GPT-5.6 Sol/Terra/Luna with restricted initial distribution to vetted partners at government's request; GPT-5.6 Sol described as flagship with "max effort" and "ultra" agent modes.\n- OpenAI reportedly leaning toward delaying IPO to 2027; CEO Altman called any reduction from $1T valuation a "non-starter" (NYT).\n- Anthropic accused Alibaba/Qwen of massive "distillation" campaign: ~25,000 fraudulent accounts, 28.8M Claude exchanges between April 22 and June 5. BABA hit new 52-week low.\n- Anthropic confidentially filed S-1 on June 1; Polymarket gives it 77% probability of beating OpenAI to public markets.\n\n**Russell Reconstitution & Index Mechanics** `Market Structure` → ongoing\n\nFriday's close marked the annual Russell reconstitution — one of the largest in history.\n\n- 43 stocks graduating from Russell 2000 to Russell 1000, including **BE** ($87.1B mkt cap, +1,158% YoY), **CRDO** ($50B), **SATS** ($28.4B), **IONQ**, **FN**, **DOCN**, **STRL**.\n- 165 Russell 2000 stocks above the traditional $5.7B small-cap cutoff; 6 now top $20B.\n- SPCX added to Russell 1000 effective Friday close; Nasdaq-100 inclusion July 7.\n- GOOGL replaces VZ in DJIA effective Monday June 29.\n- Alphabet hit by fresh AI talent departures: Gemini co-lead Noam Shazeer to OpenAI; Nobel-winning DeepMind scientist John Jumper to Anthropic — 5+ researchers left in 7 days.\n\n**Biopharma Catalysts Stacking** `Biopharma` → ongoing\n\n- **LLY** +6.6%: EMA CHMP positive opinion for Jaypirca (pirtobrutinib) in CLL across all lines of therapy; Medicare GLP-1 Bridge program launches July 1 — eligible Part D patients can access Foundayo/Zepbound for $50/month; Leerink raised PT to $1,232.\n- **VRDN** (Viridian Therapeutics): FDA approved Lumvoa for thyroid eye disease — first approved treatment with labeling for both active and chronic TED; plans immediate launch. Conference call Mon June 29 8am ET ⚠️.\n- **ABBV**: FDA approved SKYRIZI for pediatric plaque psoriasis (≥6 years) — first IL-23 inhibitor approved for pediatric patients <40kg.\n- **LNTH** (Lantheus): FDA issued Complete Response Letter for LNTH-2501 due to third-party manufacturing facility issues; no safety/efficacy concerns identified.\n- **MRNA** +13.8% (day's biggest R1000 gainer) on Science Day pipeline presentation.\n- **CAPR** −13%: FDA advisory committee meeting set for July 29 to review Deramiocel BLA for Duchenne muscular dystrophy ⚠️.\n\n**ON Semi / Synaptics — Chip M&A** `Semis / M&A` → new\n\n**ON** cratered −21% (largest R1000 decliner) after announcing all-stock acquisition of **SYNA** at ~$7B enterprise value. SYNA +5%.\n\n- Synaptics shareholders receive 1.350 ON shares per SYNA share (~19% premium on 10-day VWAP).\n- Deal expands ON's TAM by $30B to $243B by 2030; targets Physical AI (autonomous vehicles, robotics, AR/VR).\n- Expected accretive to non-GAAP EPS within 18 months; ~$200M annual synergies.\n- Close expected mid-2027, subject to SYNA shareholder approval and regulatory clearance.\n- Wells Fargo raised SYNA PT to $160 (Overweight).\n\n**Bitcoin / Crypto Winter Deepens** `Crypto` → ongoing\n\nBTC at ~$60,460, down from $126,300 ATH. Spot Bitcoin ETFs had worst weekly outflows ever: −$1.79B this week, −$4.06B in June (monthly record).\n\n- MSTR (Strategy) stock dropped from $543 peak to $82; total debt >$8B; preferred stock STRC fell to $72.50 (26% below par).\n- Rosen Law Firm announced investigation into Strategy and Saylor for potential securities violations.\n- Peter Schiff: "MSTR is in a death spiral."\n- Bitcoin declared dead for 472nd time; on-chain supply held at loss reached record 10.83M BTC.\n- CFTC launched "extensive investigation" into Polymarket (Politico); bipartisan senators Curtis/Schiff urged probe over alleged deceptive marketing.\n- Polymarket suffered ~$3M front-end exploit after third-party vendor compromise; pledged full refunds.\n\n**📡 Undercurrents** *(developing, not yet dominant)*\n- **SK Hynix** planning US ADR listing on Nasdaq — would give US investors direct access to world's largest HBM supplier alongside MU; could broaden the AI memory trade.\n- **IBM** unveiled 0.7nm "nanostack" chip architecture (world's first sub-1nm); expects earliest commercial adoption within 5 years — potential long-term foundry disruption.\n- **Private credit gating wave continues**: Morgan Stanley's $7B North Haven fund honored only 43% of Q2 redemption requests (11.6% of units sought withdrawal); Cliffwater, Partners Group, Ares all restricting redemptions.\n- **FCC widened ban** on Chinese telecom/surveillance equipment (Huawei, ZTE, Hikvision, Dahua) to include legacy models; takes effect early July.\n- **GameStop** reaffirmed eBay acquisition pursuit despite initial rejection; projected FY2026 adj EBITDA >$600M vs $345.4M in FY2025.\n- **Meta/Zuckerberg** reportedly exploring working with Polymarket and Kalshi; separately directed Meta to build standalone prediction market app "Arena."\n- **Circle (CRCL)** +5.9%: signed MOU with Nomura for on-chain finance and stablecoin infrastructure collaboration in Japan.\n\n---\n\n### 📰 Channel Pulse\n\n| Channel | Material fact |\n|---------|--------------|\n| Earnings | **APOG** Q1 adj EPS $0.57 vs $0.45 est, rev $342.7M vs $333.8M est; affirmed FY27 guide $2.70-$3.25 EPS. **DRI** Q4 adj EPS $3.66 vs $3.63 est, rev $3.719B missed $3.728B est; FY27 sales guide $13.60-13.75B. **MKC** Q2 EPS $0.80 vs $0.69 est, rev $1.937B vs $1.912B est. **BB** Q1 EPS $0.04 vs $0.03 est, rev $152.9M (+26% YoY) vs $138.18M est; raised FY27 guidance. **CNVS** Q4 EPS $0.05 vs $(0.01) est, rev $26.0M vs $21.9M est. |\n| Macro | May PCE headline +4.1% YoY, core +3.4% YoY (both highest since 2023); Q1 GDP revised to 2.1%; goods trade deficit widened to $105.8B (widest in >1 year). |\n| Tech | OpenAI launched GPT-5.6 Sol/Terra/Luna with government-restricted distribution; Anthropic Mythos 5 cleared for ~100 partners; Apple Vision Pro hardware chief Paul Meade leaving for OpenAI. |\n| Geopolitical | US struck Iranian missile/drone storage after Hormuz drone attacks on commercial shipping; Iran retaliated against US positions; WTI −4.7% to ~$68.55, worst month since March 2020. |\n| M&A | ON Semi acquiring Synaptics in $7B all-stock deal; AES shareholders approved $10.7B GIP/EQT takeover at $15/share; Bio-Techne (TECH) acquired by Merck KGaA for $11.3B ($73/share). |\n| Regulation | CFTC launched "extensive investigation" into Polymarket; FCC widened Chinese telecom equipment ban effective July; Trump threatened 100% tariffs on countries with digital services taxes. |\n\n---\n\n### 👁 On The Radar\n\n- **NKE** reports Q4 earnings **Tue June 30** — consensus rev $10.85B (−2.19% YoY); multiple PT cuts (Stifel $50, Deutsche Bank $43). ⚠️\n- **STZ** (Constellation Brands) reports Q1 earnings **Tue June 30** — consensus EPS $3.25, rev $2.4B. ⚠️\n- **SPCX** joins Nasdaq-100 **Mon July 7**; JP Morgan estimates ~$4.3B in passive inflows. IPO quiet period ends same day. ⚠️\n- **GOOGL** replaces VZ in DJIA effective **Mon June 29** open. ⚠️\n- **VRDN** conference call **Mon June 29 8:00am ET** following FDA approval of Lumvoa for thyroid eye disease. ⚠️\n- **LLY** Medicare GLP-1 Bridge program coverage begins **Tue July 1** — Foundayo/Zepbound at $50/month for eligible Part D patients. ⚠️\n- **CAPR** FDA advisory committee meeting **July 29** for Deramiocel BLA (Duchenne muscular dystrophy). ⚠️\n- **INTC** reports quarterly results **July 23** — EPS est $0.19 vs $(0.10) YoY; Goldman initiated Neutral at $150.\n- **SPCX** lockup expiry begins **Aug 8** (11.8% of total float eligible); former SEC chair Gensler warned of "great rebalancing."\n- **MU** Q4 guide implies ~$50B rev and ~$31 EPS — consensus had been ~$43B/$25; next report est ~late Sept.\n- **BofA** now expects 3 Fed rate hikes in 2026 (Sept/Oct/Dec) to 4.25-4.50%.\n\n---\n\n### 🚫 What to Ignore Today\n\n- **Jensen Huang Zen story / Micron CEO Berkeley anecdote** — profile pieces with no market-moving content; color filler.\n- **Dave Ramsey relationship advice / Suze Orman Social Security commentary** — personal finance content with zero portfolio relevance.\n- **Usha Vance Old Navy dress / JD Vance budget joke** — political/lifestyle noise; no market signal despite GAP ticker mention.	\N	{"calls": [{"step": "step4_synthesis", "model": "claude-opus-4-6", "api_model": "claude-opus-4-6", "input_tokens": 486787, "output_tokens": 6278, "pricing_model": "claude-opus-4-6", "input_cost_usd": 2.433935, "total_cost_usd": 2.590885, "output_cost_usd": 0.15695, "cache_read_cost_usd": 0.0, "cache_write_cost_usd": 0.0, "cache_read_input_tokens": 0, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 0}], "call_count": 1, "started_at": "2026-06-28T01:26:31.513583+00:00", "completed_at": "2026-06-28T01:29:36.179562+00:00", "current_step": null, "pricing_note": "Token counts from Anthropic Messages API response.usage. USD estimated from published claude.com/api pricing (input, 5m cache write, cache read, output) — Anthropic does not return dollar amounts on the response.", "total_cost_usd": 2.5909, "total_input_tokens": 486787, "total_output_tokens": 6278, "total_cache_read_input_tokens": 0, "total_cache_creation_input_tokens": 0}	{"asof": "2026-06-28", "counts": {"prompt": {"by_section": {"Channel: tech": 21, "Channel: movers": 32, "Channel: general": 98, "Channel: markets": 18, "Channel: earnings": 19, "Channel: equities": 29, "Channel: commodities": 8, "Ticker Group: r1d_screener": 36, "Ticker Group: vol_spike_5d": 34, "Ticker Group: ti65_mid_small": 1, "Ticker Group: ti65_mega_large": 20, "Channel: macro_economic_events": 13}, "articles_total": 329}, "upserted": 329, "api_by_fetch": {"ticker": {"DD": {"error": null, "api_rows": 0, "