2026-10-05	daily	perplexity	complete	\n\n### 🗓 Monday, October 5, 2026 — Pre-Market Brief\n\n**One-liner**: Schneider Electric's $22.6B cash bid for **PTC** (+37% PM), Brazil's Bolsonaro-led election lifting **ITUB/BBD** 13%+, and a Qualcomm–Huawei patent deal headline the Monday session, while Friday's tape was shaped by **TSLA** Q3 delivery beat (+4.65%), **ACN** post-earnings profit-taking (−6.31%), the **ON/SYNA** revised $5.7B cash deal, Toshiba's HDD expansion crushing **STX/WDC**, and a broad semiconductor rally after September payrolls of just 29,000.\n\n---\n\n### ⚡ Top Movers At a Glance\n\n| Ticker | Move | Cap | Catalyst | Theme |\n|--------|------|-----|----------|-------|\n| **PTC** | +37% PM | Large | Schneider Electric $205/sh all-cash acquisition ($22.6B equity) | Industrial Software |\n| **ITUB** | +13% PM | Large | Bolsonaro leads Brazil first round 47% vs Lula 45% | Macro / EM |\n| **BBD** | +13% PM | Large | Same Brazil election catalyst | Macro / EM |\n| **EWZ** | +12% PM | ETF | Brazil election; runoff Oct 25 | Macro / EM |\n| **TSLA** | +4.65% | Mega | Q3 deliveries 486,532 vs 456K–461K est | EV / Auto |\n| **SPCX** | +7.35% | Mega | 3 launches in <13 hrs; Starship Flight 14 orbit milestone | Space |\n| **ASX** | +6.08% | Mega | Aug revenue +45.7% YoY; LEAP guidance >$3.5B; AMD Venice ramp | Test & Adv. Packaging |\n| **ARM** | +5.18% | Mega | Broad semi rally; Nvidia BlueField-4 Arm-based disclosure | IP Licensing / AI |\n| **TXN** | +4.44% | Mega | Broad semi rally; no new catalyst identified | Analog |\n| **FCX** | +4.00% | Mega | Q3 copper ~830M lbs in line; Grasberg ramping to 80% by mid-2027 | Materials |\n| **AVGO** | +3.35% | Mega | $42B convertible-note facility for Anthropic infrastructure | AI Compute |\n| **DELL** | +3.84% | Mega | Broad AI-infra rally; no new catalyst identified | AI Infrastructure |\n| **KLAC** | +3.27% | Mega | Broad semi rally; Q1 FY27 earnings Oct 28 ⚠️ | Chip Equipment |\n| **ASML** | +3.25% | Mega | Broad semi rally; Q3 earnings ~Oct 13 ⚠️ | Chip Equipment |\n| **AMD** | +2.95% | Mega | World Labs $8.2B all-stock deal; Vultr Helios order via HPE | AI Compute |\n| **QCOM** | ~+2.9% PM | Mega | Huawei multi-year patent deal: 5G, AI, compute, networking | Wireless |\n| **ACN** | −6.31% | Mega | Post-Q4 profit-taking after +16% Oct 1; FY27 guide 3–6% organic | IT Services |\n| **INTC** | −0.56% | Mega | Foundry losses $2.1B/qtr; High-NA EUV in production; Q3 Oct 22 ⚠️ | Semiconductors |\n| **TER** | +8.00% | Large | AI memory test revenue record $212M; broad semi rally | Test Equipment |\n| **HPE** | +7.36% | Large | $1.2B Vultr Helios order; networking rev guide raised; Juniper synergies to $800M | AI Infrastructure |\n| **JBS** | +7.46% | Large | No in-window catalyst identified | Food / Protein |\n| **STM** | +7.03% | Large | Broad semi rally; Q3 earnings Oct 29 ⚠️ | Analog |\n| **MTSI** | +6.62% | Large | 3.2Tb optical chipset launch; BMO upgrade to Outperform | Optical / Photonic |\n| **ON** | +6.01% | Large | Revised SYNA deal to $123/sh cash ($5.7B); removes dilution | Semiconductors |\n| **MPWR** | +5.79% | Large | Broad semi rally; Wall St Zen upgrade to Buy | Power Semi |\n| **NTAP** | +5.22% | Large | SMCI AI-factory collaboration (Sep 29); 2.1× avg vol | Storage / AI Infra |\n| **DKNG** | +5% PM | Large | BofA upgrade to Buy; prediction-mkt fees est $400M by 2027 | Internet / Platforms |\n| **WFC** | +1% PM | Large | Morgan Stanley upgrade to Overweight | Banking |\n| **FICO** | −6.6% | Large | Bloomberg: FHFA may require only 2-of-3 credit bureaus | Credit Scoring |\n| **SYNA** | +14.08% | Mid | ON revised to $123/sh all-cash from ~$7B stock deal | Semiconductors |\n| **MXL** | +14.99% | Mid | Optical-chip demand; 26–28 wk lead times; Sep +50.7% | Comms / Networking |\n| **IMOS** | +14.61% | Mid | Memory-packaging demand; Aug profit +118% YoY; 2.7× vol | Test & Packaging |\n| **WOLF** | +13.19% | Small | 200mm SiC substrate launch; 49.6% short interest; AI DC rev 2× YoY | Wide-Bandgap |\n| **AZTA** | +12.38% | Small | FQ3 EPS $0.16 vs $0.10 est; rev $161M vs $150M est | Life Sciences |\n| **WNC** | +12.68% | Small | Post-payroll rate-relief trade; backlog +14% QoQ to $956M | Transport Equip |\n| **NNBR** | +24.24% | Small | $50M PIPE at $3.30; retiring Series D pfd; 6× vol | Specialty Mfg |\n| **STX** | ~−10% | Large | Toshiba to double AI-DC HDD capacity by FY2027; $380M investment | Memory / HDD |\n| **WDC** | ~−10% | Large | Same Toshiba HDD expansion catalyst | Memory / HDD |\n\n---\n\n### 🧭 Narrative Threads\n\n**Semiconductor Broad Rally — Payroll-Driven Rate Relief + AI Demand** `AI Compute · Chip Equipment · Analog · Test & Packaging` → ongoing\n\nFriday's September payrolls miss (29K vs 90K est) crushed near-term hike odds from ~64% a week ago to ~18–23%, sending yields lower and lifting rate-sensitive, high-multiple semis. SOXX +2.18%, SMH +2.07%. The rally layered on top of ongoing AI-infrastructure demand data.\n\n- **ARM** +5.18%, **AMD** +2.95%, **AVGO** +3.35%, **TXN** +4.44%, **KLAC** +3.27%, **ASML** +3.25%, **MPWR** +5.79%, **STM** +7.03%, **ON** +6.01%, **MTSI** +6.62%.\n- **ASX** +6.08%: Aug Taiwan revenue TWD 82.2B (+45.7% YoY); LEAP sales guidance >$3.5B FY26, >$7B FY27; benefiting from AMD Venice ramp and TSM order spillover.\n- **TER** +8.00%: AI memory test revenue hit record $212M in Q2; third consecutive quarter >$200M. Q3 earnings Oct 27 ⚠️.\n- **IMOS** +14.61%: Taiwan semi-test/packaging sector surged; Aug net profit +118% YoY; memory-packaging ~51% of Q2 rev; gross margin expanded to 17.96% from 6.6% a year ago. UBS PT NT$166.\n- **MXL** +14.99%: Optical-chip lead times 26–28 weeks; CFO targeting ~3× market-share gains over 2–3 yrs; Sep single-month gain +50.7%; StoneX PT $125, Stifel PT $120.\n- **WOLF** +13.19%: Launched premium 200mm SiC substrate (<0.01 micropipes/cm²); AI DC revenue >2× YoY; ~49.6% short interest — squeeze potential. Median PT $28 vs $35.28 close.\n- **INTC** reportedly planning ~10% PC processor price increase effective Oct 5 (DigiTimes/supply chain).\n- **ASML** Q3 earnings ~Oct 13 ⚠️; FY26 guide €43–45B. **KLAC** Q1 FY27 Oct 28 ⚠️. **STM** Q3 Oct 29 ⚠️.\n- TSMC Q3 results Oct 15 ⚠️; advanced-node prices could rise 5–10% next year, advanced packaging 10–15% (TrendForce/CNA).\n- Samsung prelim Q3 results Oct 8 ⚠️.\n- TrendForce: Q4 conventional DRAM contracts +10–15% QoQ, NAND +15–20%.\n\n**AI Infrastructure Financing & Capex Cycle** `AI Compute · AI Infrastructure` → ongoing\n\nAnthropic's IPO prospectus revealed the scale of AI-infrastructure commitments: $518B over 10 years, with ~80% non-cancelable. Broadcom sits at the center of the financing chain.\n\n- **AVGO** +3.35%: Agreed to provide Anthropic up to **$42B in convertible notes** for infrastructure tied to a $125.2B, 5-year TPU lease. Bloomberg reports a $60B total package: $42B Class A senior secured (syndicated by BofA, Citi, MS) + $18B Class B junior (led by Blackstone, $9B own funds).\n- Anthropic's 10-yr commitments: ~$111B Google, ~$110B Amazon, ~$31.4B Microsoft, ~$84.5B xAI (90-day termination clause), ~$161.2B Broadcom-related equipment leases.\n- OpenAI: SoftBank and Nvidia each completed final $10B payments (Oct 1) of the $852B post-money March round. OpenAI reportedly exploring >$30B pre-IPO round at >$1.4T valuation.\n- Andreessen Horowitz estimates 2026 capex by GOOGL/AMZN/META/MSFT/ORCL at ~$780B, vs $416B in 2025; Goldman/others put it at ~$940B.\n- AllianceBernstein head of equities Nelson Yu: hyperscaler capex revisions are the "#1" item for Q3 earnings.\n- Schwab strategist Kevin Gordon: S&P 500 ~1% from highs but avg stock down 14% since Aug; one mega-cap AI-capex miss could disrupt earnings.\n- Goldman Sachs raised 2026 YE US data-center capacity forecast by 5 GW to 64 GW; cut 2027 forecast by 5 GW to 90 GW.\n\n**PTC Acquisition by Schneider Electric** `Software & SaaS · Industrials` → new\n\n- **PTC** +37% PM: Schneider agreed to acquire PTC for **$205/sh cash**, equity value ~$22.6B, EV ~$23.7B; **42.3% premium** to Friday close. Close expected Q3 2027.\n- Implied valuation: 21× 2027E adj. EBITA pre-synergies, 13× post-synergies.\n- Cost synergies: €250M annual run-rate by year 3; revenue synergies ~€800M.\n- Financing: ~€5–6B new equity (accelerated bookbuild) + ~€16–17B new debt; bridge from Morgan Stanley & SocGen.\n- Schneider shares fell >7% in Europe on dilution concerns. Schneider targets Category A credit ratings post-deal.\n- Jefferies' Lucas Ferhani: fills Schneider PLM gap; Berenberg's Nay Soe Naing: healthy valuation in compressed software environment.\n- Read-through for industrial-software peers (ANSS, CDNS, SNPS) not yet quantified by sell-side.\n\n**HDD Supply Shock — Toshiba Doubles AI-DC Capacity** `Memory & Interconnect` → new\n\nToshiba plans ¥60B ($380M) investment in Philippines to double AI-data-center HDD capacity by FY2027 and target 30% market share (from ~10%).\n\n- **STX** ~−10%, **WDC** ~−10% on Friday.\n- Toshiba roadmap: 30TB-class drives in 2027, 65TB-class ~2030, 100TB-class later.\n- Citi analyst Asiya Merchant: impact may be limited — Toshiba relies on outside media/head suppliers who must also expand.\n- Morgan Stanley: HDD supply-demand gap through 2028 wider than Toshiba's addition; characterizes selloff as buying opportunity.\n- Rosenblatt: medium-term share recapture; potential contract risk in 2029–2031.\n\n**ON/SYNA Revised Merger & AMD–World Labs Deal** `Semiconductors` → ongoing\n\n- **ON** +6.01%, **SYNA** +14.08%: Amended from ~$7B all-stock to **$123/sh all-cash ($5.7B)**. Triggered by unsolicited competing "Party A" proposal deemed Superior Proposal.\n- ON financing: ~$3.9B cash + $2.45B committed term loan (Morgan Stanley); no financing condition.\n- FTC cleared; remaining non-US regulatory approvals + shareholder vote outstanding. Close target: mid-2027.\n- $200M annual run-rate synergies retained; ON says immediately EPS-accretive post-close.\n- Wells Fargo downgraded SYNA to Equal Weight, PT $140→$123 (deal price). Rosenblatt raised SYNA PT $115→$123, Neutral. Baird on ON: Hold, $108 PT.\n- **AMD** +2.95%: Acquired **World Labs** for ~$8.2B all-stock; Fei-Fei Li to join as EVP and Chief Scientist. AMD also has an OpenAI warrant for up to **160 million AMD shares** tied to deployment milestones — a separate dilution risk.\n\n**IT Services Post-ACN Earnings** `IT Services & Consulting` → new\n\n**ACN** −6.31% after surging ~16% Oct 1 on fiscal Q4 beat ($18.68B rev vs $18.03B est; adj. EPS $3.29 vs $3.18). Profit-taking drove Friday's reversal.\n\n- FY27 guide: 3–6% organic rev growth, GAAP EPS $14.39–$14.81; at least $9.5B returned to shareholders.\n- Q4 bookings $22.17B (1.2× B/B); managed services 1.4× B/B. Nearly 110K AI/data professionals.\n- ACN declared **4.9% dividend increase to $1.71/share**, payable Nov 13 to shareholders of record Oct 13.\n- ACN management: AI productivity gains are "deflationary" but offset by expanded scope and new work.\n- Truist PT $150→$220 (Hold); Morgan Stanley PT $175→$195 (EW); Stifel PT $225→$242 (Buy); Wells Fargo reaffirmed Reduce.\n- Peers sold off Friday: **CTSH** −3.89%, **EPAM** −5.30%, **GLOB** −5.08%, **INFY** −2.73%. No peer-specific downgrades identified; moves attributed to ACN read-through and profit-taking.\n- INFY Q2 FY27 results Oct 23 ⚠️; analysts may trim upper end of 1.5–3% rev growth guide to 2.5%.\n\n**Brazil Election — EM Risk-On** `Macro / Geopolitics` → new\n\nFlávio Bolsonaro led Brazil's first-round presidential vote 47.04% to Lula's 45.15% with 99.96% reporting. Neither crossed 50%; **runoff Oct 25**.\n\n- **ITUB** +13% PM, **BBD** +13% PM, **EWZ** +12% PM. PBR (Petrobras) +~10% PM. Nu Holdings +10.5%, Vale +8%.\n- Goldman Sachs' Alberto Ramos: higher probability of fiscal consolidation and structural reform; could stabilize credit rating.\n- SocGen: BRL to 5.10/USD by YE26, potentially <5.00 in H1 2027.\n- Sell-side consensus: 4–5% FX move, 7–8% equity rise, stronger central-bank rate-cut cycle under Bolsonaro win.\n- **Runoff Oct 25** ⚠️ — market reaction contingent on second-round outcome.\n\n**Qualcomm–Huawei Patent Deal & ARM Trial** `Wireless & Mobile · IP Licensing` → new\n\n- **QCOM** ~+2.9% PM: Huawei and Qualcomm announced multi-year, broad cross-license covering 5G, compute, AI, networking; QCOM purchasing certain Huawei U.S. patents. Royalty rate, total value undisclosed. Huawei says total patent-licensing agreements will exceed $6.9B. Subject to FTC/HSR review.\n- QCOM also licensed Huawei's **LogicFolding** chip-design technology (Bloomberg).\n- Separately, **ARM** +5.18% (Friday): **QCOM–ARM five-day Delaware jury trial began October 5**, Judge Maryellen Noreika presiding. QCOM alleges ARM withheld chip-testing tools and leaked termination threat. QCOM seeks to stop royalties potentially worth **billions of dollars for up to five years**; ARM agreement runs through 2033.\n\n**Tesla Q3 Delivery Beat + TSMC Terafab Talks** `EV / Auto` → new\n\n- **TSLA** +4.65%: Q3 deliveries 486,532 vs ~456K–461K est; production 464,391. Model 3/Y: 478,237. Energy storage 13.7 GWh vs 15.9 GWh est.\n- European demand offset softer US/China. Q3 deliveries ~2% below year-ago.\n- Musk confirmed exploratory talks between TSMC and SpaceX for proposed Texas "Terafab"; first phase ~$16.8B capex, target 2028 output.\n- Tesla Roadster reveal rescheduled Oct 15 ⚠️; Q3 financial results Oct 21 ⚠️.\n- **RIVN** produced 19,751 vehicles Q3, reaffirmed 65K–70K 2026 delivery guide; shares fell ~3% Friday despite in-line numbers.\n\n**HPE Networking Day & Helios Order** `AI Infrastructure` → new\n\n- **HPE** +7.36%: Received **$1.2B order from Vultr** for AMD Helios AI racks — first commercial Helios purchase. Each rack: 72 AMD MI455X GPUs + EPYC Venice CPUs + Juniper QFX5252 switches.\n- Cumulative FY26 AI-networking orders >$3B; FY27 networking rev growth raised to high-teens/low-20s% (from 14–17%); DC networking ~50% CAGR through FY29.\n- Juniper synergies target raised to **$800M** (from ≥$600M) by end-FY28.\n- Analyst actions: Citi PT $76→$92 (Buy); Daiwa upgrade Neutral→Outperform PT $65→$75; Truist PT $70→$75 (Buy); Wells Fargo PT $54→$63 (Hold); Susquehanna PT $40→$70 (Neutral); Barclays PT $79→$83 (OW); Morgan Stanley PT $67→$72 (OW); UBS PT $59→$66 (Neutral).\n\n**White House AI Accord & FTC Probe** `AI Policy` → new\n\nSix companies — **NVDA**, **META**, **GOOGL** (Google), Anthropic, OpenAI, xAI — signed voluntary "White House Accord on Super Intelligence" (Sep 29 signing, announced Oct 4). **MSFT attended but declined to sign.**\n\n- Commitments: internal controls, internal evaluation teams, independent external audits, board-level review. No federal enforcement mechanism, penalties, or compliance deadlines.\n- Trump created **Super Intelligence Force** led by DNI Jay Clayton; reports to Trump and Chief of Staff Wiles.\n- Treasury Sec Bessent: AI companies should self-police; US-China bilateral AI-notification system planned.\n- FTC opened industry-wide probe into **Anthropic, OpenAI, and METR** over AI-agent consumer harms; FTC plans **formal information demands and compelled executive testimony**.\n- No sell-side analyst quantified compliance costs or changed ratings for any signatory.\n\n**Geopolitics: Iran / Strait of Hormuz / Russia–China–India Sanctions / US–China Trade** `Macro / Commodities / Geopolitics` → ongoing\n\n- Iranian FM Araghchi proposed "7-day plan" to reopen Hormuz; Iran reviewing US response. Parliament speaker Qalibaf: Strait won't reopen until 7 conditions under Islamabad MoU are met.\n- Iranian crude production ~1.8M bpd (~half pre-war); no cargoes to China since mid-July.\n- OPEC+ (7 members) held Nov production targets unchanged; combined output ~25M bpd, ~5M bpd below pre-conflict.\n- G7 agreed to release **100M barrels** crude + diesel from emergency reserves; EU gasoil futures fell ~5% Oct 2.\n- Oil: Brent ~$101.59 (−0.7%) and WTI ~$90.05 (−1.2%) as of 02:34 ET Oct 5.\n- **Graham Act deadline Oct 18** ⚠️: Trump must decide on tariffs (up to 100%) on major Russian-energy buyers China/India. Energy Sec Wright: Trump "leaves every decision till the end." India's Jaishankar reaffirmed continued Russian purchases.\n- **US–China trade truce extended two months through January 10, 2027**; covers ~**€53B of goods each way** including US agricultural products and Chinese appliances/toys. US Commerce Dept slowing aircraft-part export licenses to China as leverage — affects **BA**, **GE**, **HON** via delayed approvals.\n- **XOM**: Supreme Court oral argument **October 5** in Exxon's appeal to block Boulder, Colorado climate-liability lawsuit; **Justice Alito recused**; decision expected by June 2027.\n\n**Credit-Bureau Disruption Risk** `Financials · Data & Analytics` → new\n\n- **FICO** −6.6%, **TRU** −2.4%, **EFX** −3.4% on Friday after Bloomberg reported FHFA may require only **two of three** credit bureaus for mortgage underwriting.\n- FHFA Director **Bill Pulte** could announce as soon as **October 12** ⚠️.\n- FICO most exposed given its dominant scoring-model position; structural demand reduction if one bureau is dropped per loan.\n\n**📡 Undercurrents** *(developing, not yet dominant)*\n\n- **MDB** CEO Chirantan Desai resigned to lead Meta's AI initiative; former CEO Dev Ittycheria returned as interim CEO. Shares −14.7% for the week. Stifel reiterated Buy, $465 PT.\n- **MRVL** Investor Day Oct 6 ⚠️: Piper Sandler forecasts FY31 rev $45B/EPS ~$19; Morgan Stanley raised PT $246→$268 (Hold). Google agreement could represent $120B XPU-attach opportunity.\n- BofA flagged AI-agent disruption risk for **ASANA, MNDY, BL, DBX, HUBS, INTU, PAYC, PCTY, ZM** from Meta's Muse and OpenAI's Dots.\n- **Micron** long-term customer commitments rose to $32B (from $22B in June); Taiwan union strike-authorization vote through Tuesday. D.A. Davidson PT $2,000→$2,100 (Buy). Samsung prelim Q3 Oct 8 ⚠️.\n- **SNPS** +15.1% 5D after OpenAI partnership (GPT-Synopsys) and Amazon custom-silicon IP deal; StoneX PT $570 (Buy).\n- **Netflix** hit new 52-week low $67.06 on Oct 2, ~half its price one year earlier.\n- **MAT** +14.9% on week: Authentic Brands Group approached re: takeover at >$20/sh (~$6B). [rumor]\n- Panmure Liberum analyst **Joachim Klement** forecasts S&P 500 at **5,000 by end-2027** (>35% below Oct 2 close of **7,722.72**), citing higher yields.\n- Fundstrat: equity valuations historically compress when 10Y exceeds ~5.5%; 10Y at 5.255% Monday morning.\n- Dallas Fed President **Lorie Logan** scheduled to speak **18:00 ET and 23:00 ET Monday Oct 5** ⚠️ — last week said rates may need to rise 50bp+; remarks could move rate expectations.\n- **CVE** agreed to acquire Athabasca Oil for **$12/sh** cash-and-stock (max $4.3B cash + max 44.4M CVE shares), **$5.7B EV**; close expected **December 2026**.\n\n---\n\n### 📌 Quick Hits\n\n- **AZTA** +12.38%: FQ3 non-GAAP EPS $0.16 vs $0.10 est; rev $161M vs $150M est; new interim CEO Dr. Madaus; Pomerantz investigating possible securities violations re: prior CEO departure.\n- **NNBR** +24.24%: $50M PIPE at $3.30/sh; proceeds to retire Series D pfd; CEO says next step is refinancing term loan; Q3 sales highest in 8 years.\n- **SDEV** +104.37%: Holds ~2.3B SKY tokens (~10% supply); co said Sep 29 no MNPI; PM Oct 5 +51.7% to ~$11.52.\n- **XRPN** +68.25%: Evernorth XRP-treasury combo approved Sep 30; expected close Oct 7, Nasdaq listing Oct 8; ~473M XRP at close.\n- **QMCO** +8.18%: FQ1 adj. EPS $0.18 vs −$0.17 est; rev $80.8M vs $75M est; new COO James Clancy (ex-Dell).\n- **WOLF** +13.19%: 200mm premium SiC substrate <0.01 micropipes/cm² launched; ~49.6% short interest.\n- **WNC** +12.68%: Rate-relief trade; backlog $956M (+14% QoQ); Q3 guide $440–460M rev; ex-div Oct 8.\n- **SILC** +11.93%: No identified catalyst; Portolan Capital bought 126K shares at $48.58 (Sep 23), now 5.06% holder.\n- **TBCH** +9.38%: Wall Street Zen upgrade Hold→Buy Oct 3; 4.2× avg vol.\n- **