2026-07-10	daily	benzinga	complete	### 🗓 July 10, 2026 — Pre-Market Brief\n\n**One-liner**: Memory/storage stocks surge (SNDK +13%, MU +6%, STX +8%) on Meta's leaked $145B AI infrastructure memo and Micron's $250B US fab commitment, SK Hynix debuts on Nasdaq today, while Delta beats on Q2 earnings and the US signals continued "technical talks" with Iran.\n\n---\n\n### ⚡ Top Movers At a Glance\n\n| Ticker | Move | Cap | Catalyst | Theme |\n|--------|------|-----|----------|-------|\n| **SNDK** | +13.4% | Mega | Meta leaked memo shows NAND flash purchase from SanDisk | Memory / AI Infra |\n| **ARM** | +11.1% | Mega | Broad semi rebound; dip-buying after pullback | AI Compute |\n| **STX** | +7.9% | Mega | Wells Fargo upgrade to OW; PT raised $900→$1100 | Memory / Storage |\n| **WDC** | +7.8% | Mega | Micron $250B US invest + Meta infra expansion | Memory / Storage |\n| **MU** | +6.0% | Mega | $250B US fab plan; first concrete NY megafab | Memory / AI Infra |\n| **GLW** | +5.7% | Mega | Fiber optic/AI data center demand tailwind | Optical / AI Infra |\n| **AMD** | +5.3% | Mega | ARK says chips "more performant per dollar" vs NVDA; GS PT $640 | AI Compute |\n| **LRCX** | +5.3% | Mega | Mizuho PT $380→$400; TD Cowen PT $340→$400; Needham PT $300→$390 | Semi Equipment |\n| **PANW** | +5.2% | Mega | Broad tech rebound; BTIG PT $150→$186 | Cybersecurity |\n| **MRVL** | +5.2% | Mega | RBC maintains OP w/ $360 PT; data center revenue >50% growth | AI Compute |\n| **LITE** | +13.6% | Large | Meta 14GW compute buildout drives fiber optic demand | Optical / AI Infra |\n| **CBRS** | +10.7% | Large | 200MW European AI data center expansion by end-2027 | AI Compute |\n| **HPE** | +10.2% | Large | 5D +19.4%; AI server demand momentum | AI Infra / Servers |\n| **NOK** | +9.9% | Large | NestAI defense partnership for AI-enabled 5G ops | Defense Tech |\n| **RIVN** | +8.3% | Large | Recovery from $1.2B offering; reclaims major MAs | EV / Idiosyncratic |\n| **FLEX** | +7.2% | Large | Cerebras CS-3 manufacturing partnership expansion | Contract Mfg / AI |\n| **STRL** | +7.0% | Large | Infrastructure/construction cycle momentum | Infrastructure |\n| **FTAI** | +6.9% | Large | Rebound from 5D -7.4% selloff | Aviation / Idiosyncratic |\n| **JLHL** | +219.2% | Micro | No identifiable catalyst; 107x avg volume | Idiosyncratic |\n| **LASR** | +27.5% | Mid | $44M JLWS laser weapon contract; ceiling $627M | Defense Tech |\n| **BTDR** | +16.8% | Mid | Breaks ground $36M Nevada SEALMINER factory | Crypto Mining / Infra |\n| **TXG** | +15.7% | Mid | Morgan Stanley PT $22→$37; whale call options activity | Life Sciences |\n| **AEHR** | +14.0% | Mid | Follow-on FOX-XP order from silicon photonics customer ⚠️ | Semi Equipment |\n| **ALGM** | +13.3% | Mid | Broad semi rebound | Semiconductors |\n| **MXL** | +13.1% | Mid | Broad semi rebound | Semiconductors |\n| **FLNC** | +11.2% | Mid | Energy storage rebound | Energy Storage |\n| **MARA** | +11.0% | Mid | Acquires 1,200-acre TX site; up to 2GW by Apr 2028 | Crypto / Digital Infra |\n\n---\n\n### 🧭 Narrative Threads\n\n**AI Memory & Storage Supercycle — Meta Capex Leak, Micron $250B, SK Hynix Debut** `Memory / AI Infra` → ongoing\n\nThe session's dominant thread. A leaked Meta internal memo (Reuters) revealed the company is purchasing NAND flash from **SNDK**, DRAM from Samsung, and fiber optics from Sumitomo, while planning to scale compute infrastructure from 7GW this year to 14GW in 2027. Meta's in-house "Iris" AI chip starts manufacturing in September (developed with Broadcom, fabricated by TSM). Separately, **MU** CEO Sanjay Mehrotra announced the company is increasing US manufacturing investment to >$250B through 2035, targeting 40% of DRAM produced domestically and 90,000+ jobs. First concrete poured at the NY megafab ahead of schedule. MU also announced up to $3B to strengthen US semiconductor supply chain, including $500M financing for GlobalWafers' Texas silicon wafer facility.\n\n- **SNDK** +13.4% — directly named as Meta's NAND flash supplier in the leaked memo; revenue growth 251% YoY; D/E ratio 0.01; P/E 63.5x.\n- **MU** +6.0% — $250B US investment plan; $3B supply chain investment; forward P/E ~6.1x per Benzinga analysis; revenue growth 345.7% YoY.\n- **STX** +7.9% — Wells Fargo upgraded to Overweight, PT $900→$1100; Susquehanna PT raised to $775.\n- **WDC** +7.8% — sympathy with MU/Meta infrastructure announcements.\n- **SK Hynix** (SKHY) begins Nasdaq trading today; offering 7x oversubscribed; ~$28B raise; Jim Cramer says stock worth buying but "start small"; trades at ~7x expected earnings.\n- Commerce Secretary Lutnick told Samsung and SK Hynix they have "no choice" but to build US AI memory fabs; Samsung + SK Hynix plan $880B collectively for AI memory expansion.\n- Semiconductor ETFs drew record $7.1B inflows on Tuesday (SOXX alone $5.4B, largest single-day since 2001 launch). SOXL drew $1.2B.\n- Citi Wealth warned Chinese domestic memory chips are "gaining international recognition," posing margin overhang risk for SNDK, MU, WDC.\n- **AMAT** +2.2% — CEO Dickerson told Nikkei Asia company has "tremendous visibility" into 24-month demand; Mizuho PT $540→$650; TD Cowen PT $525→$700; Needham PT $530→$740.\n- **LRCX** +5.3% — Mizuho PT $380→$400; TD Cowen PT $340→$400; Needham PT $300→$390. Earnings July 29 ⚠️.\n- Next catalyst: SK Hynix first trading day today; TSMC earnings July 16 ⚠️; AEHR earnings July 14 ⚠️.\n\n---\n\n**Broad Semiconductor Rebound — Dip-Buying After $1T Nvidia Slide** `AI Compute` → ongoing\n\nThe semiconductor sector staged a sharp bounce after a multi-day selloff that erased ~$1T from NVDA alone since its May 14 peak. The VXN/VIX ratio hit 1.7, a 23-year high, indicating extreme relative tech volatility. ARK Invest published research saying **AMD** chips "can already be more performant per dollar" than Nvidia on select workloads; AMD server CPU revenue share reached 46% in Q1 2026 (from near-zero in 2017). AMD's Helios rack-scale solution arriving H2 2026 with commitments from OpenAI, Meta, Oracle.\n\n- **ARM** +11.1% — broad rebound; 84.3% above 200-day SMA; earnings July 29 ⚠️; TD Cowen PT $475, UBS PT $470, BofA PT $460.\n- **AMD** +5.3% — GS reiterated Buy, PT $640; Wells Fargo PT $615; Cantor Fitzgerald PT $700; YTD +155%; P/E 172.5x. Q2 earnings Aug 4 ⚠️.\n- **MRVL** +5.2% — RBC maintains OP, PT $360; data center revenue expected >50% growth this year and next. Earnings Aug 27 ⚠️.\n- **AEHR** +14.0% — follow-on FOX-XP order from lead silicon photonics customer for 300mm wafer burn-in system. Earnings July 14 ⚠️.\n- **ALGM** +13.3%, **MXL** +13.1%, **TSEM** +6.7%, **ASX** +8.2% — broad semi sympathy.\n- **CBRS** +10.7% — Cerebras announces 200MW European AI data center capacity by end-2027; expanded Flex manufacturing partnership to ramp CS-3 production ~7x through 2026.\n\n---\n\n**AI Infrastructure Optical & Networking** `Optical / Networking` → ongoing\n\n**LITE** +13.6% led the optical connectivity cohort as Meta's 14GW compute buildout directly implies massive fiber optic demand. **GLW** +5.7%, **CIEN** +6.1% (+11.5% 5D), **NOK** +9.9% all participated. Nokia announced a defense partnership with NestAI integrating AI, deployable 5G, and sensing for NATO operations.\n\n- **LASR** +27.5% — nLIGHT secured $44M initial Joint Laser Weapon System OTA contract; total program ceiling up to $627M; Craig-Hallum PT raised $80→$100; Needham PT $80→$90.\n\n---\n\n**Delta Air Lines Q2 Beat; Aircraft Tariffs Shelved** `Airlines / Macro` → new\n\n**DAL** reported Q2 adj. EPS $1.56 vs $1.47 est (+6.1% beat); revenue $19.76B vs $17.53B est (+12.7% beat). Revenue up 18.7% YoY, though EPS down 25.7% YoY from $2.10. Guided Q3 adj. EPS $2.00–$2.50 vs $1.99 est. Affirmed FY26 adj. EPS $6.50–$7.50 vs $5.93 est. Separately, Commerce Dept completed Section 232 aircraft investigation, recommending against immediate tariffs but giving trade partners a 6-month negotiation window.\n\n---\n\n**Circle OCC Approval / Crypto Infrastructure** `Crypto / Fintech` → new\n\n**Circle** (CRCL) received OCC approval to establish First National Digital Currency Bank N.A. ("Circle National Trust") — the first federally chartered national trust bank for digital asset custody. Will offer fiduciary digital asset custody for Circle and affiliates; may extend to institutional customers; designed to enable future USDC Reserve management under federal oversight. **COIN** trading higher in sympathy (+4.5% premarket). Bitcoin consolidating between $60K–$70K for 307 days, the third-longest $10K-range consolidation in BTC history.\n\n- **MARA** +11.0% — acquired 1,200-acre Texas site from HIF USA; up to 2GW power capacity by April 2028; would more than double MARA's portfolio to ~4.8GW.\n- **BTDR** +16.8% — broke ground on $36M, 187K sq ft Nevada SEALMINER manufacturing facility; 10,000 units/month target; 70 local jobs.\n- **HOOD** — Robinhood launched Robinhood Chain (L2 blockchain) mainnet; CEO Tenev says "great for memes too." Barclays raised PT $82→$122.\n\n---\n\n**Analyst Upgrades Across Software & E-Commerce** `SaaS / Software` → new\n\nStifel upgraded **TWLO** Hold→Buy, PT $175→$260; upgraded **SHOP** Hold→Buy, PT $110→$150. BTIG raised **FTNT** PT $150→$186. BTIG raised **NET** PT $269→$314. Needham raised **MDB** PT $400→$430. Baird lowered **CTSH** PT $72→$55.\n\n---\n\n**Consumer Staples Under Pressure** `Consumer / Macro` → ongoing\n\n**PEP** Q2 revenue $24.18B (+6.4% YoY) beat $23.96B est, but core EPS $2.20 missed $2.21. Citigroup downgraded PEP Buy→Neutral, PT $170→$145. Management cited high gas prices pressuring convenience store impulse purchases. Reaffirmed FY26 organic revenue growth 2–4%, adj. EPS $8.55–$8.71. Consumer staples ETFs (XLP, VDC) face headwinds from 30Y Treasury yields >5%, July 24 tariff cliff (10% Section 122 tariffs expiring, replacement surcharges up to 15% possible in August), and June NFP of just 57K.\n\n---\n\n**📡 Undercurrents** *(developing, not yet dominant)*\n- EU Commission issued preliminary finding that Instagram and Facebook's addictive design (infinite scroll, autoplay, push notifications) breaches Digital Services Act; **META** faces potential fine up to 6% of global annual turnover.\n- Fed Chair Warsh created five external task forces including an AI economic impact panel led by Marc Andreessen, Xbox CEO Asha Sharma, and Stanford's Charles Jones; Warsh has argued AI could support lower interest rates.\n- Morgan Stanley's chief US economist said AI productivity boom could keep interest rates above post-2008 levels if technology is labor-augmenting rather than labor-replacing.\n- CME FedWatch: July hike odds 24.1%, September hike odds 58.9%; 30-year Treasury yields pushing above 5%.\n- CCC Intelligent Solutions (CCC +13% overnight) reportedly exploring sale; hired Morgan Stanley to advise; market cap ~$3.3B.\n- Berkshire Hathaway cash pile reached record $397B — enough to buy 474 of 500 S&P companies outright.\n\n---\n\n### 📰 Channel Pulse\n\n| Channel | Material fact |\n|---------|--------------|\n| Earnings | **DAL** Q2 adj. EPS $1.56 vs $1.47 est; revenue $19.76B vs $17.53B est; affirmed FY26 adj. EPS $6.50–$7.50 |\n| Earnings | **WDFC** Q3 EPS $2.33 vs $1.56 est; sales $195.1M vs $172.8M est (+24% YoY); raised FY26 guidance; +15% after-hours |\n| Macro | US initial jobless claims 215K vs 218K est; US engaging in "technical talks" with Iran per MS Now report |\n| Tech | Meta leaked memo: Iris AI chip manufacturing starts September; 7GW compute this year, 14GW next year; purchasing NAND from SanDisk, DRAM from Samsung |\n| Tech | **MU** increases US investment to >$250B through 2035; first concrete at NY megafab |\n| IPO | SK Hynix ADR debut today (SKHY); ~$28B offering 7x oversubscribed; Jim Cramer says buy but start small |\n| Defense | **LASR** wins $44M JLWS laser weapon contract; program ceiling up to $627M |\n| Regulation | Circle received OCC approval for national digital currency trust bank |\n| Regulation | EU Commission preliminary finding: Meta's Instagram/Facebook addictive design breaches DSA |\n\n---\n\n### 👁 On The Radar\n\n- **SK Hynix (SKHY)** Nasdaq debut today July 10 — first trade will set tone for memory sector and broader semis.\n- **TSMC (TSM)** Q2 earnings July 16 — consensus EPS $3.77 vs $2.47 YoY, revenue $39.76B vs $30.07B. ⚠️\n- **AEHR** earnings July 14 — EPS est. loss of $0.02, revenue $18.69M. ⚠️\n- **Bank earnings** July 14–15: JPM, WFC, C, GS, BAC; whisper numbers running above consensus per Arora Report.\n- **ARM** Q1 FY2027 earnings July 29 — EPS est. $0.36, revenue $1.27B. ⚠️\n- **LRCX** Q4 earnings July 29 — EPS est. $1.68, revenue $6.66B. ⚠️\n- Section 122 tariffs (10% global) expire July 24; replacement surcharges up to 15% possible in August per NRF data.\n- Anthropic IPO confidentially filed (Reuters, June 1); timing and terms undisclosed.\n\n---\n\n### 🚫 What to Ignore Today\n\n- **JLHL** +219%: No identifiable catalyst; micro-cap ($261M), 107x avg volume, repeated halt history — pure speculative noise.\n- **SVC** +408% 5D: Prior-week restructuring move already absorbed; 0.2x avg volume on today's +0.3% — stale story.\n- Automated Benzinga industry comparison articles for MSFT, NVDA, MU, SNDK, AT&T — no new information, boilerplate financial ratio summaries.	\N	{"calls": [{"step": "step4_synthesis", "model": "claude-opus-4-6", "api_model": "claude-opus-4-6", "input_tokens": 234834, "output_tokens": 4533, "pricing_model": "claude-opus-4-6", "input_cost_usd": 1.17417, "total_cost_usd": 1.287495, "output_cost_usd": 0.113325, "cache_read_cost_usd": 0.0, "cache_write_cost_usd": 0.0, "cache_read_input_tokens": 0, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 0}], "call_count": 1, "started_at": "2026-07-10T11:51:09.451538+00:00", "completed_at": "2026-07-10T11:53:16.113978+00:00", "current_step": null, "pricing_note": "Token counts from Anthropic Messages API response.usage. USD estimated from published claude.com/api pricing (input, 5m cache write,