ructure |\n| TRI | +3.2% | Large | No identifiable company-specific catalyst | SaaS |\n| E | +3.1% | Large | WTI rebound; crude sector bid | Energy |\n| TEAM | +3.0% | Large | Pulled forward US federal security auth submission by 6 months; air-gapped deployment announced | SaaS |\n| VEEV | +3.0% | Large | Another top-20 biopharma selected Vault CRM; Q last EPS $2.35 vs $2.23 est | SaaS |\n| ILMN | +2.9% | Large | UBS upgraded Buy, PT $135→$260 (Sept 9); Q2 EPS $1.31 vs $1.23 est | Healthcare / Genomics |\n| DVN | +2.8% | Large | Toms Capital (top-5 holder) urged strategic alternatives incl. sale; oil bid | Energy |\n| CDNS | +2.7% | Large | No identifiable company-specific catalyst | Chip Equipment |\n| NNBR | +14.0% | Small | Raised 2026 net-sales guide $460-480M→$470-490M; Q2 rev $128.7M vs $116.1M est | Specialty Materials |\n| FSLY | +12.6% | Small | D.A. Davidson PT $21→$23, Neutral; prior Q adj. EPS $0.15 vs $0.07 est | EdgeAI / CDN |\n| MEI | +11.5% | Small | No identifiable company-specific catalyst | Analog |\n| SECZ | +10.9% | Small | 52-week high; bullish analyst actions and unspecified regulatory development cited | Fintech / Digital Assets |\n| ACMR | +7.6% | Small | No identifiable company-specific catalyst; 3.8× volume | Chip Equipment |\n| NUAI | +6.6% | Small | Neocloud theme momentum; +60% month-to-date; no specific Sept 23 catalyst | AI Infrastructure |\n| BHF | +6.3% | Small | No identifiable company-specific catalyst | Financials |\n| CBRL | +6.2% | Small | FQ4 adj. EPS $0.99 vs $0.74 YoY; revenue $849.3M vs $834.6M est; margins beat | Consumer / Restaurants |\n| VICR | +6.1% | Small | Q3 sequential rev-growth guide raised >20% from ~10%; licensing royalties cited | Power / AI Infra |\n| VIA | +5.8% | Small | No identifiable company-specific catalyst | SaaS |\n| IONQ | +5.2% | Small | First real-time quantum error-correction decoder on off-shelf CPU announced; NVDA install 2027 | Quantum Computing |\n| QMCO | +5.1% | Small | FQ1 2027 adj. EPS $0.18 vs -$0.24 est; rev $80.8M vs $71.5M est; first positive non-GAAP NI since 2023 | AI Infrastructure |\n| REAX | -9.2% | Small | No identifiable company-specific catalyst | Internet & Platforms |\n| SDGR | -6.2% | Small | No new catalyst identified; Tectora deal valuation discussion only | SaaS |\n| CHPT | -6.7% | Small | No new Sept 23 catalyst; prior Q rev $116M vs $105M est already reported; CFO/GC RSU-related share sales | EV Infrastructure |\n| FWDI | -5.5% | Small | No identifiable company-specific catalyst | Specialty Materials |\n| SRAD | -4.6% | Small | No identifiable company-specific catalyst | SaaS |\n| CRCL | -3.6% | Large | Crypto risk-off; insider sales $40.3M; Q rev $701M, EPS $0.18 vs $0.26 est | Fintech / Digital Assets |\n| MSTR | -2.8% | Large | Bitcoin -1.98% to $84,414; ASST acquired 1,355 BTC vs MSTR's 950 BTC, overshadowed | Crypto Treasury |\n\n---\n\n### 🧭 Narrative Threads\n\n**Rate Shock Dominates the Tape** `Macro` → ongoing\n\nThe day's dominant macro event: the September Flash Composite PMI printed 58.4 (vs. 56.0 prior), a 5-year high, with Services PMI at 58.7 and Manufacturing PMI at 57.0. The strong data drove the 10-year Treasury yield up 13.89 bps to 5.106% — its highest close since 2007 and largest single-day rise since April 2025, touching 5.135% intraday — while the 2-year rose 11.4 bps to 4.891%, hitting its highest since May 2024. Fed rate-hike expectations for October increased on the data. SPY -0.73%, QQQ -0.83%, SOXX -1.23%, Russell 2000 -1.77%. IGV +1.26% was the notable outlier — enterprise software bucked the bond selloff.\n\n- 10-year: 5.106% close; intraday high 5.135%; last at these levels 2007\n- 2-year: 4.891%; intraday high 4.947%; highest since May 2024\n- Flash Composite PMI: 58.4 vs. 56.0 prior (highest since July 2021)\n- Richmond Fed Manufacturing: -2 vs. +4 prior (divergence within the data)\n- WTI: $92.16/bbl +1.8%; Brent: $103.08/bbl +~3.9%; energy the only S&P sector to advance\n- Gold: conflicting reports ($4,287–$4,322); Bitcoin -1.98% to $84,414\n- 5-year Treasury auction scheduled; results not yet in gathered data ⚠️\n- Fed's Barr spoke at 10:05 a.m. on housing/economic outlook; specifics not captured in sources\n\n---\n\n**Cybersecurity Sector Decouples from QQQ Selloff** `SaaS / Cybersecurity` → ongoing\n\n**CRWD** +4.8%, **PANW** +4.3%, **OKTA** +4.2%, **FTNT** +2.7% all advanced while QQQ fell -0.83%. The First Trust Nasdaq Cybersecurity ETF gained 1% on the session. Coverage attributed the move to a sector-wide bid rather than any single company catalyst, though multiple analyst actions provided support.\n\n- Wedbush initiated **CRWD** Outperform, citing 25% YoY ARR growth and 51% increase in net-new ARR; latest reported revenue $1.47B, net-new ARR $333M, Falcon Flex ARR $2.29B\n- BofA raised **CRWD** PT to $260, citing AI-related attack surfaces as growth catalyst\n- Scotiabank raised **OKTA** PT $165→$190, maintained Sector Outperform; consensus PT $177.61, consensus Moderate Buy\n- **PANW** announced new AI defense service pre-session; next earnings November 12, 2026 ⚠️\n- **PANW** and **PLTR** flagged as technical buy signals by IBD post-session\n- Josh Brown cited **FTNT** range $150–$175, 50-DMA ~$160, 200-DMA ~$114 as attractive entry zone\n- IGV +1.26% confirms the enterprise software / security bid was broad; **TEAM** +3.0%, **VEEV** +3.0%, **CRM** +2.4%, **NOW** +2.1%, **ZS** +2.0%, **RBRK** +2.1% all participated\n\n---\n\n**AI-Enterprise Software Catches a Bid Despite Rate Pressure** `Software & SaaS` → ongoing\n\nIGV +1.26% vs. XLK -0.47% is the divergence. Enterprise SaaS names moved as a group, driven in part by specific catalysts at **TEAM** and **VEEV**, a Morgan Stanley call on **TWLO**, and the broader Meta Connect event driving AI-agent demand expectations.\n\n- **TEAM** +3.0%: pulled forward US federal higher security authorization submission by 6 months; committed to fully air-gapped deployment for government customers — specific, datable government-market catalyst\n- **VEEV** +3.0%: another top-20 biopharma selected Vault CRM; last reported EPS $2.35 vs $2.23 est, revenue $927.96M +17.6% YoY; Andra AP-fonden sold 22.4% of its position (18,540 shares) in Q2\n- **TWLO** +1.75%: Rosenblatt raised PT $275→$290, Buy; Morgan Stanley identified TWLO as best-exposed comms-software name if Meta's Muse increases API volumes; FY2025 revenue $5.07B +13.7% YoY, FCF $997M\n- **PLTR** +3.9%: AI-adjacent enterprise software bid; no single company catalyst, but IBD technical buy signal flagged\n- Stifel upgraded **MSFT** Hold→Buy, PT $575 (not in tape universe but lifted sector sentiment)\n- UBS initiated **CRWV** (CoreWeave) Buy, PT $120 — neocloud theme active\n\n---\n\n**Meta Connect & the Muse AI Agent Distribution Effect** `Internet & Platforms` → new\n\nMeta Connect event held September 23 with Zuckerberg keynote at 7:00 p.m. ET. **META** +1.21% on the session. The Muse AI agent — introduced September 8 — had 902,000+ downloads in 6 days per Bloomberg. Two commerce/travel partnerships announced:\n\n- **SHOP** partnership: Muse agents can complete checkouts across Shopify's platform — rumored pre-market as a catalyst for SHOP; however **SHOP** closed -3.47% (tape authoritative), suggesting the partnership was either pre-priced in the 5D +11.1% run or outweighed by rate sensitivity\n- **Expedia** partnership: Muse can access Expedia to plan trips; EXPD -4.0% on session (not in tape universe)\n- Morgan Stanley: **TWLO** and Wix best-exposed to Muse-driven API/communications volume increase\n- **META** +1.21% vs. QQQ -0.83% — held up on AI-agent excitement despite rate pressure\n- Meta Connect outcomes (product announcements, hardware reveals) from 7 p.m. ET keynote not yet captured ⚠️\n\n---\n\n**Energy Sector: Sole Green Zone; Diesel Export Ban Threat Pressures Refiners** `Energy` → new\n\nEnergy was the only S&P sector to advance. WTI +1.8% to $92.16, Brent +~3.9% to $103.08 after 5 consecutive down sessions. Iran's president stated Tehran would not surrender to US pressure, cited alongside higher oil prices. **BP** +3.1%, **EQNR** +2.67%, **E** +3.12%, **DVN** +2.82% all gained on the crude rebound. Refiners diverged:\n\n- **VLO** -0.31%, **MPC** -0.72%, **PSX** -0.91%: all cited in connection with White House reports of a potential 90-day diesel-export ban; no decision confirmed\n- Jefferies rated VLO Hold, PT $401 (Sept 21); UBS rated MPC Buy, PT $450 (Sept 21) — both outside window\n- **DVN** +2.82%: Toms Capital (top-5 shareholder) urged strategic alternatives including potential sale in a letter sent earlier in September; oil bid amplified the move\n- EIA gasoline stocks and crude-import data on calendar; prior: gasoline +0.794Mb, crude imports -1.18Mb\n\n---\n\n**IonQ Quantum Breakthrough + NVIDIA Partnership** `Quantum Computing` → new\n\n**IONQ** +5.2%, 4.5× average volume. Announced September 22 after close:\n\n- First end-to-end, real-time quantum error-correction decoder running on a single off-the-shelf CPU, validated across hundreds of logical qubits and millions of logical operations\n- Superion 256 quantum computer to be installed at Nvidia's Accelerated Quantum Research Center in 2027\n- Multi-year strategic partnership with South Korea's SDT for Superion 256 delivery\n- Rigetti Computing and D-Wave Quantum each rose >5.5% in premarket on September 23 (not in tape universe)\n- 5D return +16.6%, suggesting event was partially anticipated; single-day +5.2% on confirmation\n\n---\n\n**Quantum Corporation Earnings Beat — First Profitable Quarter Since 2023** `AI Infrastructure` → new\n\n**QMCO** +5.1%, 3.8× average volume.\n\n- FQ1 2027: adj. EPS $0.18 vs. consensus -$0.24 (beat by $0.42); revenue $80.8M vs. $71.5M est\n- First positive non-GAAP adjusted net income since 2023\n- 52-week high hit intraday at $27.82\n- Chief Accounting Officer Laura A. Nash stepped down effective September 1; remains in transition role through end of 2026; no disagreements reported\n\n---\n\n**Vicor Power — Q3 Revenue Upgrade Signals AI Infra Power Demand** `Power & Wide-Bandgap` → new\n\n**VICR** +6.1%, 2.2× volume, 5D +55.3%.\n\n- Q3 2026 sequential revenue-growth guide raised from ~10% to >20%\n- Licensing royalties cited as component of the revision; dollar amount not disclosed\n- No new analyst actions identified for the September 23 window\n- Move extends a 55.3% 5-day run, suggesting sequential upgrades are compounding\n\n---\n\n**Cracker Barrel Earnings Beat Drives Consumer Upside** `Consumer Discretionary` → new\n\n**CBRL** +6.2%, 3.4× volume.\n\n- FQ4 (ended July 31, 2026): adj. EPS $0.99 vs. $0.74 prior year; revenue $849.3M vs. $834.6M FactSet consensus\n- Revenue -2.2% YoY but above estimate; restaurant-level margins, COGS, and adj. EBITDA margins each beat\n- GAAP EPS $0.54 ($12.2M net income)\n- No updated FY2027 guidance captured in available sources\n\n---\n\n**NN Inc. — Industrial Guidance Raise** `Specialty Materials` → new\n\n**NNBR** +14.0%, 10.3× average volume — highest volume multiple in the universe.\n\n- 2026 net-sales guide raised to $470-490M from $460-480M\n- 2026 adj. EBITDA guide raised to $58-68M from $55-65M\n- Q2 revenue $128.7M vs. $116.1M est (+19.3% YoY); adj. EPS $0.11 vs. $0.01 est; adj. EBITDA $17.94M (13.9% margin)\n- Presented at D.A. Davidson 25th Annual Diversified Industrials & Services Conference September 23\n\n---\n\n**Crypto Treasury / ASST vs. MSTR Divergence** `Digital Assets` → ongoing\n\n- **ASST** -1.59% despite acquiring 1,355 BTC for ~$107.7M at avg $79,475/BTC — larger than **MSTR**'s reported 950 BTC for ~$75.7M\n- **MSTR** -2.81%; Zacks upgraded Strong Sell→Hold September 21; Barclays raised PT $125→$160 Overweight September 15 (outside window)\n- Bitcoin -1.98% to $84,414; CRCL -3.56% (crypto-adjacent, EPS miss $0.18 vs $0.26 est, CEO sold 56,200 shares)\n- CME announced Bitcoin Cash and Uniswap futures launching October 19, 2026\n- Spot Bitcoin ETFs net positive for 2026 YTD after ~$999M inflows on September 21 (reported September 23); BlackRock IBIT led with $381.4M; Ethereum ETFs +$270M same session\n\n---\n\n**Semiconductor Equipment — ACMR Spike, SOX Weakness** `Chip Equipment` → ongoing\n\nSOXX -1.23%, SMH -1.00% — semis underperformed broader market. Within the universe: **ACMR** +7.57% (3.8× volume, no identified catalyst), **AMD** -0.90%, **ALAB** -0.88%, **INTC** -0.49%, **SMCI** +0.17%.\n\n- **SMCI**: began shipping NVIDIA Vera Rubin NVL72 racks; >$60B new orders in FQ4; FY2027 revenue guide $65-72B; FY2026 net sales $39.1B vs $22.0B prior year; capacity expansion to >6,000 racks/month\n- **ACMR**: 3.8× volume with no identifiable catalyst — flag for follow-through\n- **AMD**: 5D +20.9% (from prior AI chip demand expectations); -0.9% on Sept 23; earnings scheduled after 4:00 p.m. ET **today** — consensus EPS $1.08, revenue $22.3B ⚠️\n- **ARM**: +0.76% after 5D +37.5% surge post-prior event; next earnings November 4, 2026 ⚠️\n- Citi raised **MU** PT $1,150→$1,300; Wells Fargo lowered **MU** PT $1,525→$1,400 ahead of MU earnings (next Wednesday) ⚠️\n- AVGO Q4 guidance described as 12% above consensus in a September 23 report\n\n---\n\n**Ingersoll Rand — Data Center Cooling Read-Through** `AI Infrastructure` → ongoing\n\n**IR** +3.4%, 2.2× volume.\n\n- Q2 2026 revenue ~$2.05B (+8.5% YoY); adj. EPS $0.86 vs ~$0.80 est\n- September 17 investor conference: July organic orders high-single-digit growth in Americas; liquidity ~$3.8B\n- Worthington Enterprises (not in tape universe) separately: CEO identified ASME tanks for liquid cooling of AI data centers as a demand opportunity; WOR Q1 adj. EPS $0.82, sales +13%, $4M tariff refund\n\n---\n\n**Fastly — Magnitude of Move vs. Catalyst Gap** `EdgeAI / CDN` → ongoing\n\n**FSLY** +12.57%, 5.2× volume — second-largest 1D gain in the mid/small universe.\n\n- D.A. Davidson raised PT $21→$23, maintained **Neutral** — a small PT raise on a below-consensus rating drove a 12.6% session move\n-