2026-06-06	losers	benzinga	complete	### 🗓 2026-06-06 — R1D Losers Brief\n\n**One-liner**: AI‑linked semiconductor and technology names carnaged after Broadcom’s unchanged AI outlook collided with a hot May jobs report that rekindled rate‑hike fears, while space stocks sank on an S&P index‑eligibility blow.\n\n---\n\n### 📉 Losers At a Glance\n\n| Ticker | 1D | Cap | Sector | Driver type | Catalyst (≤12 words) |\n|--------|---:|-----|--------|-------------|----------------------|\n| **MU** | -14.2% | Mega | Technology | Sector | Broadcom’s flat AI guide + rate‑hike fears from May jobs |\n| **MRVL** | -13.2% | Mega | Technology | Sector | Profit‑taking after AI‑run; Broadcom AI outlook disappointment |\n| **ARM** | -12.9% | Mega | Technology | Sector | Broadcom‑triggered semiconductor profit‑taking |\n| **SNDK** | -12.5% | Mega | Technology | Sector | Broadcom AI reset drags memory/storage stocks |\n| **GLW** | -11.5% | Mega | Technology | Sector | Sympathy selloff with tech/AI hardware peers |\n| **WDC** | -11.1% | Mega | Technology | Sector | Memory‑chip weakness following Broadcom’s guidance |\n| **QCOM** | -11.0% | Mega | Technology | Sector | Broadcom‑led semi rout; JPMorgan flags diversification ahead of investor day |\n| **AMD** | -10.9% | Mega | Technology | Sector | Broadcom sympathy; profit‑taking in AI GPU names |\n| **SCCO** | -10.7% | Mega | Basic Materials | Unknown | No material ticker‑specific news; likely commodity/risk‑off move |\n| **INTC** | -10.7% | Mega | Technology | Sector | Broadcom hangover; profit‑taking after 459% 12‑month run |\n| **ORCL** | -10.7% | Mega | Technology | Sector | AI capex concerns; pre‑earnings profit‑taking |\n| **LRCX** | -10.2% | Mega | Technology | Sector | WFE stocks slammed in broad semiconductor selloff |\n| **AMAT** | -9.7% | Mega | Technology | Sector | Chip‑equipment names crushed alongside Broadcom spill |\n| **KLAC** | -9.2% | Mega | Technology | Sector | Sympathy with semiconductor equipment peers |\n| **STX** | -9.0% | Mega | Technology | Sector | Storage/HDD sector caught in AI‑hardware unwind |\n| **ASTS** | -14.5% | Large | Technology | Sector | S&P kills SpaceX fast‑entry hopes; space stocks crumble |\n| **NBIS** | -13.9% | Large | Communication Services | Mixed | AI infrastructure cooldown + NVIDIA insider sells $221M |\n| **NOK** | -13.6% | Large | Technology | Idiosyncratic | Profit‑taking after 208% Y/Y rally; €500M bond sale |\n| **FSLR** | -13.6% | Large | Energy | Sector | Clean‑energy rout on tax‑credit phaseout fears + yield spike |\n| **IONQ** | -13.2% | Large | Technology | Idiosyncratic | Risk‑off in high‑beta quantum; Quantinuum IPO fades |\n| **FN** | -12.8% | Large | Technology | Unknown | No material ticker‑specific news; likely sympathy with tech selloff |\n| **STRL** | -12.6% | Large | Industrials | Unknown | No material ticker‑specific news; possible profit‑taking |\n| **CRCL** | -12.0% | Large | Financial Services | Idiosyncratic | Mizuho cuts PT to $85 amid crypto‑linked weakness |\n| **SMCI** | -12.0% | Large | Technology | Sector | AI server maker hit by Broadcom‑driven hardware rout |\n| **BE** | -11.7% | Large | Industrials | Idiosyncratic | Tariff‑relief optimism fades; risk‑off rotation |\n| **PL** | -26.2% | Mid & small | Industrials | Mixed | $1.5B ATM offering dilutes; space sector sympathy fallout |\n| **ZUMZ** | -25.9% | Mid & small | Consumer Cyclical | Idiosyncratic | Q1 EPS miss, weak Q2 guidance; consumer pressures |\n| **POET** | -22.0% | Mid & small | Technology | Unknown | No material ticker‑specific news; likely sympathy with tech rout |\n| **TE** | -21.5% | Mid & small | Industrials | Unknown | No material ticker‑specific news |\n| **BRUN** | -21.3% | Mid & small | Technology | Unknown | No material ticker‑specific news; PT raised but irrelevant |\n| **MX** | -21.3% | Mid & small | Technology | Unknown | No material ticker‑specific news |\n| **BLDP** | -20.6% | Mid & small | Industrials | Unknown | No material ticker‑specific news |\n| **DGXX** | -20.0% | Mid & small | Financial Services | Unknown | No material ticker‑specific news |\n| **PDYN** | -19.8% | Mid & small | Technology | Unknown | No material ticker‑specific news |\n| **FCEL** | -19.7% | Mid & small | Industrials | Unknown | No material ticker‑specific news |\n\n---\n\n### 🧭 Thematic / Sector Weakness\n\n#### 🔻 AI / Semiconductor Rout → `Technology` + `Communication Services`\n\n**MU** (-14.2%), **MRVL** (-13.2%), **ARM** (-12.9%), **SNDK** (-12.5%), **GLW** (-11.5%), **WDC** (-11.1%), **QCOM** (-11.0%), **AMD** (-10.9%), **INTC** (-10.7%), **ORCL** (-10.7%), **LRCX** (-10.2%), **AMAT** (-9.7%), **KLAC** (-9.2%), **STX** (-9.0%), **SMCI** (-12.0%), **NBIS** (-13.9%)\n\n- Broadcom (AVGO) reiterated its FY2027 AI semiconductor revenue target of >$100B instead of raising it, triggering a violent sector‑wide profit‑taking cascade.\n- The May nonfarm payrolls print surged to 172,000 (est. 85,000), pushing 2‑year Treasury yields above 4.15% and cementing expectations for a Fed rate hike by year‑end.\n- Micron’s “Why Is Micron Stock Falling Friday?” article explicitly ties the decline to Broadcom’s unchanged AI outlook and rate‑hike jitters.\n- Marvell fell after an explosive multi‑day rally fueled by NVIDIA CEO Jensen Huang’s “next trillion‑dollar company” comment at Computex; the Broadcom shock sparked aggressive profit‑taking.\n- ARM’s article cites Broadcom’s guidance as the primary catalyst, with Mizuho raising its PT to $500 but momentum overstretched.\n- SanDisk, Western Digital, and Seagate were caught in the memory/storage downdraft despite Goldman Sachs’ positive view on memory supply tightness.\n- QCOM slid despite JPMorgan’s upbeat preview of its June 24 Investor Day, as the Broadcom‑induced selloff overwhelmed stock‑specific narratives.\n- Intel and AMD both fell in sympathy; Intel’s article notes profit‑taking after a 459% 12‑month run, while AMD’s notes the quantum‑AI collaboration with JPMorgan but the tape overwhelmed.\n- Oracle dropped as BNP Paribas flagged concerns over debt‑funded AI capex potentially hitting $80‑100B in FY27, adding to AI infrastructure jitters.\n- Equipment names (LRCX, AMAT, KLAC) were slammed alongside the SOX index, with no stock‑specific news.\n- SMCI, a server builder, was hit by the same AI hardware unwind; its article references the Broadcom trigger.\n- Nebius slid due to AI infrastructure cooling; a separate catalyst was NVIDIA director Mark Stevens’ $221M share sale, but the sector theme was dominant.\n\n#### 🚀 Space Stocks Collapse → `Technology` (Communication Equipment) + `Industrials` (Aerospace & Defense)\n\n**ASTS** (-14.5%), **PL** (-26.2%)\n\n- S&P Global ruled out changes to index eligibility rules, dashing hopes that SpaceX could quickly join the S&P 500 post‑IPO, removing a passive‑flow catalyst for the entire sector.\n- AST SpaceMobile fell 10.4% intraday, as did Rocket Lab, Redwire, and others; the “Space Stocks Bleed” article explicitly names ASTS among the casualties.\n- Planet Labs, a satellite‑imaging company, was doubly hit: it announced a $1.5B at‑the‑market equity offering, sparking severe dilution fears, while also suffering from the broader space‑sector rout that AXTs and other space names experienced.\n\n#### ☀️ Clean Energy / Solar Pressure → `Energy` (Solar)\n\n**FSLR** (-13.6%)\n\n- The “Nasdaq 100 Plunges Over 3%…” article notes First Solar fell ~11% as part of a clean‑energy rout tied to threatened rollback of solar tax credits and the surge in Treasury yields.\n- The Invesco WilderHill Clean Energy ETF cratered >9%, and Enphase Energy was singled out as the worst Russell 1000 decliner; FSLR was caught in the same wave.\n\n---\n\n### 🎯 Idiosyncratic Movers\n\n**NOK** (–13.6%, Large) → Idiosyncratic  \n- Nokia issued €500M in senior unsecured notes due 2032, using proceeds to refinance existing debt. Combined with profit‑taking after a 208% 12‑month rally, the stock fell sharply. Broader tech weakness added to the move.\n\n**IONQ** (–13.2%, Large) → Idiosyncratic  \n- High‑beta quantum computing name faded after a fast run‑up on CHIPS Act grant speculation. Quantinuum’s IPO opened 13% above its $60 offer price but then faded, cooling enthusiasm across quantum peers.\n\n**CRCL** (–12.0%, Large) → Idiosyncratic  \n- Mizuho maintained a Neutral rating but slashed its price target from $135 to $85. The stock, a crypto‑exposed financial services firm, also suffered from the sharp decline in Bitcoin (BTC) to $59,163, its lowest since 2024.\n\n**BE** (–11.7%, Large) → Idiosyncratic  \n- Bloom Energy gave back some of its recent tariff‑relief gains as traders reassessed the impact of the new 15% tariff rate on steel/aluminum derivatives and a broader risk‑off tone. No fresh company‑specific negative.\n\n**ZUMZ** (–25.9%, Mid & small) → Idiosyncratic  \n- Zumiez reported Q1 EPS of –$0.82, missing consensus by a penny, and guided Q2 EPS to –$0.23 to –$0.08 versus expectations of $0.09. Sales guidance of $210M–$215M was below the $218.4M estimate. Management cited “increasing pressure on consumers” and a dynamic macro environment.\n\n**SCCO, FN, STRL, POET, TE, BRUN, MX, BLDP, DGXX, PDYN, FCEL** – No material ticker‑specific news in window.  \n- For each of these tickers, no Benzinga article explains the move. The declines likely reflect sympathy with the broad market selloff, sector rotation, or profit‑taking in extended positions. Without a documented catalyst, they are categorized as unexplained.\n\n---\n\n### 📊 Sector Roll-up\n\n| Sector | Losers (count) | Avg 1D | Shared driver? |\n|--------|---------------:|-------:|----------------|\n| Technology | 17 | –13.1% | Yes – Broadcom AI outlook, rate‑hike fears |\n| Industrials | 7 | –19.0% | No – mix of space, clean energy, idiosyncratic |\n| Consumer Cyclical | 1 | –25.9% | No – earnings miss, weak guidance |\n| Basic Materials | 1 | –10.7% | No – likely macro/commodity |\n| Energy | 1 | –13.6% | Yes – clean‑energy tax‑credit fears |\n| Communication Services | 1 | –13.9% | Partial – AI cooldown + insider selling |\n| Financial Services | 2 | –16.0% | No – analyst cut & crypto weakness |\n| Consumer Cyclical | 1 | –25.9% | No – earnings miss |\n\n---\n\n### 👁 Watch\n\n- **Micron (MU) earnings June 24** – Consensus expects EPS of $19.30 on revenue of $33.88B. A miss or cautious guidance could extend the semi rout; a beat could offer a floor.\n\n- **Broadcom (AVGO) fallout** – Further analyst downgrades or cuts to AI revenue estimates could prolong sector weakness. Goldman Sachs and BofA both reiterated Buy ratings, but the stock’s 12.6% drop shows fragile sentiment.\n\n- **SpaceX IPO (SPCX) on June 12** – Priced at $135/share, implying a $1.75T valuation. If the debut disappoints, space stocks (ASTS, PL) could face another leg down. Conversely, a strong opening might revive sentiment.\n\n- **Oracle (ORCL) earnings** – With capex expectations as high as $100B for FY27, any whiff of slower spending or weaker OCI growth could hit the stock and the broader AI infrastructure theme.\n\n- **Clean energy policy** – Possible acceleration of federal tax‑credit phaseouts could keep pressure on FSLR and the solar complex. Watch for legislative updates.\n\n- **Nokia (NOK) post‑bond restructuring** – The new €500M note adds modest leverage; monitor for any further capital‑raising moves or margin commentary.	\N	{"calls": [{"step": "synthesis", "model": "deepseek-v4-pro", "api_model": "deepseek-v4-pro", "input_tokens": 135133, "output_tokens": 7291, "pricing_model": "deepseek-v4-pro", "input_cost_usd": 0.058783, "total_cost_usd": 0.065126, "output_cost_usd": 0.006343, "cache_read_cost_usd": 0.0, "cache_write_cost_usd": 0.0, "cache_read_input_tokens": 0, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 0}], "call_count": 1, "started_at": "2026-06-07T00:43:17.434001+00:00", "completed_at": "2026-06-07T00:45:34.130522+00:00", "current_step": null, "pricing_note": "Token counts from DeepSeek Chat Completions API response.usage. USD estima