2026-06-19	losers	benzinga	complete	### 🗓 2026-06-19 — R1D Losers Brief\n\n**One-liner**: ACN’s post-earnings guidance cut triggered a broad IT-services selloff, crypto weakness dragged MSTR, and falling crude pressured Energy, while the rest of the tape was dominated by company-specific news or absent catalysts.\n\n---\n\n### 📉 Losers At a Glance\n\n| Ticker | 1D | Cap | Sector | Driver type | Catalyst (≤12 words) |\n|--------|---:|-----|--------|-------------|----------------------|\n| **IBM** | -4.3% | Mega | Technology | Sector | Accenture guidance cut ripples through IT services |\n| **LMT** | -4.1% | Mega | Industrials | Unknown | No material ticker-specific news in window |\n| **RTX** | -3.1% | Mega | Industrials | Unknown | No material ticker-specific news in window |\n| **SAP** | -2.9% | Mega | Technology | Unknown | No material ticker-specific news in window |\n| **NEM** | -2.8% | Mega | Basic Materials | Unknown | No material ticker-specific news in window |\n| **APP** | -2.8% | Mega | Technology | Unknown | No material ticker-specific news in window |\n| **RIO** | -2.7% | Mega | Basic Materials | Unknown | No material ticker-specific news in window |\n| **BP** | -2.7% | Mega | Energy | Sector | Oil falls 2% on US-Iran deal reopening Strait of Hormuz |\n| **COP** | -2.6% | Mega | Energy | Sector | Oil falls 2% on US-Iran deal reopening Strait of Hormuz |\n| **PFE** | -2.6% | Mega | Healthcare | Idiosyncratic | CFO Dave Denton to step down Aug 15 |\n| **BHP** | -2.5% | Mega | Basic Materials | Unknown | No material ticker-specific news in window |\n| **SCHW** | -2.4% | Mega | Financial Services | Unknown | No material ticker-specific news in window |\n| **PLTR** | -2.3% | Mega | Technology | Idiosyncratic | Burry highlights Ukraine building in-house Palantir replacement |\n| **HWM** | -2.3% | Mega | Industrials | Unknown | No material ticker-specific news in window |\n| **CVX** | -2.2% | Mega | Energy | Sector | Oil falls 2% on US-Iran deal reopening Strait of Hormuz |\n| **ACN** | -17.6% | Large cap | Technology | Idiosyncratic | Q3 revenue miss; FY26 revenue guidance cut below Street |\n| **INFY** | -9.6% | Large cap | Technology | Sector | Accenture guidance cut hits IT-services peers |\n| **ASTS** | -8.8% | Large cap | Technology | Unknown | No material ticker-specific news in window |\n| **CTSH** | -8.8% | Large cap | Technology | Sector | Accenture guidance cut hits IT-services peers |\n| **STLD** | -7.9% | Large cap | Basic Materials | Idiosyncratic | Q2 EPS guidance $3.51-$3.55; analyst PT cuts |\n| **KR** | -7.9% | Large cap | Consumer Defensive | Idiosyncratic | Q1 EPS miss; margin squeeze, soft outlook |\n| **CPNG** | -6.8% | Large cap | Consumer Cyclical | Unknown | No material ticker-specific news in window |\n| **LHX** | -6.2% | Large cap | Industrials | Unknown | No material ticker-specific news in window |\n| **MSTR** | -5.8% | Large cap | Technology | Sector | Bitcoin drops below $63k; STRC preferred at record lows |\n| **NOC** | -5.5% | Large cap | Industrials | Unknown | No material ticker-specific news in window |\n| **NVCR** | -18.8% | Mid & small cap | Healthcare | Idiosyncratic | Phase 3 TRIDENT glioblastoma trial misses primary endpoint |\n| **AXTI** | -12.7% | Mid & small cap | Technology | Idiosyncratic | 8.6M-share lockup expiration June 21 pressures shares |\n| **HURN** | -12.6% | Mid & small cap | Industrials | Unknown | No material ticker-specific news in window |\n| **EPAM** | -11.6% | Mid & small cap | Technology | Sector | Accenture guidance cut hits IT-services peers |\n| **AADX** | -11.0% | Mid & small cap | Industrials | Unknown | No material ticker-specific news in window |\n| **AVEX** | -10.7% | Mid & small cap | Industrials | Unknown | No material ticker-specific news in window |\n| **GLOB** | -10.1% | Mid & small cap | Technology | Sector | Accenture guidance cut hits IT-services peers |\n| **AAMI** | -10.0% | Mid & small cap | Financial Services | Unknown | No material ticker-specific news in window |\n| **INOD** | -8.8% | Mid & small cap | Technology | Unknown | No material ticker-specific news in window |\n| **RXT** | -8.8% | Mid & small cap | Technology | Unknown | No material negative news; likely profit-taking after large run |\n\n---\n\n### 🧭 Thematic / Sector Weakness\n\n**Information Technology Services — Accenture Guidance Cut Ripples** → `Technology`\n\n- **ACN** -17.6%, **IBM** -4.3%, **INFY** -9.6%, **CTSH** -8.8%, **EPAM** -11.6%, **GLOB** -10.1%\n- Accenture reported Q3 revenue $18.700B (miss $18.745B est.) and narrowed FY26 sales guidance to $71.763B-$72.460B from prior $71.763B-$73.157B, below the $74.006B Street view.\n- IBM, INFY, CTSH, EPAM, and GLOB all fell on the Accenture read-across; no separate company‑specific news triggered those moves.\n- The Benzinga article explicitly named CTSH, GLOB, and EPAM as declining on the Accenture read-across; IBM was called out in its own article as falling after Accenture lowered guidance.\n\n**Energy — Oil Decline on Iran Peace Deal** → `Energy`\n\n- **BP** -2.7%, **COP** -2.6%, **CVX** -2.2%\n- WTI crude fell ~2% to $75 after the US‑Iran 14‑point MOU committed to reopen the Strait of Hormuz, lifting the naval blockade and promising Iranian crude waivers.\n- The Iran deal’s oil‑supply implications directly pressured energy stocks; the sector was the worst performer in the S&P 500 that day.\n\n**Crypto / Bitcoin — Hawkish Fed Sinks Digital Assets** → `Technology`\n\n- **MSTR** -5.8%\n- Bitcoin slid below $63,000 after Fed Chair Warsh kept rates unchanged but signaled a hawkish shift, with the dot plot pointing to a potential hike in 2026.\n- MSTR’s STRC preferred stock hit record lows ($85.32 intraday), as the company’s Bitcoin‑treasury model came under renewed scrutiny.\n- Broader crypto market fell ~4%, dragging down MSTR as a high‑beta Bitcoin proxy.\n\n---\n\n### 🎯 Idiosyncratic Movers\n\n**PFE** (-2.6%, mega) → Idiosyncratic\n- CFO Dave Denton will step down on August 15 to take a role outside healthcare. Interim CFO Cecile Guegan named. The departure overshadowed Pfizer’s reaffirmed FY26 guidance.\n\n**PLTR** (-2.3%, mega) → Idiosyncratic\n- Michael Burry highlighted that Ukraine’s defense minister stated the country built its own Delta software “no worse” than Palantir and is increasingly using it instead, adding weight to Burry’s bearish thesis.\n\n**STLD** (-7.9%, large) → Idiosyncratic\n- Steel Dynamics issued Q2 EPS guidance of $3.51‑$3.55 (vs. Q1 $2.78) after the close on June 17; B of A and Wells Fargo cut price targets. The market reacted negatively to the outlook and analyst actions.\n\n**KR** (-7.9%, large) → Idiosyncratic\n- Q1 adjusted EPS $1.58 missed the $1.59 consensus; gross margin contracted on higher transportation costs, egg deflation, and price investments. Management guided Q2 ID sales (ex‑fuel) roughly in line with Q1, and FY EPS reaffirmed at $5.10‑$5.30.\n\n**NVCR** (-18.8%, mid) → Idiosyncratic\n- Phase 3 TRIDENT trial in newly diagnosed glioblastoma did not meet its primary endpoint of improved overall survival (median OS 17.7 vs. 17.5 months, p=0.519).\n\n**AXTI** (-12.7%, mid) → Idiosyncratic\n- A stock lockup expiration of 8.6 million shares is set for June 21, 2026, creating near‑term selling pressure. (Separately, AXT’s subsidiary secured a $25.4M InP wafer supply agreement, but that did not offset the lockup overhang.)\n\n**LMT** (-4.1%), **RTX** (-3.1%), **SAP** (-2.9%), **NEM** (-2.8%), **APP** (-2.8%), **RIO** (-2.7%), **BHP** (-2.5%), **SCHW** (-2.4%), **HWM** (-2.3%), **ASTS** (-8.8%), **CPNG** (-6.8%), **LHX** (-6.2%), **NOC** (-5.5%), **HURN** (-12.6%), **AADX** (-11.0%), **AVEX** (-10.7%), **AAMI** (-10.0%), **INOD** (-8.8%), **RXT** (-8.8%)\n- No material ticker‑specific news in window for any of these names. Moves may be technical, sympathy, or unexplained. For RXT, the decline followed a large recent rally on an AI‑pivot narrative and appears to be profit‑taking; no negative company‑specific news appeared.\n\n---\n\n### 📊 Sector Roll-up\n\n| Sector | Losers (count) | Avg 1D | Shared driver? |\n|--------|---------------:|-------:|----------------|\n| Technology | 13 | -6.4% | Yes — IT‑services cluster (ACN guide cut); crypto weakness (MSTR) |\n| Industrials | 8 | -5.2% | No — mostly unknown catalysts |\n| Basic Materials | 4 | -3.7% | No — STLD guidance but rest unknown |\n| Energy | 3 | -2.5% | Yes — crude decline on Iran deal |\n| Healthcare | 2 | -10.7% | No — separate idiosyncratic events |\n| Financial Services | 2 | -6.2% | No — both unknown |\n| Consumer Defensive | 1 | -7.9% | No — KR earnings miss |\n| Consumer Cyclical | 1 | -6.8% | No — unknown |\n\n---\n\n### 👁 Watch\n\n- **IBM Q2 earnings** on July 22 (est. EPS $3.00; rev $17.85B) — will test whether IT‑services demand concerns extend beyond Accenture.\n- **PFE CFO transition** effective Aug 15; leadership vacuum may keep the stock on a defensive footing.\n- **MSTR STRC preferred dividend payment** due June 30; any forced BTC sales to cover obligations could amplify downside in Bitcoin and MSTR.\n- **ACN’s $4.18B cybersecurity acquisition** (Dragos, runZero, NetRise) expected to close Aug‑Sep 2026; integration progress and accretion timeline remain key.\n- **NVCR** to present additional TRIDENT analyses; any signal of benefit in subgroups could prompt a relief rally.\n- **AXTI lockup expiration** on June 21, 2026 — post‑lockup selling could keep pressure on shares.	\N	{"calls": [{"step": "synthesis", "model": "deepseek-v4-pro", "api_model": "deepseek-v4-pro", "input_tokens": 109658, "output_tokens": 8795, "pricing_model": "deepseek-v4-pro", "input_cost_usd": 0.047701, "total_cost_usd": 0.055353, "output_cost_usd": 0.007652, "cache_read_cost_usd": 0.0, "cache_write_cost_usd": 0.0, "cache_read_input_tokens": 0, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 0}], "call_count": 1, "started_at": "2026-06-19T14:10:53.949148+00:00", "completed_at": "2026-06-19T14:13:02.077947+00:00", "current_step": null, "pricing