2026-07-10	losers	benzinga	complete	### 🗓 2026-07-10 — R1D Losers Brief\n\n**One-liner**: Rackspace’s guidance cut, AstraZeneca’s trial failure, and PepsiCo’s earnings miss drove the day’s biggest losses, while consumer defensives broadly weakened on no single shared catalyst.\n\n---\n\n### 📉 Losers At a Glance\n\n| Ticker | 1D | Cap | Sector | Driver type | Catalyst (≤12 words) |\n|--------|---:|-----|--------|-------------|----------------------|\n| AZN | -5.4% | Mega | Healthcare | Idiosyncratic | Phase 3 CARDIO‑TTRansform trial missed primary endpoint |\n| COST | -4.2% | Mega | Consumer Defensive | Mixed | JPM PT cut + June sales update; broader staples rotation |\n| PEP | -4.1% | Mega | Consumer Defensive | Idiosyncratic | Q2 adj. EPS miss, high gas prices pressuring demand |\n| PM | -2.9% | Mega | Consumer Defensive | Unknown | No material ticker‑specific news in window |\n| CRM | -2.8% | Mega | Technology | Idiosyncratic | KeyBanc downgrade to Sector Weight, soft business trends |\n| IBM | -2.6% | Mega | Technology | Idiosyncratic | Starbucks building in‑house tools to replace IBM software |\n| XOM | -2.5% | Mega | Energy | Idiosyncratic | Mizuho PT cut to $170, Neutral rating |\n| PLTR | -2.4% | Mega | Technology | Idiosyncratic | Rebound Capital warns valuations still too high |\n| JNJ | -2.3% | Mega | Healthcare | Unknown | No material ticker‑specific news in window |\n| CB | -2.3% | Mega | Financial Services | Unknown | Multiple PT raises; no explicit negative catalyst |\n| LMT | -2.0% | Mega | Industrials | Unknown | No material ticker‑specific news in window |\n| COP | -1.8% | Mega | Energy | Unknown | No material ticker‑specific news in window |\n| PBR | -1.6% | Mega | Energy | Unknown | No material ticker‑specific news in window |\n| UL | -1.6% | Mega | Consumer Defensive | Unknown | No material ticker‑specific news in window |\n| BP | -1.6% | Mega | Energy | Idiosyncratic | California lawsuit over AI fuel‑pricing tool |\n| AXON | -4.2% | Large | Industrials | Unknown | No material ticker‑specific news in window |\n| CINF | -4.1% | Large | Financial Services | Unknown | No material ticker‑specific news in window |\n| TW | -3.5% | Large | Financial Services | Idiosyncratic | Barclays PT cut to $114 |\n| MCK | -3.4% | Large | Healthcare | Unknown | No material ticker‑specific news in window |\n| NTR | -2.9% | Large | Basic Materials | Unknown | No material ticker‑specific news in window |\n| AMX | -2.8% | Large | Communication Services | Unknown | No material ticker‑specific news in window |\n| ICE | -2.7% | Large | Financial Services | Idiosyncratic | Barclays PT cut to $180; regulator to block CME oil‑contract move |\n| AZO | -2.6% | Large | Consumer Cyclical | Unknown | No material ticker‑specific news in window |\n| DOW | -2.5% | Large | Basic Materials | Unknown | No material ticker‑specific news in window |\n| DVN | -2.5% | Large | Energy | Idiosyncratic | Truist PT cut to $61 |\n| RXT | -32.9% | Mid & small | Technology | Idiosyncratic | Cut FY26 revenue guide; $250M common share offering |\n| CABO | -13.1% | Mid & small | Communication Services | Idiosyncratic | Q2 sales guidance $346M‑$352M vs $350.87M est |\n| BOIL | -11.9% | Mid & small | Financial Services | Unknown | No material ticker‑specific news in window |\n| GVA | -11.5% | Mid & small | Industrials | Idiosyncratic | Goldman Sachs downgrade to Sell, PT $139 |\n| STUB | -9.9% | Mid & small | Technology | Unknown | No material ticker‑specific news in window |\n| BRBR | -9.8% | Mid & small | Consumer Defensive | Unknown | No material ticker‑specific news in window |\n| MDA | -9.2% | Mid & small | Technology | Idiosyncratic | Upsized $819M bought deal at $35.60/share |\n| ROAD | -7.5% | Mid & small | Industrials | Unknown | No material ticker‑specific news in window |\n| TSAT | -7.3% | Mid & small | Communication Services | Unknown | No material ticker‑specific news in window |\n| EPAC | -7.1% | Mid & small | Industrials | Idiosyncratic | Acquisition of SFE Group; FY26 guide cut on ME conflict |\n\n---\n\n### 🧭 Thematic / Sector Weakness\n\n*No shared sector/theme driver identified from ticker articles.*  \nConsumer Defensive names weakened broadly (COST, PEP, PM, UL, BRBR) but each fall traced to a distinct trigger—JPMorgan cut for COST, a Q2 miss for PEP, and no article‑based catalyst for PM, UL, or BRBR. Energy, Technology, and Financials also appeared in the losers list without overlapping documented catalysts.\n\n---\n\n### 🎯 Idiosyncratic Movers\n\n**AZN** (-5.4%, Mega) → Idiosyncratic  \n- Phase 3 CARDIO‑TTRansform trial of eplontersen for ATTR‑CM missed primary endpoint vs placebo.  \n- Pre‑specified subgroup showed nominally significant HR 0.71 with monotherapy, but no benefit in patients already on stabilizers.  \n- Full data to be presented at ESC Congress in August 2026.\n\n**COST** (-4.2%, Mega) → Idiosyncratic (with broad staple overhang)  \n- JPMorgan lowered price target from $1,110 to $1,100, Overweight maintained.  \n- June sales update: net sales $29.24B (+10.6% YoY), US comps +10.6%, Canada +3.7%; gasoline deflation and FX flagged as headwinds.  \n- An article on consumer staples ETFs (Warsh’s Collateral Victim) highlighted Costco as a top holding vulnerable to bear steepening, tariffs, and weakening consumer.\n\n**PEP** (-4.1%, Mega) → Idiosyncratic  \n- Q2 earnings: core EPS $2.20 missed $2.21 est; revenue $24.18B beat $23.96B.  \n- Management cited high gas prices slowing impulse purchases in convenience channels.  \n- Full‑year guidance reaffirmed (organic sales growth 2‑4%, adj. EPS $8.55‑$8.71).  \n- Wells Fargo cut PT to $140, Citigroup downgraded to Neutral with PT $145.\n\n**CRM** (-2.8%, Mega) → Idiosyncratic  \n- KeyBanc downgraded to Sector Weight, citing difficult‑to‑find evidence of meaningful upside and soft channel checks.  \n- Agentforce events feedback suggested product still needs development; recent quarterly results disappointing.\n\n**IBM** (-2.6%, Mega) → Idiosyncratic  \n- Bloomberg report: Starbucks building in‑house AI‑powered alternatives to replace IBM maintenance management tool, with potential rollout by end of next year.  \n- Starbucks aims to cut $2B in costs, with $10M in software savings targeted this fiscal year.\n\n**PLTR** (-2.4%, Mega) → Idiosyncratic  \n- Rebound Capital warned Palantir remains a “trap” at ~80x forward earnings; forward‑deployed engineering costs mask low true gross margins.  \n- Competition from Amazon ($1B) and Microsoft ($2.5B) embedding engineers, plus European data‑sovereignty headwinds (France migrating to ChapsVision).\n\n**BP** (-1.6%, Mega) → Idiosyncratic  \n- California drivers sued BP and other gas station operators, alleging use of AI‑powered pricing tool that coordinated fuel price increases in violation of state antitrust law.\n\n**TW** (-3.5%, Large) → Idiosyncratic  \n- Barclays lowered PT to $114 (Equal‑Weight), contributing to the selloff.\n\n**ICE** (-2.7%, Large) → Idiosyncratic  \n- Barclays PT cut to $180 (Overweight).  \n- FT report: regulator to block CME’s bid to fast‑track round‑the‑clock oil contracts — potential read‑through to exchange stocks.\n\n**DVN** (-2.5%, Large) → Idiosyncratic  \n- Truist Securities lowered PT to $61, Buy maintained.\n\n**RXT** (-32.9%, Mid & small) → Idiosyncratic  \n- Cut FY26 revenue guide to $2.45‑$2.55B from $2.60‑$2.70B; adj. EBITDA guide lowered to $285‑$295M from $305‑$315M.  \n- Reasons: planned exit from low‑margin public cloud resale, supply timing, and geopolitical delays.  \n- Filed prospectus for $250M common share offering; also filed mixed shelf.\n\n**CABO** (-13.1%, Mid & small) → Idiosyncratic  \n- Cable One provided Q2 sales guidance of $346M‑$352M, slightly below $350.87M consensus estimate.\n\n**GVA** (-11.5%, Mid & small) → Idiosyncratic  \n- Goldman Sachs downgraded to Sell from Neutral, PT $139.\n\n**MDA** (-9.2%, Mid & small) → Idiosyncratic  \n- Increased bought deal equity offering to 23M shares (from 20M) at $35.60/share, raising ~$819M gross proceeds, diluting existing shareholders.\n\n**EPAC** (-7.1%, Mid & small) → Idiosyncratic  \n- Announced acquisition of SFE Group for ~$451.4M cash, expanding addressable market.  \n- Q3 earnings call: FY26 organic growth guidance cut to 1‑2% (from previous mid‑single‑digit) due to delayed service projects in Middle East conflict.\n\n**PM, JNJ, CB, LMT, COP, PBR, UL, AXON, CINF, MCK, NTR, AMX, AZO, DOW, BOIL, STUB, BRBR, ROAD, TSAT** — No material ticker‑specific news in window; moves may be technical, sympathy, or unexplained.\n\n---\n\n### 📊 Sector Roll‑up\n\n| Sector | Losers (count) | Avg 1D | Shared driver? |\n|--------|---------------:|-------:|----------------|\n| Consumer Defensive | 5 | -4.5% | No |\n| Technology | 6 | -8.9% | No |\n| Energy | 6 | -1.9% | No |\n| Financial Services | 5 | -4.9% | No |\n| Healthcare | 3 | -3.7% | No |\n| Industrials | 5 | -6.4% | No |\n| Communication Services | 3 | -7.7% | No |\n| Basic Materials | 2 | -2.7% | No |\n| Consumer Cyclical | 1 | -2.6% | No |\n\n---\n\n### 👁 Watch\n\n- **AZN/Ionis** — Full eplontersen CARDIO‑TTRansform data at ESC Congress in August 2026 may provide clarity; monotherapy signal could support regulatory discussions.  \n- **PepsiCo** — Q3 guidance calls for gradual North America improvement; management will need to show volume inflection or risk further downgrades.  \n- **Rackspace** — FY26 guidance relies on Enterprise AI ramp; execution vs. $250M offering dilution will be closely scrutinized.  \n- **Granite Construction** — Goldman Sell follows recent weakness; any infrastructure spending updates could swing the stock.  \n- **EPAC** — SFE acquisition expected to close in F1Q27; integration and leverage reduction path will be in focus.  \n- **MDA Space** — Over‑allotment option could add further dilution; acquisition of CLS must deliver to justify the raise.  \n- **Consumer Staples EM** — With 30‑year yields above 5% and a July 24 tariff cliff, further multiple compress