KHY** | -5.22% | Mega | Technology | Sector | CXMT DDR5 progress erases >$700B memory-sector value |\n| **MRVL** | -4.99% | Mega | Technology | Sector | Broad semis rotation; CEO/president insider sales disclosed |\n| **AMAT** | -4.92% | Mega | Technology | Mixed | Needham PT $740→$580; 9 consecutive lower sessions |\n| **NOW** | -4.87% | Mega | Technology | Sector | AI-financing rotation; high P/E software sold; earnings Oct 28 |\n\n#### Large Cap\n\n| Ticker | 1D | Cap | Sector | Driver type | Catalyst (≤12 words) |\n|--------|---:|-----|--------|-------------|----------------------|\n| **BSP** | -16.43% | Large | Technology | Idiosyncratic | Post-$330M Berkshire placement; tech selloff; Airtable layoffs cited |\n| **NBIS** | -11.89% | Large | Technology | Sector | CoreWeave bond yield 16%; neocloud debt-financing contagion |\n| **CRWV** | -11.88% | Large | Technology | Sector | Own $50B debt; 2031 bond ~14.5% yield; CDS >60% default odds |\n| **TWLO** | -10.91% | Large | Technology | Sector | AI-agent monetization doubts; RBC Sell/$165 PT Oct 15 |\n| **OKTA** | -10.66% | Large | Technology | Sector | Bernstein downgrade to Mkt Perform; cybersecurity valuation concerns |\n| **MSTR** | -9.65% | Large | Technology | Idiosyncratic | MSCI non-operating eligibility rule; ~$2B potential index selling |\n| **SMCI** | -9.62% | Large | Technology | Idiosyncratic | 7th consecutive loss; CFO change; inventory $12.9B; neg FCF |\n| **CBRS** | -9.30% | Large | Technology | Sector | No company catalyst; AI-compute sector rotation |\n| **SMCIP** | -8.31% | Large | Technology | Idiosyncratic | Preferred shares sympathy to SMCI common decline |\n| **RKLB** | -7.99% | Large | Industrials | Idiosyncratic | Neutron launch delayed to ≥Q2 2027; composite dome defect |\n\n#### Mid & Small Cap\n\n| Ticker | 1D | Cap | Sector | Driver type | Catalyst (≤12 words) |\n|--------|---:|-----|--------|-------------|----------------------|\n| **GRAL** | -15.37% | Mid | Healthcare | Idiosyncratic | CEO sold 20K shares Oct 15; pending FDA Galleri decision |\n| **SHAZ** | -14.60% | Small | Technology | Unknown | No ticker-specific catalyst identified in research window |\n| **KOPN** | -13.53% | Small | Technology | Unknown | No earnings/downgrade catalyst; semis rotation may explain |\n| **TLK** | -13.35% | Mid | Comm. Services | Idiosyncratic | Phase 2 fiber spin-off restructuring mechanics concern |\n| **BAND** | -13.09% | Small | Technology | Mixed | Morgan Stanley initiated Equalweight $56; rate/inflation pressure |\n| **BLZE** | -12.90% | Small | Technology | Unknown | No company-specific catalyst identified in research window |\n| **AEHR** | -12.74% | Small | Technology | Unknown | No company-specific catalyst; 7-day decline 20.6% |\n| **SECZ** | -12.61% | Small | Technology | Idiosyncratic | Guidance cut to $70–80M; pos. EBITDA target withdrawn; Citi downgrade |\n| **WGS** | -12.61% | Small | Healthcare | Unknown | No catalyst; low volume 32,972 shares vs 919K avg |\n| **TEM** | -11.93% | Mid | Healthcare | Mixed | AI-rotation pressure; earnings Nov 2; Moderna collab announced |\n\n---\n\n### 🧭 Thematic / Sector Weakness\n\n---\n\n**Cybersecurity Contagion — PANW Firewall Vulnerability** → `Software - Infrastructure / Cybersecurity`\n\n- **PANW** -6.91%, **FTNT** -6.79%, **CRWD** -6.37%, **OKTA** -10.66%\n- Palo Alto Networks disclosed a network-firewall platform vulnerability earlier in the week and issued a security advisory Wednesday; CISA added it to the Known Exploited Vulnerabilities (KEV) catalog during the week (Investor's Business Daily, Oct 16).\n- The KEV listing drove sector-level selling: Fortinet, CrowdStrike, Okta, and Zscaler were all cited as declining on the same catalyst (Investor's Business Daily, Oct 16).\n- PANW's weekly decline reached -18%; the First Trust Nasdaq Cybersecurity ETF fell approximately 10% for the week; CrowdStrike also fell approximately -18% over the same five sessions (247WallSt, Oct 16).\n- Bernstein analyst Peter Weed separately downgraded **OKTA**, Palo Alto Networks, and SentinelOne to Market Perform from Outperform, citing cybersecurity-sector gains of nearly 100% since early 2026 and valuation at or above fair value (MarketBeat via OKTA research, Oct 16).\n- What links these names: all are pure-play cybersecurity software vendors. The PANW-specific vulnerability raised questions about product risk across the sector, while the Bernstein downgrade on valuation compounded the move. No company-specific vulnerability was reported for FTNT, CRWD, or OKTA beyond the PANW contagion read-through.\n\n---\n\n**Memory Sector — CXMT DDR5 Competition Shock** → `Semiconductors / Memory`\n\n- **SKHY** -5.22% (mega); knock-on to broader semis including **ARM** -5.98%, **AMD** -5.87%, **LRCX** -5.26%, **AMAT** -4.92%, **MRVL** -4.99%, **INTC** -6.35%\n- China's ChangXin Memory Technologies (CXMT) announced progress on a denser "4F2" DRAM cell architecture and said it planned to ship DDR5 server-memory modules before end of 2026; reports said the announcement had erased more than $700 billion in memory-sector market value since approximately October 10 (Investing.com, Oct 16).\n- The CXMT development raised concerns about market-share and pricing-power erosion at SK hynix, Samsung, and Micron (Yahoo Finance, Oct 16–17).\n- Reports also noted SK hynix delayed equipment orders for its next-generation HBM4 production line, creating capacity-roadmap uncertainty, and accepted approximately a 10% increase in ASML EUV/DUV spare-parts prices from 2027 (Investing.com, Oct 16).\n- Foreign investors sold ₩16.88 trillion of SK hynix shares and ₩6.98 trillion of Samsung shares over the preceding month (Money Today, Oct 16).\n- Goldman Sachs told clients that CXMT concerns were overstated and maintained a positive view on SKHY and Samsung, but this did not arrest Friday's decline (247WallSt, Oct 16).\n- The Philadelphia Semiconductor Index fell 3.83%, with all 30 constituents lower; AMD, ARM, Micron, Marvell, KLA, and Intel were all cited in the same sector-down narrative (Investing.com via Yahoo Finance, Oct 17).\n- What links these names: the CXMT DDR5 announcement was the primary documented supply-side shock. Chip-equipment names (LRCX, AMAT) faced compounding pressure from investor concern that lower memory pricing expectations reduce capex budgets for wafer-fab equipment.\n\n---\n\n**AI-Spend Durability / Financing Rotation** → `Software - Application / AI Infrastructure`\n\n- **SNOW** -8.17%, **PLTR** -6.95%, **NET** -5.94%, **NOW** -4.87%, **CRWV** -11.88%, **NBIS** -11.89%, **TWLO** -10.91%, **SMCI** -9.62%, **CBRS** -9.30%, **TEM** -11.93%\n- CoreWeave's (**CRWV**) 2031 dollar-bond yield reached approximately 16% Thursday and stood near 14.5% Friday; five-year CDS exceeded 1,000 bps, implying roughly 60% default odds within five years (CNBC, Oct 16; Reuters, Oct 16).\n- **NBIS** fell as neocloud peers faced the same debt-financing scrutiny; Morningstar maintained a $150 estimate while noting Nebius was less leveraged than CoreWeave/IREN, with $8.5 billion long-term debt (up from <$1 billion a year earlier) and quarterly interest expense rising to $119 million from $4.8 million (Yahoo Finance, Oct 16).\n- A Gartner warning published October 14 that a large share of neocloud operators might not survive independently was recirculated alongside CRWV bond-market concerns (TECHi, Oct 16).\n- Gartner projected ~$7 trillion of AI-infrastructure investment through 2030 but estimated AI data-center sales would peak at $3.6 trillion, stoking returns-on-investment doubts (Chosun, Oct 16).\n- **SNOW**, **NET**, and cloud/SaaS peers fell as investors questioned how large-scale AI infrastructure would be financed and when it would generate returns; 247WallSt cited Snowflake -7%, Cloudflare -7%, and Datadog -6% in the same thematic frame (247WallSt, Oct 16).\n- **TWLO** faced the additional read-through that AI-agent communication expansion may favor large cloud providers over specialized CPaaS vendors; RBC maintained Sell/$165 on Oct 15 (Markets Insider, Oct 17).\n- **SMCI** declined in its seventh consecutive session amid investor concern about AI-server-buildout financing; Barclays had argued on Wednesday that business AI spending growth was driven primarily by higher memory-component prices rather than genuine demand acceleration (AOL, Oct 16).\n- What links these names: CoreWeave's bond-market distress on October 16 served as the session's concrete manifestation of AI-financing-cost concerns, spreading to neocloud operators (NBIS), AI-server builders (SMCI), AI infrastructure software (SNOW, NET, NOW, PLTR), and communications platforms tied to AI-agent optionality (TWLO). No company-specific earnings warning was reported for most of these names.\n\n---\n\n### 🎯 Idiosyncratic Movers\n\n---\n\n**STX** (-5.63%, Mega) → Idiosyncratic\n\n- Wells Fargo maintained an Overweight rating but cut its price target sharply to $865 from $1,180 on Friday, October 16 — a 26.7% target reduction — while simultaneously cutting Western Digital's target to $450 from $730 (Yahoo Finance, Oct 16 08:21 ET).\n- The dual HDD-sector PT cut was the proximate trigger, isolating STX and WD from the broader semiconductor/software rotation.\n- Seagate's most recently reported fiscal Q4 showed EPS of $5.71 vs. $5.10 consensus and revenue of $3.63 billion vs. $3.50 billion consensus, up 48.5% year over year; no new earnings warning was issued (MarketBeat, Oct 16).\n- A quarterly dividend of $0.74/share was paid October 7, 2026 (MarketBeat, Oct 16).\n\n---\n\n**AMAT** (-4.92%, Mega) → Mixed\n\n- Needham cut its price target from $740 to $580 on October 16, retaining Buy; Evercore ISI had cut from $700 to $590 on October 15, retaining Outperform (MarketBeat/Yahoo Finance, Oct 16).\n- CEO Gary Dickerson said at SEMICON West on October 13 that Applied Materials would not provide a specific 2026 wafer-fab-equipment spending forecast, creating visibility concerns; long-term 2030 targets (~54% adj. gross margin, ~40% adj. operating margin, >$12B services revenue) were reiterated but did not offset near-term uncertainty (Yahoo Finance, Oct 16).\n- October 16 marked AMAT's ninth consecutive lower session, with a cumulative 24.5% decline; trailing valuation of ~35.1× earnings vs. 34.0× sector median (Trefis, Oct 17).\n- China export-control risk and a reported market-share decline from 20.0% (2024) to 17.9% (2025) in wafer-fab equipment were cited as structural concerns (TradingKey, Oct 16).\n- The company-specific PT cuts and CEO forecast withdrawal distinguish AMAT's move within the broader chip-equipment selloff.\n\n---\n\n**BSP** (-16.43%, Large) → Idiosyncratic\n\n- Berkshire Hathaway agreed to buy 10 million newly issued BSP shares at $33 each in a $330 million private placement announced October 14, lifting its stake to approximately 4%; combined with Berkshire's $600 million IPO investment in July 2026, total disclosed Berkshire investment reached $930 million (CNBC, Oct 17).\n- BSP had reached $58.56 on Wednesday October 14 (announcement day); Friday's close of $46.87 represents a ~20% retracement from that peak, as the market digested dilution and execution risk.\n- CNBC reported substantial separation packages for a large portion of Airtable's pre-acquisition team following BSP's ~$1.3 billion acquisition of Airtable agreed in August 2026 (CNBC, Oct 17).\n- BSP also agreed in September 2026 to acquire Miro for $1.36 billion, vs. Miro's $17.5 billion peak valuation; back-to-back acquisitions raised integration and cash-deployment concerns (CNBC, Oct 17).\n- BSP proceeds from the placement are designated for further acquisitions; no new company announcement was identified between Friday close and Saturday 09:00 ET.\n\n---\n\n**MSTR** (-9.65%, Large) → Idiosyncratic\n\n- MSCI adopted new eligibility rules for "non-operating companies" that could make Strategy ineligible for its global equity indexes; MSCI plans to publish the affected-company list on November 11, with implementation after the November 30 close (Yahoo Finance, Oct 16).\n- Fundstrat's Sean Farrell estimated potential forced selling by MSCI-linked products at approximately $2 billion (Yahoo Finance, Oct 16 15:18 ET).\n- Strategy contested the framework as discriminatory and argued its Bitcoin business is reported as an operating segment under U.S. GAAP (Yahoo Finance, Oct 16 12:32 ET).\n- Bitcoin was trading near $82,000, recording a second consecutive weekly decline, adding a crypto-market factor to the session's pressure (Yahoo Finance, Oct 16).\n- Cantor Fitzgerald maintained Overweight and raised its PT from $186 to $208 on October 16 — the upgrade did not arrest the decline (TIKR, Oct 17).\n\n---\n\n**SMCI** (-9.62%, Large) → Idiosyncratic\n\n- Mark Long was announced as CFO, succeeding David Weigand, amid ongoing concerns about inventory buildup ($12.9 billion at June 2026 quarter-end), negative free cash flow (-$6.97 billion for the 12 months ended June 2026), accounting weaknesses, governance, potential dilution, regulatory probes, and short interest (Seeking Alpha/Yahoo Finance, Oct 16–17).\n- October 16 marked SMCI's seventh consecutive losing session; the stock lost approximately 22% for the week (GuruFocus, Oct 16).\n- CEO Charles Liang and a director each sold 100,000 shares under Rule 10b5-1 plans; reported insider sales totaled $16.44 million over the prior 90 days (MarketBeat, Oct 16).\n- Rosenblatt reiterated Buy with a $51 PT, citing >$60 billion of AI-server orders from >20 customers and an 11% fiscal 2027 gross-margin forecast; consensus was Hold at $39.38 across 16 analysts (Yahoo Finance/Business Insider, Oct 16).\n\n---\n\n**SMCIP** (-8.31%, Large) → Idiosyncratic\n\n- No distinct SMCIP-specific news was identified in the window; the preferred-share decline tracked SMCI common's seventh consecutive losing session (MarketWatch, Oct 16).\n- The SMCIP move is best characterized as sympathy/structural to SMCI's ongoing fundamental concerns (CFO change, negative FCF, insider sales, accounting issues).\n\n---\n\n**RKLB** (-7.99%, Large) → Idiosyncratic\n\n- Rocket Lab disclosed that engineers found air pockets in the carbon-composite forward dome of Neutron's first-stage propellant tank; the Neutron maiden launch was pushed from Q4 2026 to no earlier than Q2 2027 (Yahoo Finance, Oct 15–17).\n- Four analyst firms cut price targets: Citizens $130→$90 (Outperform), Goldman Sachs $75→$67 (Neutral), Clear Street $129→$105 (Buy), KeyBanc $135→$100 (Overweight); Needham kept Buy/$120 (Investing.com/Yahoo Finance, Oct 16).\n- CEO Peter Beck said the manufacturing defect was corrected; the company reported a $2.36 billion backlog at end of Q2 2026 (Yahoo Finance, Oct 15–17).\n- ARK Investment Management purchased shares post-decline; purchase size not disclosed (Yahoo Finance, Oct 16 09:37 ET).\n\n---\n\n**GRAL** (-15.37%, Mid) → Idiosyncratic\n\n- CEO Joshua Ofman sold 20,000 shares for approximately $2.737 million on October 15 at prices from $132.67–$139.17 under a Rule 10b5-1 plan adopted December 2, 2025; the transaction was reported during the window (Investing.com, Oct 17).\n- Weiss Ratings reaffirmed a "sell (D-)" rating on October 15 (MarketBeat, Oct 16).\n- GRAIL's FDA Galleri advisory panel voted 10–0 on safety but only 6–4 on effectiveness on September 23; final FDA approval remains pending, and the effectiveness recommendation is non-binding (Bitget, Oct 16).\n- Analysts held a consensus Hold (5 Buy, 6 Hold, 1 Sell), average PT $119.80; GRAIL's reported price-to-sales was approximately 34× after a ~74% run over the preceding month (MarketBeat/Bitget, Oct 16).\n- Reported 2025 cash-burn guidance was $310 million; latest quarterly EPS was -$2.56 vs. consensus -$2.66; revenue $44.69M vs. $42.39M estimate (MarketBeat, Oct 16).\n\n---\n\n**TLK** (-13.35%, Mid) → Idiosyncratic\n\n- Investor concern focused on the mechanics of Telkom's Phase 2 wholesale-fiber restructuring: a partial spin-off into a subsidiary and a proposed buyback for dissenting shareholders at Rp2,290/share (KoalaGains, Oct 16).\n- TLK reached a new 52-week low of $10.89 during the session; volume was 527,122 shares (MarketBeat, Oct 16).\n- No earnings miss was reported; the move was driven by restructuring-mechanics uncertainty. Third-quarter earnings expected around October 29 (KoalaGains, Oct 16).\n\n---\n\n**SECZ** (-12.61%, Small) → Idiosyncratic\n\n- Securitize lowered 2026 revenue guidance to $70–80 million from $85 million and withdrew its expectation for positive adjusted EBITDA in 2026 (MarketBeat, Oct 16).\n- Q2 revenue was $14.4 million, down 5% year over year; net loss was $21.7 million (-$2.37/share); transaction volume was $5.3 billion; tokenized AUM was $4.3 billion (MarketBeat, Oct 16).\n- Citi downgraded Securitize approximately three days before October 16 (MetaCap, Oct 16).\n- Pomerantz announced two investor-investigation notices in October (MarketBeat, Oct 16).\n\n---\n\n**BAND** (-13.09%, Small) → Mixed\n\n- Morgan Stanley initiated coverage at Equalweight with a $56 price target, published 05:01 ET on October 16; an initiation at an Equalweight/hold-equivalent is a notable negative signal for a stock that had been running (Investing.com, Oct 16 05:01 ET).\n- A hotter-than-expected September inflation print pushed Treasury yields higher and was cited as a contributing factor; no fresh company-specific announcement was tied directly to the inflation release (Yahoo Finance, Oct 16 08:19 ET).\n- Bandwidth's last reported quarter beat: adj. EPS $0.37 vs. $0.36 consensus; revenue $219.90M vs. $217.62M, +22.2% year over year (MarketBeat, Oct 16).\n\n---\n\n**TEM** (-11.93%, Mid) → Mixed\n\n- Tempus AI announced Q3 2026 results will be released before market open November 2, followed by an 8:45 a.m. ET call (Market Chameleon, Oct 16); the upcoming earnings date may have prompted pre-positioning.\n- JPMorgan raised its PT to $70 from $45 (Neutral retained); Guggenheim had raised to $92/$65; Canaccord to $95/$80 — analyst support was broadly constructive (MarketBeat, Oct 16).\n- Tempus expanded a Moderna-Merck collaboration on intismeran autogene (melanoma neoantigen therapy + Keytruda); no contract value disclosed (ScanX, Oct 16 01:44 ET).\n- A market report noted TEM was down approximately 5% in pre-market before the session, with the decline attributed to broad AI-rotation selling rather than a company-specific negative (Wall Street Plumber, Oct 16).\n\n---\n\n**SHAZ** (-14.60%, Small) → Unknown\n\n- No material ticker-specific news identified in the research window. The move may be technical, sympathy to the broader tech selloff, or unexplained.\n\n---\n\n**KOPN** (-13.53%, Small) → Unknown\n\n- No earnings release, analyst downgrade, FDA action, or company-specific announcement was identified as the direct October 16 catalyst. Kopin announced a CEO appearance at an October 20 AI panel regarding its MicroLED Neural I/O optical interconnect work with Fabric.AI (GlobeNewswire, Oct 16 08:45 ET) — this was a promotional event, not a negative catalyst.\n- Volume was 2.2× the 10-day average; the broader semiconductor rotation (SOXX -4.16%) and Kopin's reported expensive valuation (not profitable at operating-income level) likely contributed. No separate analyst PT change or rating cut was reported (MarketBeat, Oct 16–17).\n\n---\n\n**BLZE** (-12.90%, Small) → Unknown\n\n- No material ticker-specific news identified in the research window. Vanguard Capital Wealth Advisors purchased 100,500 shares in Q3 (Transcript Daily, Oct 16), a positive indicator that did not prevent the decline. The move may be technical or sympathy to broad cloud/SaaS selling.\n\n---\n\n**AEHR** (-12.74%, Small) → Unknown\n\n- No company-specific catalyst identified in the research window. A Simply Wall St report cited a 20.6% decline over seven days and estimated intrinsic fair value at $18.27 vs. the session close near $69, characterizing the shares as trading at a 279% premium — suggesting valuation compression risk (Simply Wall St, Oct 16). No earnings, analyst action, or corporate announcement was reported.\n\n---\n\n**WGS** (-12.61%, Small) → Unknown\n\n- No material ticker-specific news identified in the research window. Volume of 32,972 shares was 96% below the cited 919,692 average (MarketBeat, Oct 16), suggesting the move may be technical or driven by thin liquidity rather than a fundamental catalyst. GeneDx's last reported quarter was a beat (EPS $0.01 vs. -$0.19 consensus; revenue $114.44M vs. $111.01M).\n\n---\n\n### 📊 Sector Roll-up\n\n| Sector | Losers (count) | Avg 1D | Shared driver? |\n|--------|---------------:|-------:|----------------|\n| Technology | 28 | -8.7% | Yes — cybersecurity vuln, memory CXMT shock, AI-financing rotation |\n| Healthcare | 3 | -13.3% | No — GRAL (CEO sale/FDA), WGS (unknown), TEM (AI rotation mixed) |\n| Industrials | 1 | -8.0% | No — RKLB Neutron delay idiosyncratic |\n| Communication Services | 1 | -13.4% | No — TLK fiber restructuring idiosyncratic |\n\n---\n\n### 👁 Watch\n\n- **LRCX** earnings: October 21, 2026 (after 10 consecutive losing sessions, -24.0% cumulative); revenue, gross margin, and AI capex guidance are key focal points. Consensus "Moderate Buy," avg PT $364.59.\n- **NET** (Cloudflare) earnings: October 29, 2026; stock -17.9% in five sessions, trailing EPS -$0.58, operating margin -8.1%. Zacks downgraded to Strong Sell October 14.\n- **NOW** (ServiceNow) earnings: October 28, 2026; consensus EPS $1.02, revenue $4.09B. Q2 GAAP operating income fell 54.75% year over year to $162M; subscription gross margin declined to 73.5% from 80%. Failed $149.60 breakout entry on October 16.\n- **TWLO** earnings: October 29, 2026; stock off -15.7% over five sessions, RBC Sell/$165 on record. Q3 guidance EPS $0.45–$0.49.\n- **MSTR** MSCI list publication: November 11, 2026 (affected-company list); implementation after November 30 close. Estimated ~$2B of forced selling if excluded. Shareholder vote on preferred dividends October 28; Q3 results October 29.\n- **PANW/CRWD/FTNT/OKTA** — PANW firewall vulnerability: CISA KEV catalog listing is active; closing arguments in Arm/Qualcomm-related bench trial scheduled for the following week. Monitor for additional CVE disclosures or regulatory guidance that could extend the cybersecurity sector drawdown. PANW -18% and CRWD -17% over five sessions entering Monday.\n- **SKHY/memory sector** — CXMT DDR5 server-memory shipments expected before end of 2026; SK hynix HBM4 equipment-order delay adds capacity-roadmap uncertainty. ASML spare-parts price increase (~10%) effective from 2027 is a structural cost headwind.\n- **CRWV/NBIS** — CoreWeave 2031 bond yield and 5-year CDS remain elevated (yield ~14.5%, CDS >1,000 bps); any rating-agency action or refinancing announcement would move the neocloud complex. Morningstar maintains $150 NBIS estimate; Michael Burry disclosed leveraged June put options on NBIS.\n- **RKLB** — No Neutron launch date before Q2 2027; CEO Peter Beck said the company would announce a launch date after additional qualification work. Citizens PT $90, Goldman $67; ARK purchased shares post-decline. Track Q3 2026 results for backlog and Electron-cadence updates (backlog $2.36B at Q2 end).\n- **GRAL** — FDA final Galleri decision pending (advisory panel 6–4 effectiveness, 10–0 safety on September 23); no timeline disclosed. 2025 cash-burn guidance $310 million; stock up ~74% in the month prior to Friday, leaving valuation elevated at ~34× trailing sales.\n- **SMCI** — Fiscal 2027 revenue target $65–72B; fiscal Q4 revenue $11.1B (near low end of guidance); inventory $12.9B; negative FCF -$6.97B (TTM June 2026). New CFO Mark Long transition risk. Monitor SEC/regulatory probe developments.\n- **TLK** — Q3 earnings expected ~October 29; Phase 2 fiber-subsidiary spin-off and Rp2,290 dissenter buyback mechanics remain unresolved. Stock at new 52-week low $10.8