2026-06-24	daily	benzinga	complete	### 🗓 June 24, 2026 — Pre-Market Brief\n\n**One-liner**: Memory/semiconductor stocks attempt a rebound after Tuesday's KOSPI-led 10%+ crash dragged **MU** −13%, **SNDK** −14%, and **ARM** −10%, with all eyes on **MU** earnings after the bell today; **FDX** slides ~7% pre-market on margin compression despite a Q4 beat; **CBRS** drops ~10% after a Q1 double-beat; **IBM** extends its rally +6% on a JPMorgan upgrade to Overweight ($291 PT); and **WEN** surges ~20% on a new CFO hire amid 37% short interest.\n\n---\n\n### ⚡ Top Movers At a Glance\n\n| Ticker | Move | Cap | Catalyst | Theme |\n|--------|------|-----|----------|-------|\n| **MU** | −11.5% | Mega ($1.2T) | Q3 earnings today AMC; options pricing ±14% move | Memory / AI Compute ⚠️ |\n| **SNDK** | −14.1% | Mega ($337B) | Sympathy with global memory selloff; MU earnings catalyst | Memory / AI Compute ⚠️ |\n| **ARM** | −9.9% | Mega ($389B) | Broad semi profit-taking; no company-specific news | AI Compute ⚠️ |\n| **MRVL** | −8.7% | Mega ($246B) | Broad semi selloff; BofA raised PT $240→$365 Buy | AI Networking |\n| **WDC** | −8.1% | Mega ($253B) | Memory sector selloff sympathy | Memory |\n| **INTC** | −5.0% | Mega ($688B) | Broad semi selloff; BofA raised PT $135→$160 Buy | Foundry / AI Compute |\n| **AMD** | −4.5% | Mega ($900B) | Broad semi selloff; BofA raised PT to $560 Buy | AI Compute |\n| **IBM** | +6.1% | Mega ($237B) | JPMorgan upgrade Neutral→Overweight, PT $270→$291; Trump praise | Quantum / AI / Hybrid Cloud |\n| **T** | +3.7% | Mega ($154B) | Alphabet replacing VZ in DJIA effective June 29 (rotation) | Telecom |\n| **JNJ** | +3.6% | Mega ($550B) | Defensive rotation amid tech selloff | Healthcare / Defensive |\n| **MRK** | +3.4% | Mega ($285B) | EU approves Keytruda + Padcev combo for bladder cancer | Pharma |\n| **DELL** | +3.3% | Mega ($278B) | Morgan Stanley PT raised $448→$477 EW; Trump AI chip push | AI Infrastructure |\n| **PM** | +3.1% | Mega ($270B) | Defensive rotation; 3.29% dividend yield | Consumer Staples / Defensive |\n| **VZ** | +3.0% | Mega ($189B) | Being replaced in DJIA by GOOG effective June 29 | Telecom |\n| **ACN** | +6.2% | Large ($77B) | Bouncing from −24% 5D drop; $2B buyback increase to $7.5B total FY26 | IT Services |\n| **AXON** | +5.8% | Large ($35B) | Software/defensive rotation within tech | Public Safety Tech |\n| **NOW** | +4.2% | Large ($96B) | Software rebound; beaten-down SaaS names bid | SaaS |\n| **GEHC** | +4.9% | Large ($28B) | RBC Capital initiates Outperform, PT $80 | Healthcare Equipment |\n| **FDX** | −6.7% pre | Large | Q4 rev $25B beat ($24B est), adj EPS $6.31 beat ($5.96); FedEx Express margin shrank 7.7% vs 8.4% YoY | Logistics |\n| **CBRS** | −10% pre | Large ($50B) | Q1 adj loss −$0.04 beat (−$0.16 est); rev $193.4M beat ($181.6M est); $20B+ OpenAI deal announced | AI Compute |\n| **CRDO** | −9.8% | Large ($56B) | Broad semi selloff; BofA raised PT $252→$340 Buy | AI Networking |\n| **SMCI** | −6.7% | Large ($22B) | Semi selloff; Odine partnership; Mizuho PT $44 Neutral | AI Servers |\n| **BLZE** | +42.9% | Small ($487M) | 5-yr $335M storage deal with CoreWeave; Craig-Hallum upgrade Hold→Buy PT $16 | Cloud Storage / AI Infra |\n| **ARQQ** | +37.8% | Small ($364M) | Quantum sector momentum; no specific catalyst identified | Quantum Computing |\n| **WEN** | +18.5% pre | Mid ($1.1B) | New CFO Steve Cirulis appointed; 37% short float | Restaurants / Short Squeeze |\n| **FCEL** | +14.7% pre | Mid ($1.4B) | 380 MW data center power deal with Fit Energy | Clean Energy / Data Center Power |\n| **MAAS** | +16.8% | Mid ($6.2B) | Continued momentum; no specific catalyst identified | Idiosyncratic |\n| **QNT** | +12.5% | Mid ($2.4B) | Trump signed two quantum computing executive orders | Quantum Computing |\n| **INFQ** | +12.1% | Mid ($2.3B) | Launched America's Quantum Space Initiative; Trump quantum EOs | Quantum Computing |\n| **BBBY** | +13.7% | Small ($431M) | Wedbush raised PT $8→$10 Outperform | Retail / Turnaround |\n| **HQ** | −18.7% | Small ($1.7B) | Profit-taking after +74% 5D run; no specific catalyst | Quantum Computing |\n| **HYLN** | −19.2% | Small ($1.1B) | Continued selloff; no specific catalyst identified | EV / Clean Transport |\n| **DHI** | −11% pre | Large ($44B) | No specific catalyst; housing sector pressure | Homebuilding |\n| **VRNS** | +6.9% | Small ($4.1B) | Bloomberg: Varonis weighing sale after takeover interest; Needham PT raised $36→$40 Buy | Cybersecurity / M&A |\n\n---\n\n### 🧭 Narrative Threads\n\n**Semiconductor / Memory Selloff & MU Earnings** `AI Compute / Memory` → ongoing\n\nThe dominant story: Tuesday's ~10% KOSPI crash — triggered by reports SK Hynix may be shifting DRAM capacity away from HBM — cascaded into U.S. memory and semiconductor names. **MU** fell 13.2% to $1,052, **SNDK** −14.1%, **MRVL** −8.7%, **ARM** −9.9%, **AMAT** −6.4%, **INTC** −5.0%, **AMD** −4.5%. All are bouncing 2–5% in Wednesday pre-market ahead of **MU** Q3 earnings after the close.\n\n- **MU** Q3 consensus: EPS $20.76 (vs $1.91 YoY), revenue $35.75B (vs $9.3B YoY — 284% growth). Has beaten both lines for 8 consecutive quarters but closed lower post-earnings in 6 of 8.\n- Options imply ~14% move in either direction for MU this week.\n- Freedom Capital Markets' Jay Woods: "HBM is the growth story, but guidance is the stock story." Recommends taking profits; re-entry target $955 (20-day MA).\n- BofA Securities (Vivek Arya): raised **MU** PT $950→$1,500, Buy; raised total semi industry sales forecast to $2.7T by 2030; HBM market to ~$246B by 2030 (from ~$35B in 2025).\n- Futurum Equities (Rolf Bulk): **MU** PT $1,500, Buy. CEO Daniel Newman: "The era of memory as a commodity is over. Memory is strategic."\n- BofA also raised PTs on **AMAT** ($540→$720), **LRCX** ($330→$480), **MRVL** ($240→$365), **CRDO** ($252→$340), **ARM** ($335→$460 Neutral), **INTC** ($135→$160), **ALAB** ($240→$450 Neutral).\n- Wedbush's Dan Ives called the selloff a "gut check moment," not a demand signal; AI revolution "in the 3rd inning."\n- SOX index has recorded 9 single-day gains of 5%+ in the last 60 sessions — a volatility pattern last exceeded during the dot-com bust.\n- **SK Hynix** plans Nasdaq ADR listing July 10; targeting up to ₩45.45T ($29.4B) raise — would surpass **BABA**'s $21.8B 2014 record ADR listing.\n- **QCOM** agreed to acquire Modular Inc (financial terms undisclosed; expects to issue up to 19.2M shares); reportedly in talks with ByteDance for custom chip design services. BofA maintains Underperform, PT $165→$195.\n- Netherlands lobbying US to drop chip export curbs targeting **ASML** sales (Bloomberg).\n- Next catalyst: **MU** earnings tonight after close ⚠️; **SMCI** earnings est. Aug. 4 ⚠️\n\n**IBM Quantum & AI Upgrade** `Quantum / AI` → new\n\n**IBM** +6.1% on a JPMorgan upgrade and Trump White House praise, outperforming a weak tape (Nasdaq −2.2% Tuesday).\n\n- JPMorgan (Brian Essex): upgraded **IBM** Neutral→Overweight, PT $270→$291, citing AI and software strategy confidence.\n- Morgan Stanley (Erik Woodring): maintained Equal-Weight, PT $225→$267.\n- Citigroup: Buy, PT $375. Barclays: Overweight, PT $350. Wedbush: Outperform, PT $320.\n- Trump praised CEO Arvind Krishna at White House quantum event; admitted regret selling IBM stock upon taking office: "That was not a good move."\n- Commerce Dept awarding ~$1B to IBM for Anderon quantum wafer foundry (IBM matching with own $1B). IBM announced $10B quantum investment over 5 years.\n- IBM joined OpenAI's Daybreak Cyber Partner Program; launched AI-powered application security service (Project Lightwell, $5B initiative).\n- Q2 earnings expected July 22: EPS est $3.00 (vs $2.80 YoY), revenue est $17.85B (vs $16.98B YoY) ⚠️\n\n**Quantum Computing Sector** `Quantum` → new\n\nTrump signed two executive orders Monday accelerating quantum computing development and post-quantum cryptography migration.\n\n- **QNT** (Quantinuum) +12.5%, **INFQ** (Infleqtion) +12.1%, **ARQQ** +37.8% on quantum momentum.\n- **HQ** (Horizon Quantum) −18.7% after +74% 5D run (profit-taking).\n- Executive orders target research-capable quantum computer by 2028; agencies to adopt quantum-resistant encryption by 2030–2031.\n- Commerce Dept ~$2B quantum initiative includes investments in D-Wave, IonQ, Rigetti, Infleqtion.\n- **INFQ** launched America's Quantum Space Initiative with Voyager Technologies and University of Colorado Boulder.\n\n**FedEx Q4: Beat But Margin Compression** `Logistics` → new\n\n**FDX** −6.7% pre-market despite beating Q4 estimates on both lines.\n\n- Q4 revenue $25.0B vs $24.04B est; adj EPS $6.31 vs $5.96 est.\n- Federal Express segment operating margin shrank to 7.7% from 8.4% YoY.\n- International export package yields +10%; Asia-Europe lane double-digit export revenue growth.\n- CY2026 guidance: adj EPS $16.90–$18.10 (~20% adj EPS growth); capex at historic low 4% of revenue.\n- **FDXF** (FedEx Freight Holdings): Jefferies initiates Buy, PT $200.\n\n**Cerebras Systems Q1: Beat & Sell** `AI Compute` → new\n\n**CBRS** −10% pre-market after Q1 results exceeded expectations on both lines.\n\n- Q1 adj loss −$0.04/share vs −$0.16 est; revenue $193.4M vs $181.6M est (+94% YoY).\n- Hardware revenue +59%, cloud/services revenue +178%. Gross margin 45%.\n- Announced $20B+ multi-year deal with OpenAI (750 MW inference compute) and AWS partnership.\n- Q2 revenue guide $194M vs $174M est; FY revenue guide $855M–$865M vs $824M est.\n- Needham: reiterates Buy, PT $300.\n- CEO Feldman: biggest AI constraint is now data center availability, not chip supply or demand.\n\n**Defensive Rotation & Software Rebound** `Macro / Sector Rotation` → ongoing\n\nTuesday's session was a textbook defensive rotation: Consumer Staples (XLP +1.7%), Real Estate (XLRE +1.4%), Healthcare (XLV +0.9%) led. Software rallied even as semis bled — **NOW** +4.2%, Cloudflare +5%, Rubrik +6%.\n\n- Dow +0.2% vs Nasdaq 100 −2.4% on Tuesday; Dow composition (lighter chip exposure) cushioned.\n- **GOOG** to replace **VZ** in DJIA effective June 29.\n- Advancing stocks outnumbered decliners 1.8-to-1 on Tuesday despite headline index drops.\n- Jeremy Siegel: sharp oil decline has "drastically shifted the monetary policy conversation"; finds it "very difficult to envision the Fed raising rates this year."\n- S&P Global mfg PMI 55.7 (May 55.1, est 54.8) — highest since May 2022. Services PMI 51.3 (May 50.7, est 51).\n- PCE inflation print due Thursday ⚠️; Fed stress test results today at 4:00 PM ET ⚠️\n\n**Oil / Iran / Gas Prices** `Commodities / Geopolitics` → ongoing\n\nTrump ordered DOJ probe into oil companies for not cutting pump prices fast enough as crude drops.\n\n- WTI ~$72/bbl (−1.7%); Brent ~$75.6 (−1.5%). Gas national avg $3.928/gal (down from $4.515 a month ago; up from $3.224 YoY).\n- Paul Krugman: "rockets and feathers" — gas rises like rockets, falls like feathers. Relief won't be quick.\n- IAEA chief Grossi confirmed inspections of Iran nuclear sites will happen under US deal; Iran officials deny new IAEA agreement.\n- Senate passed Iran War Powers resolution 50-48.\n- Trump claims Iran informed US: no tolls/charges on Strait of Hormuz shipping.\n- SPR expected to hit 43-year low per upcoming DOE data (~47% of 714M bbl capacity).\n\n**Housing Bill & Macro** `Macro / Real Estate` → new\n\nTrump to sign 21st Century ROAD to Housing Act today — most significant federal housing legislation in 30+ years.\n\n- Limits institutional investors' ability to buy single-family homes. 45+ provisions.\n- Passed Senate 85-5.\n- NAHB: regulations add avg $131,734 (26.4%) to new single-family home cost.\n- Housing starts fell 15.4% MoM in May to 1.18M annualized (lowest since May 2020).\n- May new home sales data due today 10:00 AM ET ⚠️\n\n**Blaze Storage Deal** `Cloud Storage / AI Infrastructure` → new\n\n**BLZE** +42.9% (38.2× avg volume) after signing a 5-year, $335M multi-exabyte storage deal with CoreWeave.\n\n- Backblaze will provide HDD-based storage supporting CoreWeave's managed storage tiers for AI workloads.\n- Craig-Hallum upgraded Hold→Buy, PT $6.50→$16. Needham: Buy, PT $8.50→$14.\n- CoreWeave issued warrants to purchase up to ~4.2M BLZE shares at $7.60 exercise price.\n\n**Private Credit & AI Data Center Risk** `AI Infrastructure / Financial Risk` → developing\n\nNYU's Aswath Damodaran warned private credit lenders funding AI data centers are "setting itself up for a beating."\n\n- "Who are these lunatics who are lending money to the data centres?"\n- **APO** and **BX** announced $35B Anthropic AI computing capacity expansion; BX plans $30B AI data centers in Japan.\n- Goldman Sachs raised hyperscaler capex forecast to $5.3T between 2025–2030.\n\n**📡 Undercurrents** *(developing, not yet dominant)*\n- Analysts now expect S&P 500 earnings to grow ~24% annually over next 5 years — roughly double the historical norm and higher than dot-com era projections (Acquirers Funds' Tobias Carlisle calls this "a sentiment indicator, not a forecast").\n- BCA Research's Peter Berezin: "In the initial phase of the dotcom bust, tech stocks crashed while the rest of the market went up. We may be entering a similar phase now."\n- Howard Lutnick's Commerce Dept reportedly investigating Chinese state-subsidized robotics imports; potential crackdown on humanoid robots signaled ("the arms race that is coming").\n- **VRNS** (Varonis Systems) +6.9% on 10.2× avg volume after Bloomberg reported the cybersecurity firm is weighing a sale after takeover interest.\n- Quarter-end rebalancing expected to drive >$100B institutional equity selling into bonds (Arora Report analysis).\n- Corporate trend shifting from "token max" to "token min" as AI model costs rise; Chinese open-source models running at ~⅛ the cost of top US models for certain tasks.\n\n---\n\n### 📰 Channel Pulse\n\n| Channel | Material fact |\n|---------|--------------|\n| Earnings | **FDX** Q4: rev $25.0B beat $24.04B est, adj EPS $6.31 beat $5.96; FedEx Express margin compressed to 7.7% from 8.4% YoY. **CBRS** Q1: loss −$0.04 beat −$0.16 est, rev $193.4M beat $181.6M; announced $20B+ OpenAI deal. **MU** reports after close today: EPS est $20.76, rev est $35.75B. |\n| Macro | S&P Global US mfg PMI 55.7 in June (highest since May 2022); services PMI 51.3. Trump to sign 21st Century ROAD to Housing Act today. Fed stress test results due 4:00 PM ET. PCE inflation Thursday. |\n| Tech | **QCOM** agreed to acquire Modular Inc; in talks with ByteDance for custom chip design. **ASML**: Netherlands lobbying US to drop chip export curbs. **SK Hynix** targets $29.4B Nasdaq ADR listing July 10. |\n| Commodities | WTI crude ~$72/bbl (−1.7%); Trump ordered DOJ probe into oil companies for slow gas price cuts. Gold $4,079 (−0.75%). Gold ETFs saw first weekly inflow (+5.1 tonnes) since mid-April after $7.6B outflows. |\n| Equities | Michael Burry flagged **PLTR** "new lower low" after "low volume top" — shares near 52-week lows, down ~30% YTD. Coatue's Philippe Laffont argues "Mag 7" is now "Mag 11-12" including BRK, LLY, TSM, AVGO. |\n| Geopolitics | IAEA chief confirmed Iran nuclear inspections "will happen" under US-Iran deal. Senate passed Iran War Powers resolution 50-48. Trump: no Hormuz tolls. |\n\n---\n\n### 👁 On The Radar\n\n- **MU** (Micron) Q3 earnings after market close today, June 24. Consensus EPS $20.76, rev $35.75B. Options imply ±14% move. Stock has fallen post-earnings in 6 of last 8 reports despite beating every time. ⚠️\n- **SK Hynix** Nasdaq ADR listing targeted for July 10; up to ₩45.45T ($29.4B) raise — would be largest ADR listing in history.\n- **SMCI** earnings estimated Aug. 4: EPS est $0.69 (vs $0.41 YoY), rev est $11.73B (vs $5.76B YoY). ⚠️\n- **ARM** Q1 FY2027 earnings expected ~July 29: EPS est $0.36, rev est $1.27B. ⚠️\n- **IBM** Q2 earnings expected July 22: EPS est $3.00, rev est $17.85B. ⚠️\n- Fed annual bank stress test results today at 4:00 PM ET.\n- May new home sales data today at 10:00 AM ET.\n- PCE inflation print (Fed's preferred gauge) due Thursday, June 26. ⚠️\n- **GOOG** replaces **VZ** in Dow Jones Industrial Average effective prior to open Monday, June 29.\n- Quarter-end institutional rebalancing: estimated >$100B equity-to-bond rotation expected through week-end.\n- USMCA renegotiation deadline July 1; Greer signaled auto/steel tariffs on Mexico to remain.\n- **GameStop** board removed proposed CEO Performance Award from proxy to focus on proposed eBay acquisition.\n\n---\n\n### 🚫 What to Ignore Today\n\n- Micro/nano-cap pre-market movers (FRTT +60%, PLSM +152%, numerous sub-$100M names in pre-market mover lists) — these are low-liquidity noise with no institutional relevance.\n- Tesla PR strategy commentary (Gary Black's thread on TSLA's lack of PR) — opinion-driven, no new financial data; TSLA's fundamental story unchanged.\n- Broad "industry comparison" articles (NVDA vs peers, MRVL vs peers, TSLA vs peers, MSFT vs peers) — these are auto-generated Benzinga content repeating publicly available ratio data with no new analyst insight.	\N	{"calls": [{"step": "step4_synthesis", "model": "claude-opus-4-6", "api_model": "claude-opus-4-6", "input_tokens": 523405, "output_tokens": 5768, "pricing_model": "claude-opus-4-6", "input_cost_usd": 2.617025, "total_cost_usd": 2.761225, "output_cost_usd": 0.1442, "cache_read_cost_usd": 0.0, "cache_write_cost_usd": 0.0, "cache_read_input_tokens": 0, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 0}], "call_count": 1, "started_at": "2026-06-24T12:26:13.151882+00:00", "completed_at": "2026-06-24T12:29:00.949804+00:00", "current_step": null, "pricing_note": "Token counts from Anthropic Messages API response.usage. USD estimated from published claude.com/api pricing (input, 5m cache write, cache read, output) — Anthropic does not return dollar amounts on the response.", "total_cost_usd": 2.7612, "total_input_tokens": 523405, "total_output_tokens": 5768, "total_cache_read_input_tokens": 0, "total_cache_creation_input