2026-07-02	daily	benzinga	complete	### 🗓 July 2, 2026 — Pre-Market Brief\n\n**One-liner**: META surged ~10% on a Bloomberg report it is building a cloud business to sell excess AI compute ("Meta Compute"), triggering a violent rotation—software/SaaS and financials rallied while semiconductors cratered (SMH −4.5%), with NBIS −16%, CRWV −13%, GLW −13%, MU −8%, AMAT −11%; Dow hit a new record above 52,600 as Fed Chair Warsh at Sintra called inflation "too high" but declined to signal a July hike.\n\n---\n\n### ⚡ Top Movers At a Glance\n\n| Ticker | Move | Cap | Catalyst | Theme |\n|--------|------|-----|----------|-------|\n| **META** | +9.5% | Mega | Bloomberg: building "Meta Compute" cloud to sell excess AI capacity | AI Cloud / Hyperscaler |\n| **AMAT** | −11.1% | Mega | Profit-taking; broad semi selloff on Meta cloud news | Semis / AI Infra |\n| **MU** | −8.4% | Mega | Semi rotation out; CEO Mehrotra reiterated supply tight beyond 2027 | Memory / AI Infra |\n| **SNDK** | −8.5% | Mega | Profit-taking after +857% YTD; BofA raised PT to $2,500 Buy | Memory / AI Infra |\n| **INTC** | −7.8% | Mega | Broad semi selloff; +254% YTD profit-taking | Foundry / Semis |\n| **MRVL** | −5.9% | Mega | Semi rotation; Cramer named it Q2 winner | Networking / AI Infra |\n| **AMD** | −5.3% | Mega | Semi rotation; Wells Fargo raised PT to $615 OW | AI Compute |\n| **GLW** | −13.0% | Mega | Meta cloud news threatens optical infrastructure premium | Optical / AI Infra |\n| **APP** | +9.8% | Mega | Software rotation beneficiary; 5D +22% | Ad-Tech / SaaS |\n| **PLTR** | +8.3% | Mega | Nvidia sovereign AI deal + Surf Air expansion; software rotation | AI Software / Defense |\n| **NOW** | +6.6% | Mega | Guggenheim upgrade to Buy PT $125; Accenture AI launch | SaaS |\n| **SHOP** | +6.4% | Mega | Software rotation | E-commerce / SaaS |\n| **CRM** | +3.9% | Mega | Guggenheim upgrade to Buy PT $228 | SaaS |\n| **MSFT** | +3.7% | Mega | Software rotation; planned layoffs (thousands) reported | Cloud / SaaS |\n| **PDD** | +8.0% | Mega | No specific catalyst identified | China E-commerce |\n| **IBKR** | +6.7% | Mega | June DARTs +53% YoY; launched Korean equities via Nextrade | Brokerage |\n| **SCHW** | +4.2% | Mega | Securities trading rally day | Brokerage |\n| **NBIS** | −15.7% | Large | Meta cloud news; direct competitive threat to neocloud model | Neocloud / AI Infra |\n| **CRWV** | −12.7%† | Large | Meta cloud news threatens GPU cloud business model | Neocloud / AI Infra |\n| **RDDT** | +13.2% | Large | Exclusive: CFO says 500M weekly users "early innings"; AI data deals | Social / AI Data |\n| **GH** | +13.6% | Large | Continued momentum; BTIG raised PT to $190 Buy | Genomics / Diagnostics |\n| **COIN** | +9.9% | Large | Robinhood Chain mainnet launch; crypto bounce; BTC +2.9% | Crypto |\n| **MSTR** | +8.4% | Large | BTC bounce to ~$59.9K; Saylor defends at BTC Prague | Bitcoin Proxy |\n| **HOOD** | +8.3% | Large | Robinhood Chain public mainnet; London event; June data strong | Fintech / Crypto |\n| **MDB** | +7.1% | Large | Software rotation; 5D +18.9% | Database / SaaS |\n| **RBLX** | +7.0% | Large | Software rotation; 5D +21.4% | Gaming / Metaverse |\n| **SE** | +7.6% | Large | No specific catalyst identified | SE Asia E-commerce |\n| **ELV** | +6.6% | Large | Sector strength; Medicare GLP-1 Bridge program launches July 1 | Managed Care |\n| **ALAB** | −7.8% | Large | Semi selloff; still +11.3% 5D | AI Interconnect |\n| **PRGS** | +15.0% | Small | Q2 adj. EPS $1.62 vs $1.49 est; rev $253.5M vs $242.7M est; raised FY guide | Enterprise Software |\n| **FCEL** | −12.0% | Small | Pullback after +67% weekly rally on Fit Energy deal + EXIM $49M financing | Clean Energy |\n| **FUBO** | +11.4% | Small | Adeia patent suit news; continued momentum | Streaming |\n| **SOC** | +34.7% | Small | Priced $100M stock at $3.08 + $300M convertible notes; Roth MNT Buy PT $15 | Offshore Energy |\n| **RGC** | +31.6% | Small | No specific catalyst; 8.0× avg volume | Biotech / Idiosyncratic |\n| **AMPL** | +14.9% | Small | Raymond James initiates Strong Buy PT $10 | Analytics / SaaS |\n| **FRHC** | +18.7% | Small | No specific catalyst identified | Fintech / EM Brokerage |\n\n*†CRWV not in ticker universe but central to the META narrative.*\n\n---\n\n### 🧭 Narrative Threads\n\n**Meta Compute Fractures the AI Trade** `AI Cloud / AI Infra` → new\n\n**META** +9.5% on 2.5× average volume after Bloomberg reported the company is developing "Meta Compute"—an initiative to sell excess AI computing capacity to external developers, potentially offering both hosted model access (like AWS Bedrock) and raw GPU capacity (like CoreWeave). The initiative is led by Santosh Janardhan, Daniel Gross, and Dina Powell McCormick. Meta declined to comment; plans could change.\n\n- The report triggered the session's sharpest rotation: software (**IGV** +4.2%, **FDN** +2.6%) surged while semiconductors (**SMH** −4.5%) and neocloud names cratered.\n- **NBIS** −15.7%: directly threatened because Nebius has a multi-year capacity agreement with Meta worth up to $27B; Meta entering commercial cloud could make it both customer and competitor.\n- **CRWV** −12.7%: Meta selling raw compute directly challenges CoreWeave's core GPU-as-a-service business.\n- **GLW** −13.0%: Corning's AI premium (including $6B Meta connectivity deal) repriced as investors questioned whether hyperscaler capex flowing to third parties would slow.\n- Samantha LaDuc (LaDuc Trading) warned the move creates a "butterfly effect" across the entire AI buildout stack—optics, photonics, semis, energy, industrials.\n- **MAGS** (Roundhill Magnificent Seven ETF) +2.8%, validating a rotation back into cash-rich mega-caps from specialized infra plays.\n\n---\n\n**Semiconductor Profit-Taking / Rotation** `Memory / Semis` → ongoing\n\nThe semi selloff was the mirror image of the software rally. After Q2 saw AMD, MU, INTC add ~$2T in combined market cap, Wednesday's session marked aggressive profit-taking across the stack.\n\n- **MU** −8.4%: CEO Mehrotra reiterated AI memory demand exceeded customer expectations; supply constrained beyond 2027; ~$200B global capex commitment. BofA raised **SNDK** PT to $2,500 (Buy), modeling NAND supply/demand imbalance through 2027.\n- **SNDK** −8.5%: BofA models June-Q rev $9.1B and EPS $37.01 vs Street $8.35B/$34.26; stock up 857% YTD.\n- **AMAT** −11.1%: after best month since 1975 (+54% in June); Susquehanna PT $900. Michael Burry disclosed short at $729.40.\n- **INTC** −7.8%: still +254% YTD; Burry also shorted INTC. Cramer called INTC his "favorite stock" and a "national treasure."\n- **AMD** −5.3%: Wells Fargo raised PT to $615 (OW); Cantor to $700 (OW); UBS to $670 (Buy). Still trading at ~194× trailing P/E.\n- **ARM** −3.4%: broader semi weakness.\n- **KLA** −10.1%, **SMTC** −10.7%, **TER** −9%+: chip equipment names hit hardest.\n- New memory ETFs launched: **KMEM** (Kurv Memory Select ETF) and **DISK** (Tema Memory ETF) both targeting DRAM/NAND/HBM exposure.\n- **DRAM** ETF crossed $25B AUM, +173% since April 2 listing—fastest ETF ever to hit $10B, $15B, $20B, $25B milestones.\n\n---\n\n**SaaS / Software Rebound** `SaaS` → new\n\nSoftware names rallied sharply as capital rotated out of semis, with **IGV** +4.2%—the best-performing industry ETF of the day.\n\n- **CRM** +3.9%: Guggenheim upgraded to Buy, PT $228; analyst says stock at 3.7× recurring revenue and 11× EV/FCF is "too harsh" even with AI risk.\n- **NOW** +6.6%: Guggenheim upgraded to Buy, PT $125; Accenture AI services launch adds to narrative.\n- **MDB** +7.1%, **OKTA** +3.5%, **SNOW** +2.9%, **DDOG** +1.4%: broad SaaS strength.\n- **PRGS** +15.0%: Q2 adj. EPS $1.62 beat $1.49; rev $253.5M beat $242.7M; raised FY EPS guide to $6.09–$6.21 (from $5.91–$6.03) vs $5.98 est; raised FY rev guide to $990M–$1.002B.\n- **AMPL** +14.9% on 3.5× vol: Raymond James initiated Strong Buy, PT $10.\n\n---\n\n**Macro: Warsh at Sintra, Soft Data** `Macro` → ongoing\n\nFed Chair Warsh at ECB Sintra: "prices are too high"; committed to 2% target; declined to signal July action; said committee should have a "good family fight" before deciding. Tapped ex-BoE Chief Mervyn King for new Fed communications task force.\n\n- CME FedWatch: 69.5% odds of hold at July 29 meeting (vs ~63% prior); ~77% chance of at least one hike by September; >90% by October.\n- ADP June: +98K jobs vs +113K est (down from 122K in May).\n- ISM Manufacturing PMI: 53.3 vs 53.8 est; prices paid plunged to 73.0 from 82.1 (vs 77.7 est).\n- S&P Global Manufacturing PMI revised lower: 53.9 vs 55.7 prelim.\n- Construction spending May: +0.1% as expected.\n- Crude oil inventories: −3.775M bbl draw vs −2.9M est.\n- Cleveland Fed's Hammack warned AI spending could keep inflation elevated: "these hyperscalers will pay almost any price."\n- 10Y yield eased to 4.47% after Warsh spoke; 2Y slipped to ~4.14%.\n- Gold +1.9% to ~$4,085/oz; silver +3.1%.\n- WTI crude −2.0% to $68.12; Brent −2.5% to $71.12; both below pre-Iran-war levels.\n- Trump said Iran talks "going well"; Qatar reported "positive progress" in indirect Doha meetings. Oil sold off on reduced geopolitical premium.\n- **USMCA**: U.S. declined to renew in current form; annual reviews continue; next bilateral with Mexico week of July 20.\n\n---\n\n**Crypto Bounce / Robinhood Expansion** `Crypto / Fintech` → ongoing\n\nBitcoin bounced +2.9% to ~$59,900 after closing June −20% (worst month since June 2022). ETFs bled $4.5B in June—record monthly outflow. BTC supply in loss (10.45M) exceeded supply in profit (9.6M) for first time this cycle—historically a bottoming signal.\n\n- **COIN** +9.9%: Cantor said BTC bear market may be entering final stretch.\n- **MSTR** +8.4%: Michael Saylor at BTC Prague said AI capital rotation (~$500B) is pulling money from BTC temporarily; expects Q3–Q4 normalization. TD Cowen cut PT to $260 (Buy), lowered BTC year-end forecast to $100K from $140K.\n- **HOOD** +8.3%: launched Robinhood Chain public mainnet; tokenized stock trading live in 120+ countries; Robinhood Earn yields 7% on USDG stablecoin; June equity notional volumes ~$343B, options ~274M contracts, crypto ~$14B. Deutsche Bank raised PT to $113 (Buy).\n- JD Vance financial disclosure: holds $250K–$500K in Bitcoin via Coinbase account.\n- Trump crypto disclosures: >$1B crypto income in 2025 ($520M from World Liberty Financial tokens, $635M from TRUMP memecoin royalties).\n\n---\n\n**Earnings: GIS Beat, NKE Cautious, MSM Strong, FDS Flat Guide** `Earnings` → ongoing\n\n- **GIS** +8.9%: Q4 adj. EPS $0.95 vs $0.80 est; rev $4.610B vs $4.595B est. GM expanded 240bp to 34.8%. FY27 guide: organic sales −1.5% to +0.5%; adj. EPS $3.00–$3.20 vs $3.13 est. Mgmt said consumers will "remain under pressure" in FY27.\n- **NKE** −3%: Q4 rev $10.97B beat $10.86B est; adj. EPS $0.20 beat $0.13 est, but included $986M one-time tariff recovery. Sportswear and Jordan Streetwear both down double digits. Guided rev decline low-to-mid-single digits next quarters. CFO Friend departing. Wells Fargo cut PT to $40 (EW); Barclays cut to $52 (OW); BofA cut to $47 (Neutral).\n- **MSM** +6.4%: Q3 adj. EPS $1.43 vs $1.26 est; rev $1.047B vs $1.031B est (+7.8% YoY). Raised FY free cash flow conversion to ~95%. Tungsten up >500%, more price hikes planned.\n- **FDS** −1.7%: Q3 adj. EPS $4.53 vs $4.46 est; rev $622.9M vs $618.2M est. Op margin declined to 34% from 36.8% YoY. Reaffirmed FY guide.\n- **FC** (after-hours −24%): Q3 EPS $0.27 missed $0.28 est; rev $67.8M missed $68.3M est. Lowered FY rev guide to $260M–$267M from $265M–$275M.\n- **GBX**: Q3 EPS $0.60 missed $0.62 est; rev $576.5M missed $612.7M est. Narrowed FY EPS guide to $3.00–$3.15 (from $3.00–$3.50).\n- **STZ** (after-hours +3.2%): Q1 adj. EPS $3.43 beat $3.21 est; raised FY27 earnings guidance. ⚠️\n\n---\n\n**TSLA Deliveries / Optimus Production** `EV / Robotics` → ongoing\n\n**TSLA** flat (−0.45% AH). Musk said Optimus robot production will be "extremely slow at first" as everything is new. Fremont Model S/X line converted to Optimus assembly. VP Lars Moravy confirmed first production line landed, began installation. Pilot production expected this summer; high-volume not until ~summer 2027; long-term target 10M units/year.\n\n- **Tesla Q2 deliveries due Thursday July 3**. Gary Black (Future Fund) projects ~410K units vs consensus ~406K. Ross Gerber says "solid quarter" driven by gas price spike from Iran war. ⚠️\n- Burry disclosed short at $416.22.\n- California passed $135M EV incentive program excluding Tesla (HQ moved to TX); benefits Rivian, Lucid.\n- NIO June deliveries: 40,597 (+62.9% YoY); Q2: 107,658 (+49.4%). XPeng June: 40,126 (+16%). Li Auto June: 30,895 (−14.8%).\n\n---\n\n**Defense / Aerospace Contracts** `Defense` → ongoing\n\n- **AVAV** +3.2% AH: won $500M firm-fixed-price DoD counter-drone contract through June 2029.\n- **LEU** (Centrus Energy): signed $1.07B total contract (incl. options) with DOE for HALEU enrichment; 1,000 construction jobs in Ohio, 430 in Tennessee.\n- Wedbush initiated **KTOS** Outperform PT $85, calling it "misunderstood" as merchant arms supplier; hypersonics revenue expected to reach ~$1B.\n- Germany seeking U.S. co-production deals for Tomahawk, Patriot missiles ahead of NATO summit (FT report).\n\n---\n\n**Reddit's AI Data Moat** `Social / AI Data` → ongoing\n\n**RDDT** +13.2% on 1.8× volume. Benzinga exclusive: CFO Drew Vollero said Reddit has 500M global weekly users but is "early innings"; 200M U.S. weekly users. 17% YoY DAU growth in Q1. Ad revenue +74% YoY in Q1. 7 consecutive quarters of 60%+ revenue growth.\n\n- Vollero called Reddit data "oil for the modern internet"—25B+ posts/comments; most-cited source for LLMs.\n- Google and OpenAI content licensing deals described as "mutually beneficial."\n- Machine translation now in 35+ languages; focus on converting weekly to daily users.\n\n---\n\n**📡 Undercurrents** *(developing, not yet dominant)*\n- **Apple lobbying to buy Chinese-ma