2026-07-04	daily	benzinga	complete	### 🗓 July 4, 2026 — Pre-Market Brief\n\n**One-liner**: Semiconductor stocks suffered their worst 2-day SOXX drop in over a year (-12.9%) as rotation into Dow industrials and defensives drove a record Dow close above 52,900; June nonfarm payrolls missed at 57K vs 110K est, collapsing July rate-hike odds to ~20%; **TSLA** fell 7.5% despite a massive Q2 delivery beat of 480,126 vs 406K est; **META** gave back its 9% cloud-business rally; and **MU**, **SNDK**, and chip-equipment names led the selloff.\n\n---\n\n### ⚡ Top Movers At a Glance\n\n| Ticker | Move | Cap | Catalyst | Theme |\n|--------|------|-----|----------|-------|\n| TSLA | -7.5% | Mega | Q2 deliveries 480,126 vs 406K est; sell-the-news | EV / Autonomy |\n| META | -4.9% | Mega | Gave back Wed cloud-compute rally; Zuckerberg: AI bets "haven't come to fruition yet" | AI Cloud |\n| AAPL | +4.8% | Mega | BofA Buy/$380 PT; services growth; safe-haven rotation | Consumer Tech |\n| MU | -5.5% | Mega | Profit-taking; Burry short thesis; SOXX selloff | Memory / AI Compute |\n| SNDK | -14.1% | Mega | Chip rotation; BofA raises PT to $2,500 despite selloff | Memory |\n| GLW | -13.2% Wed | Mega | Meta cloud plan sparked neocloud/optics selloff | AI Infra / Photonics |\n| MRVL | +1.9% | Mega | Bouncing from Wed selloff; Cramer/Huang backing | AI Networking |\n| AMAT | +1.3% | Mega | Burry short at $729; Cantor PT raised to $850 | Semicap Equipment |\n| BE | +2.6% | Large | Brookfield financing framework raised $5B→$25B | AI Power / Clean Energy |\n| ASTS | +2.8% | Large | +19% 5D; next 3 BlueBirds launch Aug; FCC modernization | Satellite Broadband |\n| RIVN | +8.4% | Large | Q2 deliveries 12,194 beat 9-11K guide; raised FY to 65-70K | EV |\n| CRWD | +0.4% | Large | 4-for-1 split-adjusted trading began; near 52-wk high | Cybersecurity |\n| RKLB | +1.2% | Large | +18.8% 5D on $8B Iridium acquisition; Nasdaq-100 catalyst Jul 7 | Space / Launch |\n| PLTR | +5.2% | Large | DA Davidson upgrade to Buy/$175; Karp bashes token model | Enterprise AI |\n| HOOD | +9.0% | Large | Robinhood Chain mainnet; tokenized stocks 120 countries; Mizuho PT $130 | Fintech / Crypto |\n| BB | -11.5% | Mid | Profit-taking after Q1 beat drove +20% spike; RSI was 79 | Auto Software |\n| CCXI | +5.6% | Small | +37.8% 5D; Agility Robotics merger details | SPAC / Robotics |\n| FCEL | +2.1% | Mid | +17% 5D; profit-taking after 67% weekly rally on data center deals | Fuel Cells / AI Power |\n| CAR | +3.4% | Mid | $2B revolver refinanced to 2031; 61.6% short interest | Car Rental |\n| LCID | -7.9% | Large | CFO departure despite Q2 deliveries +19% YoY | EV |\n| SURG | +38.1% | Small | AT&T eliminated $50M min spend; $10.3M charges waived | Wireless / Fintech |\n| MQ | -0.2% | Small | +316.5% 5D; Morgan Stanley PT $20; Deutsche PT $18 | Payments |\n\n---\n\n### 🧭 Narrative Threads\n\n**Semiconductor Selloff & AI Capex Debate** `AI Compute / Memory` → ongoing\n\nThe sharpest two-day **SOXX** decline since March 2025 (-12.9%) hit every corner of the chip complex, dragging **MU** -5.5%, **SNDK** -14.1%, **LRCX** -7.3%, **KLAC** -5.3%, **AMAT** -3.8%, and **AAOI** -15.8% on Thursday alone. The selloff compounds Wednesday's carnage when **GLW** fell 13.2% and **CRWV** plunged 13.9%.\n\n- **Michael Burry** disclosed shorts on **NVDA** at $198.09, **TSLA** at $416.22, **CAT** at $1,060.98, **AMAT** at $729.40, and **SOXX** puts rolled to March 2027; called the Philadelphia Semiconductor Index extension above its 200-day MA "historically high" and "the beginning of the end."\n- **MU**: Burry calls MU a "destroyer of capital" with 42-year median ROIC of 4%, negative FCF 48% of the time; stock 200-day MA extension "never as extended as in 2026, not even during the dot-com peak." BofA Mohan reiterated Buy on **SNDK**, raised PT from $2,100 to $2,500, modeling $9.1B June-quarter revenue vs $8.35B consensus. Bernstein raised SNDK PT to $3,000.\n- **SOXX** RSI exited overbought for the first time since late March after 12 straight weeks above the threshold—longest streak of the current cycle.\n- **Micron** still commands the most analyst bullishness: Cantor Fitzgerald street-high PT $2,000; Barclays $2,000; Raymond James $1,500.\n- Cleveland Fed's **Beth Hammack** warned AI infrastructure demand is "insatiable" and could keep inflation elevated, potentially requiring higher rates.\n- Leveraged ETF daily rebalancing flows hit record $50B (4x since start of 2026), now 1.60% of S&P 500 futures volume—amplifying end-of-day volatility.\n\n**Rotation Into Blue Chips & Defensives** `Macro / Sector Rotation` → ongoing\n\nThe Dow Jones hit a fresh record above 52,900 on Thursday, outperforming the Nasdaq 100 (-1.9%) by 2.6 percentage points. XLV (Healthcare) +2.2%, XLP (Consumer Staples) +1.7%, XLF (Financials) +0.7% led sectors. Gold +2.3% to ~$4,124/oz; silver +3.2% to ~$61.\n\n- June nonfarm payrolls: 57K vs 110K est (100K FactSet); prior two months revised down by combined 74K. Unemployment rate edged to 4.2% (vs 4.3% est) but labor force participation fell to 61.5%, lowest since March 2021.\n- CME FedWatch: July hike probability collapsed to ~20% from ~64% a day earlier; Polymarket prices 10% chance of July hike.\n- Fed's **Warsh** at ECB Sintra: inflation "too high" but expectations have "moderated" since May swearing-in. Fed's **Daly**: "I'm a gradualist"; can't give forward guidance; oil coming down is "good news."\n- WTI crude fell to ~$68/bbl, fully erasing the Iran war premium. Citi forecasts Brent $60 by year-end. Gasoline national avg $3.82/gal; DOJ/FTC sent letter to state AGs urging investigation of elevated pump prices.\n\n**Meta Cloud Pivot & Neocloud Pressure** `AI Cloud Infrastructure` → new\n\n**META** surged 8.8% Wednesday on Bloomberg report of "Meta Compute" cloud business to sell excess AI capacity, then gave back 4.9% Thursday as investors digested capex implications and Zuckerberg admitted AI bets "haven't come to fruition yet."\n\n- Zuckerberg internal town hall: "trajectory of agentic development over at least the last four months hasn't really accelerated the way we expected"; reorganization "wasn't as clean as it could have been."\n- BofA analyst Post: every GW of AI infra made available externally could generate ~$20B annual revenue; cumulative capex ~$850B through 2030 implies ~19 GW of capacity.\n- **CRWV** -13.9% Wed, -0.3% Thu; **NBIS** -17.0% Wed, +2.1% Thu. Rosenblatt (Buy, $250 PT on CRWV) said Meta likely cannot resell CoreWeave-leased capacity.\n- Nebius Q1 2026: revenue $399M (+684% YoY), raised 2026 capex guidance to $20-25B from $16-20B; 45% adjusted EBITDA margin on AI business.\n- Samsung Foundry reportedly in talks with Meta for MTIA Gen-3 chip manufacturing ($6.5B+ deal); Anthropic also reportedly exploring custom chip with Samsung on 2nm process.\n- Meta's "Watermelon" AI model reportedly matches OpenAI GPT-5.5 on key benchmarks per Alexandr Wang.\n\n**Enterprise AI Data Sovereignty** `Enterprise AI / SaaS` → ongoing\n\n**PLTR** +5.2% after DA Davidson upgraded to Buy from Neutral, raised PT $165→$175, calling current valuation a "gift."\n\n- CEO **Alex Karp** on CNBC: "Something has gone completely wrong" with OpenAI/Anthropic token-based pricing; enterprises increasingly switching to open-weight models.\n- David Sacks endorsed Karp's thesis, citing Anthropic's Claude Design launch after a board member served on **FIG** board; Figma shares down ~50% YTD while Anthropic valuation surged.\n- Futurum Equities called Palantir the "oxygen" behind enterprise AI, arguing proprietary data protection creates an "unacquirable moat."\n- Guggenheim upgraded **CRM** to Buy/$228 (from Neutral), arguing 73% of software names trade at or below a "zero growth" multiple of 5.4x recurring revenue—worst since the GFC.\n- **ADBE** +4.1% after HSBC upgraded to Buy/$308; weak jobs data eased rate-hike fears for long-duration growth names.\n\n**Tesla Delivery Beat / EV Landscape** `EV / Autonomy` → ongoing\n\n**TSLA** -7.5% despite Q2 deliveries of 480,126 (+25% YoY, +34% QoQ) beating the 406K Street estimate by 18%. Tesla launched the Model Y L at $61,990 with free 12-month FSD subscription.\n\n- **Gene Munster**: sell-the-news driven by pre-announcement run-up, high gas prices artificially boosting demand, DOGE headwind resolution.\n- **Gary Black**: "FSD is not driving sales. The avg consumer has no idea about FSD." Cited **RIVN** +8.4% on its own delivery beat as evidence EV demand was gas-price-driven, not autonomy-driven.\n- **RIVN** raised FY26 delivery guidance to 65-70K from 62-67K after Q2 deliveries of 12,194 beat 9-11K guide.\n- **LCID** -7.9% after CFO departure despite Q2 production +24% YoY. **Ford** EV sales -40.7% YoY in Q2 (9,746 units).\n- Cathie Wood's ARK bought 96,935 TSLA shares ($38.1M) across ARKK/ARKW/ARKX on the dip.\n\n**Space & Satellite** `Space / Launch` → ongoing\n\n**RKLB** +18.8% over 5 days following $8B Iridium acquisition (cash + stock, mid-2027 close) and NASA CLPS awards. **ASTS** +19.1% over 5D; next 3 BlueBird satellites targeting Aug launch from Cape Canaveral. **SPCX** joins Nasdaq-100 on July 7, with ~$4.3B estimated passive inflows.\n\n- FCC announced July 22 vote on Space Modernization Order to cut satellite licensing from >1 year to weeks/months.\n- Amazon Leo constellation surpassed 390 satellites after 14th launch; targeting initial service later this year.\n- Elon Musk denied WSJ report that SpaceX is developing a handheld AI device, calling it "utterly false."\n- Trump said he expects Musk to donate SpaceX stock to Trump Accounts; neither Musk nor SpaceX has confirmed.\n\n**Earnings Season Setup** `Earnings` → new\n\n- **General Mills** (GIS): FQ4 adj EPS $0.95 beat $0.80 est; FY27 organic sales guided -1.5% to +0.5%; TD Cowen analyst: "Big Food is in structural decline."\n- **Constellation Brands** (STZ): FQ1 adj EPS $3.43 beat $3.21; revenue $2.43B beat $2.39B; affirmed FY EPS $11.20-$11.90.\n- **FactSet** (FDS): FQ3 revenue $622.9M beat $618.3M; adj EPS $4.53 beat $4.46; ~10% of ASV growth from discrete AI SKUs; 550+ MCP clients.\n- **MSC Industrial** (MSM): FQ3 adj EPS $1.43 beat $1.26; revenue $1.047B beat $1.031B est.\n- **Lindsay** (LNN): FQ3 EPS $1.53 beat $1.31 est; sales $160.8M beat $157.8M est.\n\n**📡 Undercurrents** *(developing, not yet dominant)*\n- **O'Reilly Automotive (ORLY)** submitted a cash bid for **Genuine Parts (GPC)** auto unit, potentially valued at $10B+; GPC surged 12.9% intraday then gave back gains after-hours. ORLY +1.8%.\n- **Robinhood (HOOD)** launched Robinhood Chain (L2 on Arbitrum), tokenized stock trading in 120+ countries, and Robinhood Earn with ~7% APY on USDG; Mizuho raised PT to $130. Trump Accounts app live with 5.5M children enrolled ahead of July 4 rollout.\n- **CrowdStrike (CRWD)** began 4-for-1 split-adjusted trading; near 52-week highs at $196.80 with RSI at 72.82.\n- Canada PM **Mark Carney** announced pipeline to west coast for 1M bbl/day to Pacific markets, targeting construction start Sept 2027—potential to diversify oil exports beyond US.\n- Former special counsel **Jack Smith** broke silence, calling Trump-era rule-of-law attacks "different in kind and scope" from anything in his lifetime; said he's "very concerned" about midterm election integrity.\n\n---\n\n### 📰 Channel Pulse\n\n| Channel | Material fact |\n|---------|--------------|\n| Macro | June NFP +57K vs +110K est; April/May revised down by combined 74K; unemployment 4.2%; labor force participation fell to 61.5% (lowest since Mar 2021); July hike odds collapsed to ~20% |\n| Earnings | GIS FQ4 adj EPS $0.95 vs $0.80 est; STZ FQ1 adj EPS $3.43 vs $3.21 est; FDS FQ3 adj EPS $4.53 vs $4.46 est; LNN FQ3 EPS $1.53 vs $1.31 est |\n| Tech | META Zuckerberg admitted AI agent development "hasn't accelerated the way we expected"; Samsung reportedly in talks with Meta ($6.5B MTIA deal) and Anthropic (custom 2nm chip) |\n| Commodities | WTI crude ~$68/bbl, fully erasing Iran war premium; gold +2.3% to ~$4,124; silver +3.2% to ~$61; Citi forecasts Brent $60 by year-end |\n| Equities | SOXX worst 2-day drop since Mar 2025 (-12.9%); Dow hit record >52,900; Guggenheim upgraded CRM to Buy, called SaaS selloff a "hallucination" |\n| Space | RKLB $8B Iridium acquisition; SPCX joins Nasdaq-100 Jul 7; Amazon Leo passed 390 satellites; FCC Space Modernization Order vote Jul 22 |\n| Crypto | SEC Chair Atkins: moved "purposely" to make US "Crypto Capital of the World"; Trump $1B+ crypto windfall disclosed; ~1.48M TRUMP token wallets underwater per WSJ |\n| Energy | DOJ/FTC urged state AGs to investigate elevated gas prices; Chevron CFO: pump price lag is normal; national avg $3.82/gal |\n\n---\n\n### 👁 On The Radar\n\n- **SpaceX (SPCX) Nasdaq-100 inclusion**: Effective before market open Monday, July 7; analysts estimate ~$4.3B in forced passive buying from index-tracking funds.\n- **TSLA Q2 earnings**: July 22 after close; Street expects EPS $0.42 on revenue $24.61B.\n- **AAPL Q3 earnings**: Estimated July 30; Street expects EPS $1.89 on revenue $108.86B. Memory supply tightness + hardware price hikes remain key watch items.\n- **INTC Q2 earnings**: July 23; Street expects EPS $0.19 on revenue $14.40B. Goldman initiated Neutral/$150; BofA Buy/$160.\n- **TSM Q2 earnings**: July 16; Street expects EPS $3.77 on revenue $39.76B. ⚠️\n- **NFLX Q2 earnings**: July 16 (confirmed); Street expects EPS $0.79 on revenue $12.58B. ⚠️\n- **VZ Q2 earnings**: July 24 (confirmed); expects $700-800M Q2 loss from held-for-sale accounting on BT JV.\n- **JPM, WFC Q2 earnings**: July 14 before open; JPM est EPS $5.61, WFC est EPS $1.71.\n- **Fed July meeting**: July 29; CME FedWatch shows 78% probability of hold, 22% hike.\n- **Trump Administration 340B hospital drug markup rule**: proposed to save patients $1.1B next year; public comment period open.\n\n---\n\n### 🚫 What to Ignore Today\n\n- **"How much $1,000 invested in X years ago"** articles: Benzinga published dozens of historical return pieces (AMAT, COST, MELI, VRT, etc.)—backward-looking filler with zero forward signal.\n- **Congressional stock trades**: Sen. Peters bought $1-15K of AT&T; Rep. Meuser bought $15-50K of SpaceX. Amounts are immaterial and lagging indicators.\n- **Trump political/cultural headlines**: Pope Leo speech, children's podcast appearance, Colombian sanctions discussion, July 4 celebration logistics—none move markets.	\N	{"calls": [{"step": "step4_synthesis", "model": "claude-opus-4-6", "api_model": "claude-opus-4-6", "input_tokens": 493784, "output_tokens": 4914, "pricing_model": "claude-opus-4-6", "input_cost_usd": 2.46892, "total_cost_usd": 2.59177, "output_cost_usd": 0.12285, "cache_read_cost_usd": 0.0, "cache_write_cost_usd": 0.0, "cache_read_input_tokens": 0, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 0}], "call_count": 1, "started_at": "2026-07-04T13:53:54.104055+00:00", "completed_at": "2026-07-04T13:56:32.287761+00:00", "current_step": null, "pricing_note": "Token counts from Anthropic Messages API response.usage. USD estimated from published claude.com/api pricing (input, 5m cache write, cache read, output) — Anthropic does not return dollar amounts on the response.", "total_cost_usd": 2.5918, "total_input_tokens": 493784, "total_output_tokens": 4914, "total_cache_read_input_tokens": 0, "total_cache_creation_input_tokens": 0}	{"asof": "2026-07-04", "counts": {"prompt": {"by_section": {"Channel: tech": 11, "Channel: movers": 31, "Channel: general": 97, "Channel: markets": 4, "Channel: earnings": 16, "Channel: equities": 34, "Channel: commodities": 8, "Ticker Group: r1d_screener": 62, "Ticker Group: vol_spike_5d": 26, "Ticker Group: ti65_mid_small": 2, "Ticker Group: ti65_mega_large": 26, "Channel: macro_economic_events": 31}, "articles_total": 348}, "upserted": 348, "api_by_fetch": {"ticker": {"T": {"error": null, "api_rows": 7, "in_window": 7}, "BB": {"error": null, "api_rows": 3, "in_window": 3}, "BE": {"error": null, "api_rows": 12, "in_window":