% YoY) vs $1.80B est; adj EPS $0.41 vs $0.35 est\n- U.S. revenue +115% YoY to $1.57B; U.S. commercial revenue +~150% YoY\n- Total commercial booking value +158% YoY (vs +47% prior quarter)\n- Adj free cash flow margin 63%; Rule of 40 score: 155\n- Cash and short-term Treasuries: $9.2B\n- Q3 revenue guide $2.16-2.164B vs $1.997B est\n- FY26 revenue guide raised to $8.15-8.16B from $7.65-7.66B vs $7.69B est\n- Deutsche Bank upgraded Hold → Buy, maintaining $200 PT; Cantor Fitzgerald raised PT $138 → $156 (Neutral); Piper Sandler maintains Overweight PT $230\n- CEO Karp criticized frontier AI labs for "Marxist overtones," arguing they seek to "colonize your enterprise" by capturing customer IP\n- Polymarket odds of PLTR hitting $150 in August jumped from 48% to 84%\n\n**AI Infrastructure Spending Confidence Broadens** `AI Compute / AI Infra` → ongoing\n\nBig-tech earnings last week (AMZN, MSFT) cemented confidence that hyperscaler AI capex translates to returns. Monday's rally extended into Tuesday pre-market across the stack.\n\n- **AMZN** +4.4% (5D +22.6%); surpassed $3T market cap for first time; CEO Jassy raised capex budget from $200B to $220B; Jim Cramer said Jassy's explanation "changed how Wall Street views Big Tech AI spending"\n- **MSFT** +6.0% (5D +25.2%); Azure/Copilot monetization reduces AI capex skepticism\n- **META** +7.4%; Cramer criticized Meta for not articulating AI capex return plan as clearly as Amazon\n- **ORCL** +9.7% (5D +18.5%); continued AI cloud momentum\n- Anthropic signed $10B computing deal with Volta Infra (Bloomberg)\n- **BTDR** +23% pre-market on 16-year, ~$4.7B colocation lease through Tydal Data Center AS for 121 IT MW in Norway; Dell Technologies as technology provider; avg $202/kW/month\n- Citadel Securities predicts $500B of debt issuance by 2028 to fund AI chip infrastructure; calls it potentially "one of the largest new sectors in investment-grade credit"\n- U.S. Treasury Secretary Bessent (CNBC): "We've seen interest rates rise