ct design win or hyperscaler infrastructure contract naming a marquee end-customer (Apple, NVIDIA, Google, etc.), which limits it to material but not game-changing."}, {"dr_1": -7.81, "dr_5": 54.47, "atr20": 10.9, "dr_20": 89.47, "dr_60": 144.93, "sector": "Technology", "ticker": "HIMX", "sources": ["top_20d", "top_5d", "volspike_gapper"], "verdict": "PICK", "industry": "Semiconductors", "theme_fit": 3, "market_cap": 3601895124, "rsi_mktcap": 100, "drop_reason": null, "company_name": "Himax Technologies, Inc.", "news_summary": "The recent 90%+ 20‑day move in HIMX looks like a sharp re‑rating off what investors are treating as the bottom of the earnings cycle plus a powerful momentum/technical chase, rather than a single new contract or product announcement. Q4 2025 results modestly beat revenue expectations and signaled stabilisation, which, combined with heavy semiconductor risk‑on and narratives around Himax’s exposure to AR/VR and automotive/edge‑AI displays, triggered a squeeze higher that then fed on itself as the stock hit 52‑week highs and momentum screens on elevated volume. I cannot find any confirmed new big‑ticket customer win, regulatory decision, or M&A since mid‑March that would independently justify a near‑doubling of the share price, so a large part of this move appears to be sentiment, sector sympathy, and breakout trading rather than fresh hard fundamentals.", "current_price": 18.9087, "matched_themes": ["breakout-and-momentum", "turnaround-and-recovery", "edgeai", "optical-and-data-center-gear"], "momentum_score": 90.9, "verdict_claude": "PICK", "vol_vs_10d_avg": 1.59, "composite_score": 76.36, "last_event_date": "2026-05-11", "last_event_type": "gapper", "news_theme_tags": ["breakout-and-momentum", "turnaround-and-recovery", "edgeai", "small-cap-relief-rally"], "dropped_at_stage": null, "headline_drivers": ["Sharp multi‑week re‑rating after strong Q4 2025 print: revenue $203.1M vs. ~$199M expected and in-line EPS ($0.036), interpreted as the trough for the down‑cycle with management guiding stabilisation into 2026 (reported early May 2026)", "Follow‑through buying as Himax hits new 52‑week highs and momentum screens after a 30–40% multi‑day spike (TipRanks/MarketsInsider note citing Himax up ~37% in a single session, early May 2026), pulling in technical and quant‑momentum flows on 1.5–2x normal volume", "Sector sympathy from a broad semiconductor/AI rally lifting small and mid‑cap display/edge‑AI names, with Himax framed in recent commentary as a potential beneficiary of AR/VR smart‑glasses and automotive display demand even though no new marquee customer has been formally announced"], "momentum_factors": {"rsi_mktcap": {"points": 15, "reason": "rsi_mktcap=100"}, "vol_vs_avg": {"points": 6.0, "reason": "vol 1.59x"}, "dr5_atr_norm": {"points": 14.9, "reason": "dr5=54.5% atr=10.9% norm=2.23"}, "multi_screen": {"points": 20, "reason": "3 screens"}, "recent_event": {"points": 20, "reason": "event 2d ago"}, "trend_alignment": {"points": 15, "reason": "both up (20d=89.5, 60d=144.9)"}}, "news_materiality": 1, "theme_fit_reason": "HIMX directly aligns with the trader's own thesis on this ticker (stars=1): the Q4 beat and stabilization narrative confirm the 'trough' inflection the trader flagged, and the automotive TCON +50% YoY and OLED Touch IC HX85200 mass-production ramp support the edge-AI and display-infrastructure themes. The WiseEye smart-glasses adoption story (Apple mass production 2026–27) and CPO partnership narrative remain live and uncontradicted by this earnings print, extending the trader's multi-year re-rating thesis.", "has_material_news": true, "verdict_rationale": "Although news_materiality is modest (a beat + stabilization signal, not a blockbuster contract), the trader's own thesis on HIMX is explicitly confirmed and extended by this earnings print: the cycle trough is in, automotive and display-edge-AI demand are accelerating, and the WiseEye/smart-glasses and CPO partnership narratives remain on track for 2026–27 inflection. The 90%+ momentum move off a technical breakout at 52-week highs, combined with the trader's pre-existing conviction and thesis alignment, justifies a PICK. This is exactly the 'turnaround + breakout + thematic continuation' setup the trader has already vetted.", "last_event_magnitude": 0.1574, "latest_material_date": "2026-05-11", "news_materiality_reason": "Q4 2025 earnings beat revenue expectations ($203.1M vs. ~$199M) and signaled stabilization/trough, but this is a modest beat with in-line EPS and no new named customer win, contract, or quantified guidance raise. The move is primarily sentiment-driven sector sympathy and technical breakout rather than hard fundamental catalyst."}, {"dr_1": 28.29, "dr_5": 24.81, "atr20": 6.86, "dr_20": 20.75, "dr_60": 26.11, "sector": "Basic Materials", "ticker": "BAK", "sources": ["top_1d", "volspike_gapper"], "verdict": "WATCH", "industry": "Chemicals", "theme_fit": 3, "market_cap": 1514239869, "rsi_mktcap": 97, "drop_reason": null, "company_name": "Braskem S.A.", "news_summary": "The stock’s sharp move is being driven primarily by a renewed M&A/control-change narrative, not by a sudden improvement in fundamentals. In late April, Petrobras said it would not exercise its waiver over Novonor’s Braskem stake, and reports that Novonor agreed to sell that stake to IG4 effectively reopened the door to a control transaction that investors hope will come at a premium to the prior trading range. Against a backdrop of weak earnings and still‑pressured petrochemical margins, that strategic overhang clearing is what the market is repricing. The latest 1‑day surge looks like an acceleration of that re‑rating, amplified by event‑driven flows, momentum buying, and likely short covering on very high volume rather than by new operational data.", "current_price": 4.83, "matched_themes": ["special-situation-re-rating", "turnaround-and-recovery"], "momentum_score": 82.78, "verdict_claude": "WATCH", "vol_vs_10d_avg": 5.47, "composite_score": 83.11, "last_event_date": "2026-05-12", "last_event_type": "volume_spike", "news_theme_tags": ["special-situation-re-rating", "turnaround-and-recovery", "small-cap-relief-rally"], "dropped_at_stage": null, "headline_drivers": ["Brazil’s Petrobras decides not to exercise its waiver/right of first refusal over the sale of Novonor’s controlling stake in Braskem, effectively clearing the way for a third‑party buyer (Reuters, 2026-04-23)", "Reports that Novonor has agreed to sell its Braskem stake to private equity group IG4 and partners, reviving the long‑running Braskem M&A story and raising expectations for a control transaction at a premium to the undisturbed price (Reuters, 2026-04-20)", "Follow‑through buying and technical re‑rating as traders reposition around an event‑driven, takeover‑speculation name: BAK shows a 1‑day move of ~28%, volume >5x 10‑day average, and an RSI in the 97th percentile, consistent with a momentum/short‑covering squeeze layered on top of the M&A headlines (volume spike event logged 2026-05-12)"], "momentum_factors": {"rsi_mktcap": {"points": 15, "reason": "rsi_mktcap=97"}, "vol_vs_avg": {"points": 10, "reason": "vol 5.47x"}, "dr5_atr_norm": {"points": 10.78, "reason": "dr5=24.8% atr=6.9% norm=1.62"}, "multi_screen": {"points": 12.0, "reason": "2 screens"}, "recent_event": {"points": 20, "reason": "event 1d ago"}, "trend_alignment": {"points": 15, "reason": "both up (20d=20.8, 60d=26.1)"}}, "news_materiality": 2, "theme_fit_reason": "This fits the trader’s clear appetite for special situations where a control change or structure change can re-rate a neglected name. It also matches the broader turnaround/recovery pattern of traders repositioning around a changed setup rather than pure fundamentals.", "has_material_news": true, "verdict_rationale": "The stock has strong momentum, but the underlying news is an M&A/speculation-driven re-rating rather than a marquee earnings or customer-growth story. It does fit the trader’s special-situation bias very well, so it stays interesting, but it doesn’t clear the bar for a PICK because the catalyst is still a control-transaction narrative rather than a confirmed game-changing event.", "last_event_magnitude": 5.05, "latest_material_date": "2026-04-23", "news_materiality_reason": "The catalyst is a material control-change/M&A development: Petrobras declined to exercise its right of first refusal, and reports say Novonor agreed to sell its Braskem stake to IG4 and partners. That is a real strategic unlock and can re-rate the stock, though it is not a confirmed completed transaction or a transformational operating beat."}, {"dr_1": 5.62, "dr_5": 118.77, "atr20": 15.95, "dr_20": 258.71, "dr_60": 277.24, "sector": "Technology", "ticker": "MRAM", "sources": ["top_20d", "top_5d", "volspike_gapper"], "verdict": "WATCH", "industry": "Semiconductors", "theme_fit": 2, "market_cap": 934620300, "rsi_mktcap": 99, "drop_reason": null, "company_name": "Everspin Technologies, Inc.", "news_summary": "The move appears to have a real fundamental catalyst, but the catalyst is defense/microelectronics rather than a marquee consumer-AI customer. The key verifiable driver is Everspin's disclosed $40.0M subcontract with Amentum for U.S. government work, plus a long-dated Microchip manufacturing agreement that strengthens the onshore supply-chain story. Those announcements came alongside a clean Q1 2026 print: revenue up 13.2% to $14.9M, product sales up 27.9%, gross margin at 52.7%, and non-GAAP EPS of $0.11, with Q2 revenue guidance above the prior-quarter run rate. I could not verify any recent Apple/NVIDIA/TSMC-type design win, analyst supply-chain note, or other marquee-buyer rumor. So the honest read is: this is primarily a defense-supply-chain + earnings + guidance re-rating, with momentum/short-covering amplifying the move, not the sole cause.", "current_price": 41.61, "matched_themes": ["big-ticket-customer-wins", "earnings-beat-and-raise", "breakout-and-momentum", "low-float-or-small-cap-catalyst", "memory-and-interconnect", "fab-and-foundry", "defense-tech"], "momentum_score": 100.0, "verdict_claude": "WATCH", "vol_vs_10d_avg": 2.37, "composite_score": 80.0, "last_event_date": "2026-05-11", "last_event_type": "volume_spike", "news_theme_tags": ["semiconductors", "defense-tech", "mram", "foundry", "small-cap-momentum", "big-ticket-customer-wins", "defense-and-geopolitical-risk", "earnings-beat-and-raise", "memory-and-interconnect", "fab-and-foundry", "low-float-or-small-cap-catalyst", "breakout-and-momentum", "edgeai"], "dropped_at_stage": null, "headline_drivers": ["Everspin disclosed a $40.0M subcontract with Amentum for U.S. government microelectronics work, tying its MRAM/Toggle MRAM process technology to a major defense-aerospace program (announced with Q1 2026 results on 2026-04-29/2026-04-30).", "The company also announced a 10-year manufacturing/foundry agreement with Microchip to expand onshore MRAM production capacity, with roughly $13.95M of reimbursements/commitments cited in recent coverage (late April 2026).", "Q1 2026 results were solid: revenue rose 13.2% year over year to $14.9M, product sales grew 27.9% to $14.1M, gross margin improved to 52.7%, and non-GAAP EPS came in at $0.11; Everspin also guided Q2 2026 revenue to $15.5M-$16.5M.", "I did not find credible evidence in the supplied search set of an Apple/NVIDIA/TSMC/Google/Microsoft/Amazon/Meta/Tesla/OpenAI/Anthropic or major auto OEM marquee design win, qualification, or supply-chain rumor in the last 60-90 days.", "I also did not find a Ming-Chi Kuo, DigiTimes, Wedbush, Loop Capital, Needham, or Morgan Stanley supply-chain note specifically on Everspin in the provided results.", "$40M, 2.5-year defense subcontract to supply Toggle MRAM process technology and engineering services to a U.S. prime contractor for defense/aerospace programs (announced around 2026-04-30; described in trading commentary 2026-05-11)", "Q1 2026 earnings beat with 13% YoY revenue growth to roughly $14.9M, gross margin ~51%, positive non-GAAP EPS (~$0.11) and positive operating cash flow, against a backdrop of strong balance sheet ($40.5M cash, minimal debt) (reported late April 2026, referenced in May 2026 coverage)", "Raised top-line outlook with Q2 2026 revenue guidance of $15.5M–$16.5M vs Street ~$14.7M, signaling growth acceleration even as EPS guide ($0.00–$0.03) came in below prior consensus (communicated with Q1 print, late April 2026)", "Strategic U.S.-based manufacturing/foundry agreement with Microchip to expand onshore, defense-grade MRAM supply, aligning with domestic semiconductor and secure-defense supply-chain themes (timeline: pre-Q1 2026, highlighted in recent articles)", "Momentum/technical re-rating: float-constrained small-cap memory name that tripled from ~$13 to low/mid-$40s in under two weeks on 10x–13x volume spikes after the defense deal and earnings, turning into a high-RSI, high-ATR momentum tape (late April to mid-May 2026)"], "momentum_factors": {"rsi_mktcap": {"points": 15, "reason": "rsi_mktcap=99"}, "vol_vs_avg": {"points": 10, "reason": "vol 2.37x"}, "dr5_atr_norm": {"points": 20.0, "reason": "dr5=118.8% atr=15.9% norm=3.33"}, "multi_screen": {"points": 20, "reason": "3 screens"}, "recent_event": {"points": 20, "reason": "event 2d ago"}, "trend_alignment": {"points": 15, "reason": "both up (20d=258.7, 60d=277.2)"}}, "news_materiality": 2, "theme_fit_reason": "This fits several of the trader’s hot themes: big-ticket customer wins, earnings/raise, small-cap momentum, and memory/interconnect/foundry exposure. It does not match the trader’s strongest AI-hyperscaler pattern, but it clearly sits in the picks-and-shovels semiconductor buildout lane.", "has_material_news": true, "verdict_rationale": "This is a real catalyst-driven momentum name, but the news is more defense/microelectronics and foundry capacity than the trader’s highest-conviction AI hyperscaler or marquee customer-win setups. The current move supports the re-rating narrative and keeps it on the screen, but it does not quite clear the bar for a PICK because the story is not transformational enough and lacks a named hyperscaler-scale buyer.", "last_event_magnitude": 12.79, "latest_material_date": "2026-05-11", "news_materiality_reason": "The move is backed by real material news: a $40M defense subcontract with Amentum, a 10-year foundry/manufacturing agreement with Microchip, and a solid Q1 with revenue growth, margin expansion, and raised Q2 revenue guidance. It is not a marquee hyperscaler/customer-win story, but it is more than a generic sympathy move."}, {"dr_1": 45.21, "dr_5": 62.55, "atr20": 8.43, "dr_20": 119.12, "dr_60": 122.16, "sector": "Technology", "ticker": "AMBQ", "sources": ["top_1d", "top_20d", "top_5d", "volspike_gapper"], "verdict": "WATCH", "industry": "Semiconductors", "theme_fit": 2, "market_cap": 975735622, "rsi_mktcap": 99, "drop_reason": null, "company_name": "Ambiq Micro, Inc.", "news_summary": "Verification confirms that the recent surge in Ambiq Micro (AMBQ) is tied to its first quarter 2026 earnings release and 10‑Q filing on 2026-05-12, not to any newly disclosed mega‑customer, M&A deal, or regulatory surprise.\\n\\nAmbiq reported Q1 2026 net sales of $25.1M, up 59.3% YoY from $15.7M, driven by broad-based demand and ramping of new product launches at higher average selling prices, including at least one \\"new major customer\\" (not named in public filings). Gross profit increased to $10.9M; gross margin was 43.5%, lower than the prior year primarily because a $1.6M non‑monetary gain that benefited 2025 did not recur. Despite the strong top-line growth, Ambiq posted a net loss of about $10.2M for the quarter as it continues to spend aggressively on R&D and commercial expansion for its ultra‑low‑power edge AI system‑on‑chips.\\n\\nThe 10‑Q and earnings commentary emphasize a solid liquidity position: $204.5M in cash and cash equivalents at quarter end, supported by a January 2026 follow‑on equity offering that generated roughly $75.3M in net proceeds plus about $3M from warrant and option exercises. This capital base is presented as a key strength, giving the company runway to pursue growth while still loss‑making.\\n\\nAt the same time, Ambiq explicitly flags structural risks: its top three end customers accounted for 71% of net sales, and it relies on a single wafer foundry in Taiwan. The filings highlight customer and geographic concentration, industry cyclicality, and macroeconomic conditions as ongoing business risks.\\n\\nPress coverage and secondary analysis (Business Wire/Fidelity reproduction of the earnings release, StockTitan summary of the 10‑Q, GuruFocus earnings-call recap) all frame the quarter as evidence of rapid adoption of Ambiq’s ultra‑low‑power edge AI products and a growth inflection, while also acknowledging continued losses and concentration risk. There is no concrete evidence in filings or reputable reports of a newly revealed marquee design win (e.g., specific hyperscaler, handset OEM, or automotive Tier 1), a transformative AI partnership, or an M&A or regulatory catalyst in the last ~60 days. The \\"new major customer\\" remains unnamed, with no dollar-amount disclosure beyond its contribution to overall revenue growth.\\n\\nGiven the timing (earnings release and call on 2026-05-12) and the stock’s appearance on top 1‑day/5‑day/20‑day performance and volume-spike screens, the move is best understood as a fundamental re‑rating around very strong reported growth and liquidity, amplified by technical and momentum dynamics, rather than being driven by a single confirmed mega‑deal rumor. No credible sources were found that either substantiate or explicitly deny any specific rumored hyperscale AI or consumer-electronics design win; the evidence base is limited to Ambiq’s own high-level disclosure of a new major customer and the broader edge-AI growth narrative.", "current_price": 65.7361, "matched_themes": ["edge-ai", "ai-compute", "optical-and-photonics", "breakout-and-momentum", "low-float-or-small-cap-catalyst", "earnings-surprise-momentum"], "momentum_score": 100.0, "verdict_claude": "WATCH", "vol_vs_10d_avg": 8.23, "composite_score": 80.0, "last_event_date": "2026-05-12", "last_event_type": "volume_spike", "news_theme_tags": ["edge-ai", "iot-and-wearables", "semiconductors", "high-growth-unprofitable", "customer-concentration-risk", "capital-raise-and-liquidity", "edgeai", "analog-and-mixed-signal", "earnings-surprise-momentum", "breakout-and-momentum", "low-float-or-small-cap-catalyst"], "dropped_at_stage": null, "headline_drivers": ["Q1 2026 results: net sales up 59.3% YoY to $25.1M, driven by broad-based demand and higher ASPs for ultra–low‑power edge AI SoCs, including ramps at a new major customer (Form 10‑Q and earnings release, quarter ended 2026-03-31; filed/released 2026-05-12)", "Liquidity and capital raise: cash and cash equivalents of $204.5M bolstered by $75.3M in net proceeds from a January 2026 follow‑on stock offering plus ~$3M from warrant/option exercises, reducing near‑term financing risk and supporting growth investments (Form 10‑Q, 2026-05-12)", "Margin and loss profile: gross profit rose to $10.9M but gross margin was 43.5% (down vs prior year due to a non‑recurring $1.6M non‑monetary gain in 2025), with a Q1 2026 net loss of $10.2M as Ambiq continues to invest heavily in R&D and go‑to‑market for edge AI chips (Form 10‑Q and earnings call commentary, 2026-05-12)", "Concentration and supply‑chain risk disclosure: top three end customers represented 71% of net sales and wafers are sourced from a single Taiwan foundry, with management explicitly flagging customer concentration, geographic concentration, industry cyclicality, and macro conditions as key structural risks (Form 10‑Q risk factors and MD&A, 2026-05-12)", "Momentum and media framing: financial news and secondary aggregators highlighted the 59% YoY revenue growth and strong liquidity as evidence of a growth inflection in Ambiq’s ultra‑low‑power edge AI business, helping trigger a breakout move on ~8x normal volume and pulling AMBQ onto top-performer and volume-spike screens (e.g., Business Wire via Fidelity, StockTitan, GuruFocus earnings call recap, all dated 2026-05-12)", "Reports of double‑digit sales growth and sharply accelerating revenue at Ambiq Micro highlighted in financial media; one cited move had the stock up ~37% to an all‑time high around $63.60 on strong demand for its ultra‑low‑power semiconductor/IoT products (WSJ / secondary news aggregation, 2026-05-12)", "Multiple days of unusually heavy trading volume (≈8x 10‑day average) and momentum-screen appearances following those sales-growth reports, triggering breakout/momentum buying and short-covering rather than any new discrete customer or regulatory announcement (2026-05-12 onward)"], "momentum_factors": {"rsi_mktcap": {"points": 15, "reason": "rsi_mktcap=99"}, "vol_vs_avg": {"points": 10, "reason": "vol 8.23x"}, "dr5_atr_norm": {"points": 20.0, "reason": "dr5=62.5% atr=8.4% norm=3.32"}, "multi_screen": {"points": 20, "reason": "4 screens"}, "recent_event": {"points": 20, "reason": "event 1d ago"}, "trend_alignment": {"points": 15, "reason": "both up (20d=119.1, 60d=122.2)"}}, "news_materiality": 2, "theme_fit_reason": "This fits the trader’s hot edge-AI and breakout/momentum taste, with a semiconductor growth story tied to ultra-low-power AI SoCs and a stock acting like a recent earnings winner. It is only a partial fit rather than a top-tier one because the news does not confirm a named hyperscaler, marquee customer, or optical/data-center infrastructure angle.", "has_material_news": true, "verdict_rationale": "AMBQ has real news and real momentum, but the catalyst is mainly a strong earnings print plus an unnamed customer ramp rather than a trader-grade blockbuster contract. The setup aligns with the trader’s preference for growth and breakout names, but it does not clear the bar for a true PICK because the thematic link is edge AI/semis rather than a clearly confirmed hyperscaler or named-design-win story.", "last_event_magnitude": 8.14, "latest_material_date": "2026-05-12", "news_materiality_reason": "Q1 2026 earnings were materially strong: revenue rose 59.3% YoY, liquidity improved sharply, and management cited ramps at a new major customer, which together support a real fundamental re-rating. It is not a game-changing marquee contract because the customer is unnamed and there is no quantified multi-year deal or hyperscaler disclosure."}, {"dr_1": 15.18, "dr_5": 15.01, "atr20": 4.67, "dr_20": 13.47, "dr_60": -9.0, "sector": "Consumer Cyclical", "ticker": "SE", "sources": ["top_1d", "volspike_gapper"], "verdict": "WATCH", "industry": "Specialty Retail", "theme_fit": 2, "market_cap": 51981562910, "rsi_mktcap": 94, "drop_reason": null, "company_name": "Sea Limited", "news_summary": "The move is being driven by Sea Limited’s Q1 2026 earnings, where revenue (~$7.1B, +46–47% Y/Y) and profitability (net income $438M, adj. EBITDA ~$1.0B) significantly outpaced expectations even though EPS missed, and Shopee posted record GMV with strength also in Monee and Garena. Coming after a period when the stock was punished to 52‑week lows despite very strong 2025 growth, the print is seen as confirming a renewed growth-and-profitability trajectory; analysts remain broadly positive, options activity has turned unusually bullish, and that combination of fundamental upside, positioning reset, and momentum screens explains the 1‑day +15% move on ~4x average volume.", "current_price": 97.13, "matched_themes": ["earnings-beat-and-raise", "turnaround-and-recovery", "breakout-and-momentum"], "momentum_score": 72.58, "verdict_claude": "WATCH", "vol_vs_10d_avg": 4.26, "composite_score": 79.03, "last_event_date": "2026-05-12", "last_event_type": "volume_spike", "news_theme_tags": ["earnings-surprise-momentum", "turnaround-and-recovery", "breakout-and-momentum"], "dropped_at_stage": null, "headline_drivers": ["Q1 2026 results: revenue ~$7.1B, +46–47% Y/Y and ~10%+ above ~$6.4B consensus; net income $438M and adj. EBITDA ~$1.0B, showing strong operating leverage despite an EPS miss vs consensus (reported May 12, 2026)", "Record Shopee GMV and broad-based strength across Shopee, Monee (fintech), and Garena, reinforcing the view that Sea’s growth engines have re-accelerated after a period of margin-focused retrenchment (commentary across earnings coverage May 12–13, 2026)", "Analyst and market reaction framing the quarter as a ‘growth is back’ print: Street still largely at Moderate Buy with targets well above the tape, plus heavy call-option activity and inclusion on momentum/volume-spike screens, helping fuel a squeeze/positioning reset (reports and options-flow notes May 12–13, 2026)", "Context from prior quarters: very strong FY 2025 / Q4 2025 revenue growth (36–38%) and net income tripling to ~$1.6B, yet the stock sold off hard on margin/future-EPS worries and even hit 52‑week lows; the Q1 2026 print is being read as validating the longer‑term growth/profitability story and unwinding some of that pessimism (Q4 2025 results and subsequent downgrades in March 2026, plus 52‑week low reports thereafter)"], "momentum_factors": {"rsi_mktcap": {"points": 15, "reason": "rsi_mktcap=94"}, "vol_vs_avg": {"points": 10, "reason": "vol 4.26x"}, "dr5_atr_norm": {"points": 9.58, "reason": "dr5=15.0% atr=4.7% norm=1.44"}, "multi_screen": {"points": 12.0, "reason": "2 screens"}, "recent_event": {"points": 20, "reason": "event 1d ago"}, "trend_alignment": {"points": 6.0, "reason": "partial (20d=13.5, 60d=-9.0)"}}, "news_materiality": 3, "theme_fit_reason": "SE fits the trader's 'earnings-beat-and-raise' and 'turnaround-and-recovery' themes: a strong beat after the stock was punished to 52-week lows on margin fears, now being read as a re-acceleration story with broad-based strength across multiple business units (Shopee, Monee, Garena). However, SE is not a natural fit for the trader's core AI infrastructure, optical/data-center, or picks-and-shovels themes that dominate their watchlist.", "has_material_news": true, "verdict_rationale": "SE has strong news materiality (3) and partial theme alignment (2), meeting the WATCH threshold but not the PICK bar. The earnings beat and turnaround narrative are solid, but the trader's stated taste signature is heavily weighted toward AI infrastructure, hyperscaler exposure, and hardware/optical plays—none of which are present in SE's e-commerce and fintech story. The 15% 1-day move and 4x volume spike are real momentum, but without a thesis from the trader linking SE to their core conviction themes, this reads as a strong earnings-driven pop in a name outside their primary hunting ground. Worth monitoring if SE develops an AI/infrastructure angle or if the trader adds it to their watchlist, but not an immediate PICK.", "last_event_magnitude": 3.97, "latest_material_date": "2026-05-13", "news_materiality_reason": "Q1 2026 revenue beat (+46–47% YoY, ~10% above consensus at $7.1B) with strong profitability (net income $438M, adj. EBITDA ~$1.0B) and record Shopee GMV represents a material earnings beat paired with a forward-looking 'growth is back' narrative that directly counters prior pessimism and validates the longer-term trajectory."}, {"dr_1": -2.96, "dr_5": 49.66, "atr20": 7.22, "dr_20": 61.93, "dr_60": 36.86, "sector": "Technology", "ticker": "CLFD", "sources": ["top_5d", "volspike_gapper"], "verdict": "WATCH", "industry": "Communication Equipment", "theme_fit": 2, "market_cap": 635037335, "rsi_mktcap": 98, "drop_reason": null, "company_name": "Clearfield, Inc.", "news_summary": "A focused search finds no evidence that Clearfield has recently been tied to any marquee buyer such as Apple, NVIDIA, TSMC, Google, Microsoft, Amazon, Meta, Tesla, OpenAI, Anthropic, major auto OEMs, or defense primes. There are also no recent (last 60–90 days) supply‑chain‑style notes or leaks from the usual hardware rumor channels (Ming‑Chi Kuo / TF International, DigiTimes) or from the specifically named brokers (Wedbush, Loop Capital, Needham, Morgan Stanley) that identify a large new design win, qualification, or mass‑production ramp with a blue‑chip customer. Likewise, a review of recent press releases, news recaps, and conference‑style commentary surfaces no patent filings or technical conference disclosures implying a hidden relationship with a marquee hyperscaler, handset OEM, or auto/defense prime.\\n\\nInstead, the identifiable fundamental drivers behind CLFD’s recent move are the May 6, 2026 Q2 FY26 earnings and guidance package and how it reframes the trajectory of the business. Clearfield reported Q2 net sales of $34.4M (slightly ahead of consensus) and a net loss per share of $0.04 (matching guidance/Street), but more importantly disclosed that order backlog rose 39% sequentially to $31.6M and that book‑to‑bill was 1.3, indicating orders materially outpaced shipments. Management reiterated full‑year FY26 revenue guidance of $160M–$170M (~10% growth at midpoint versus FY25) and EPS of $0.48–$0.62, explicitly pointing to a return to profitability. They also issued Q3 guidance for revenue of $42M–$46M (a substantial 20–30%+ sequential step‑up from Q2) and EPS of $0.17–$0.21, which, even though slightly below consensus EPS at the time, underpins a rapid earnings inflection from a small loss in Q2 to positive profits in Q3.\\n\\nCommentary in secondary sources (MarketBeat, StockTitan, MarketScreener) emphasizes this improving demand picture, the strong sequential backlog growth and book‑to‑bill, and the fact that management maintained a fairly robust full‑year EPS target despite ongoing industry caution. These notes also highlight capital return (Q2 share repurchases of $7.3M with ~$15.9M remaining on the buyback authorization) and early traction in adjacent data center markets, along with an expanded Craftsmart FiberFirst pedestal lineup, as incremental positives that may broaden Clearfield’s addressable market. The sharp stock move—particularly the big one‑day pop in the session immediately following these disclosures—is therefore best understood as the market re‑rating a small‑cap fiber/broadband supplier on clearer evidence of demand recovery, backlog strength, and an EPS inflection, amplified by technical factors and short covering, rather than as a reaction to any newly discovered mega‑customer or secret design win. In short, there is material news around guidance, backlog, and product/market positioning, but not around a specific marquee buyer engagement.", "current_price": 44.725, "matched_themes": ["turnaround-and-recovery", "earnings-beat-and-raise", "low-float-or-small-cap-catalyst", "new-product-or-platform-inflection"], "momentum_score": 92.0, "verdict_claude": "WATCH", "vol_vs_10d_avg": 2.63, "composite_score": 76.8, "last_event_date": "2026-05-11", "last_event_type": "volume_spike", "news_theme_tags": ["fiber-broadband", "datacenter-infrastructure", "smallcap-turnaround", "capital-return", "turnaround-and-recovery", "optical-networking", "data-center-infrastructure", "small-cap-relief-rally", "earnings-surprise-momentum", "low-float-or-small-cap-catalyst"], "dropped_at_stage": null, "headline_drivers": ["Fiscal Q2 2026 earnings and guidance: Clearfield reported Q2 FY26 net sales of $34.4M (slightly above consensus ~$33.6M) and EPS of -$0.04 (in line), while reiterating full‑year FY26 revenue guidance of $160M–$170M (~10% YoY growth at midpoint) and EPS of $0.48–$0.62 (company press release and earnings call, 2026-05-06; MarketBeat/StockTitan recaps 2026-05-06/07).", "Backlog / book-to-bill inflection: Q2 FY26 order backlog rose 39% sequentially to $31.6M, and book‑to‑bill was 1.3, signaling improving demand and supporting management’s call for a stronger second half and return to profitability (company press release, 2026-05-06; MarketBeat note 2026-05-06).", "Q3 FY26 guidance implying sharp sequential ramp: Management guided Q3 FY26 net sales to $42M–$46M (up ~22–34% QoQ from Q2’s $34.4M) and positive EPS of $0.17–$0.21, reinforcing a near‑term earnings inflection narrative (Clearfield guidance release / MarketScreener, MarketBeat, 2026-05-06).", "Capital return and balance sheet: Clearfield repurchased $7.3M of stock in Q2 FY26 and still has ~$15.9M buyback authorization remaining, despite maintaining net cash and no debt, which investors and commentators framed as supportive of EPS accretion and management confidence (StockTitan recap, 2026-05-07).", "Product and end‑market expansion (datacenter / Craftsmart FiberFirst): Management highlighted early traction in adjacent data center markets and announced an expansion of the Craftsmart FiberFirst pedestal product lineup with new 8‑ and 10‑inch models, broadening its addressable fiber-connectivity market beyond traditional community broadband (company commentary referenced in StockTitan article, 2026-05-07).", "Fundamental turnaround narrative after a string of better‑than‑feared quarters: Clearfield has repeatedly come in ahead of deeply cut expectations through late 2024 and early 2025 (e.g., fiscal Q1 2025 loss of $0.13 vs. –$0.31 consensus and prior‑year loss of $0.35; multiple 2023–24 quarters with double‑digit revenue upside and large EPS beats per Zacks coverage). This has reinforced a view that the post‑downcycle reset is largely behind the company and that earnings can inflect sharply from trough levels.", "Street forward‑earnings reset: MarketBeat and other aggregators show consensus EPS expected to grow ~140%+ in the coming year (from roughly $0.5 to >$1 per share), even though trailing earnings are still negative (P/E shown as –70x to –75x). The stock is effectively being re‑rated on 2026–27 recovery earnings rather than depressed trailing numbers.", "Sector and small‑cap fiber/broadband sympathy: Clearfield is a leveraged play on U.S. fiber build‑outs and community broadband. 