dham, or Morgan Stanley that introduce a new design win, program ramp, or hyperscaler attach story. Where FCEL appears, it is as part of broader commentary on fuel cells, hydrogen, or data‑center power demand rather than as the subject of a new, stock‑moving call.\\n\\nThe most concrete fundamental development remains the **March 23, 2026 introduction of FCEL’s 12.5 MW data‑center‑focused fuel‑cell power block and commentary about a sharply expanded commercial pipeline** (with a high proportion of prospective projects tied to data centers and digital infrastructure). That announcement and follow‑on coverage have fueled a narrative that FCEL is an AI‑data‑center power beneficiary. However, the disclosures stay generic at the customer level; they do not name specific hyperscalers, cloud providers, or flagship end‑markets beyond ‘data centers’ or ‘digital infrastructure.’\\n\\nGiven the absence of any newly surfaced contracts or marquee customer roadmaps, the **most honest read** is that the current price action is still primarily a **thematic/structural re‑rating plus momentum**: investors extrapolating AI‑driven power demand, mapping FCEL onto that theme (especially after the 12.5 MW data‑center block announcement), and then momentum/short‑covering flows amplifying the move. There is **no evidence** in the last 60 days of a specific, unambiguous new design win or hyperscaler partnership that would independently justify the magnitude of the recent re‑pricing.\\n\\nIf such a deal exists, it has not yet appeared in public news, analyst notes, or credible rumor channels; the market appears to be trading FCEL on **theme and technicals, not on a newly revealed marquee‑buyer contract.**", "current_price": 16.855, "matched_themes": ["ai-data-centers", "on-site-generation", "data-center-infrastructure", "new-product-or-platform-inflection", "breakout-and-momentum"], "momentum_score": 72.65, "verdict_claude": "SKIP", "vol_vs_10d_avg": 1.74, "composite_score": 59.06, "last_even