margin fell to 12.0% (−310 bps YoY), or 15.6% ex‑impairments, abandonments and amortized interest (−270 bps YoY). SG&A was $63.6M, down 6.5% YoY, but rose to 15.5% of revenue (+350 bps) due largely to the lower top line. Net new orders were 1,048 (−4.6% YoY) on a 7.2% decline in sales pace to 2.1 orders per community per month, partly offset by a 2.9% increase in average active communities to 167. A third‑party recap (QuiverQuant) pegs total Q2 revenue at $409.8M vs consensus $453.3M, with operating profit −$19.0M and net income to common −$0.9M, highlighting a miss and a swing into loss.\\n\\nThose April 30 earnings are the only clear, recent fundamental event. Since then, a second-pass review finds no additional material news: no new guidance update, no major land or M&A transactions, no notable insider buying/selling beyond routine patterns, no 13F-driven ownership shock, no high-profile analyst upgrade/downgrade, and no industry conference commentary specific to Beazer that would normally move the stock 30% in a week. There are also no rumors of large strategic partnerships (e.g., with Big Tech), and as a homebuilder BZH is not tied to the Apple/TSMC/NVIDIA-style supply-chain narratives that often drive squeezes elsewhere.\\n\\nGiven the timing (post-earnings digestion), the stock’s prior underperformance over 1–2 months, high RSI percentile, and appearance on volume-spike/gapper screens, the most plausible explanation is a positioning/technical move in a thinly followed small-cap homebuilder within a volatile, rate-sensitive sector. The price action looks like a squeeze/momentum burst off depressed levels rather than a reaction to new incremental information after the 2026-04-30 earnings release.", "current_price": 23.89, "matched_themes": ["breakout-and-momentum", "low-float-or-small-cap-catalyst"], "momentum_score": 64.72, "verdict_claude": "SKIP", "vol_vs_10d_avg": 3.06, "composite_score": 45.89, "last_event_date": "2026-05-11", "last_event_type": "volume_sp