ly‑chain revelation that the first pass missed.\\n\\nAcross news, filings, conference commentary, patent mentions, and analyst/rumor channels, there are **no credible reports in the last 60–90 days** that Apple, Microsoft, Amazon, Google, Meta, Tesla, NVIDIA, TSMC, OpenAI, Anthropic, a major auto OEM, or a defense prime has:\\n- selected FCEL for a specific data‑center, EV, or defense platform,\\n- qualified FCEL products for a named 2026–27 program, or\\n- entered into a disclosed or reliably rumored mass‑production agreement.\\n\\nSimilarly, there are **no recent FCEL‑specific notes** from Ming‑Chi Kuo, DigiTimes, Wedbush, Loop Capital, Needham, or Morgan Stanley that introduce a new design win, program ramp, or hyperscaler attach story. Where FCEL appears, it is as part of broader commentary on fuel cells, hydrogen, or data‑center power demand rather than as the subject of a new, stock‑moving call.\\n\\nThe most concrete fundamental development remains the **March 23, 2026 introduction of FCEL’s 12.5 MW data‑center‑focused fuel‑cell power block and commentary about a sharply expanded commercial pipeline** (with a high proportion of prospective projects tied to data centers and digital infrastructure). That announcement and follow‑on coverage have fueled a narrative that FCEL is an AI‑data‑center power beneficiary. However, the disclosures stay generic at the customer level; they do not name specific hyperscalers, cloud providers, or flagship end‑markets beyond ‘data centers’ or ‘digital infrastructure.’\\n\\nGiven the absence of any newly surfaced contracts or marquee customer roadmaps, the **most honest read** is that the current price action is still primarily a **thematic/structural re‑rating plus momentum**: investors extrapolating AI‑driven power demand, mapping FCEL onto that theme (especially after the 12.5 MW data‑center block announcement), and then momentum/short‑covering flows amplifying the move. There is **no evidence** in the last 60 days of a specific, unambiguous new design win or hyperscaler partnership that would independently justify the magnitude of the recent re‑pricing.\\n\\nIf such a deal exists, it has not yet appeared in public news, analyst notes, or credible rumor channels; the market appears to be trading FCEL on **theme and technicals, not on a newly revealed marquee‑buyer contract.**", "current_price": 16.855, "matched_themes": ["ai-data-centers", "on-site-generation", "data-center-infrastructure", "new-product-or-platform-inflection", "breakout-and-momentum"], "momentum_score": 72.65, "verdict_claude": "SKIP", "composite_score": 59.06, "last_event_date": "2026-05-11", "last_event_type": "gapper", "news_theme_tags": ["ai-data-centers", "on-site-generation", "hydrogen-and-fuel-cells", "clean-energy-transition", "momentum-and-short-covering", "ai-power-buildout", "data-center-infrastructure", "new-product-or-platform-inflection", "energy-security", "breakout-and-momentum", "special-situation-re-rating"], "headline_drivers": ["No credible reports in the last 60–90 days tying FuelCell Energy to marquee hyperscalers (Apple, Microsoft, Amazon, Google, Meta, Tesla, NVIDIA, TSMC, OpenAI, Anthropic, major auto OEMs, or defense primes) via design wins, qualifications, or mass‑production orders; recent coverage only references data‑center customers generically without naming top‑tier buyers.", "No recent supply‑chain or thematic notes specifically focused on FCEL from Ming‑Chi Kuo, DigiTimes, Wedbush, Loop Capital, Needham, or Morgan Stanley in the last 90 days; FCEL appears in occasional high‑level clean‑energy / hydrogen / data‑center power commentary but without new company‑specific catalysts.", "Post‑March 2026 narrative re‑rating continues around FCEL’s 12.5 MW data‑center‑oriented fuel‑cell power block and a sharply expanded pipeline; these are framed as positioning FCEL for AI‑driven data‑center power demand, but no new, nameable end‑customer program (e.g., a specific 2026–27 data‑center or EV OEM ramp) has surfaced in the last 30–60 days.", "Recent stock move appears to be driven by continued AI‑data‑center / on‑site power enthusiasm, sympathy to peers (e.g., Bloom Energy, Plug Power) and technical momentum/short covering rather than a discrete, newly discovered contract, patent partnership, or marquee design‑win disclosure.", "Massive re‑rating on AI data‑center power demand: multiple reports (Intellectia, Barchart, Zacks/Public, late April–mid April 2026) highlight that soaring electricity needs from AI/data‑center workloads are driving customers toward on‑site generation, with FuelCell Energy positioned as a key beneficiary.", "New 12.5 MW data‑center‑focused fuel‑cell platform: on Mar 23, 2026 FuelCell Energy announced a packaged 12.5 MW ‘utility‑grade power block’ solution and related manufacturing expansion plans aimed specifically at large data‑center operations.", "Explosive pipeline growth tied to data centers: management and press coverage cite ~275% growth in the business development pipeline since early 2025, with ~80%+ of it linked to data centers and digital infrastructure clients, signaling a step‑change in prospective demand.", "Sector sympathy to Bloom Energy and peers: FCEL has repeatedly gapped higher in sympathy with strong results and optimism at Bloom Energy and Plug Power (e.g., Bloom’s strong Q1 print noted around Apr 30, 2026), fueling a broad fuel‑cell/on‑site power rally.", "Momentum/technical follow‑through on prior gaps: the stock is up ~130%+ over 20 trading days with 1.7x normal volume, a 20‑day ATR ~14%, and very high RSI, indicating trend‑chasing and breakout/momentum flows amplifying the fundamental AI‑power narrative rather than any single new event in the last day."], "momentum_factors": {"rsi_mktcap": {"points": 15, "reason": "rsi_mktcap=98"}, "vol_vs_avg": {"points": 6.0, "reason": "vol 1.74x"}, "dr5_atr_norm": {"points": 4.65, "reason": "dr5=22.6% atr=14.5% norm=0.70"}, "multi_screen": {"points": 12.0, "reason": "2 scre