hich is a real fundamental catalyst rather than just price action. The large institutional stake disclosure adds a secondary validation/squeeze driver, but there is no marquee customer win or transformational contract."}, {"dr_1": 15.42, "dr_5": 13.89, "atr20": 8.42, "dr_20": 27.57, "dr_60": 41.68, "sector": "Technology", "ticker": "CRNC", "sources": ["top_1d", "volspike_gapper"], "verdict": "WATCH", "industry": "Software - Application", "theme_fit": 2, "market_cap": 496162044, "rsi_mktcap": 93, "drop_reason": null, "company_name": "Cerence Inc.", "news_summary": "The recent spike in CRNC is primarily a reaction to Cerence’s Q2 FY26 earnings on May 7, where the company beat its own guidance on revenue and adjusted EBITDA, generated strong free cash flow, and then raised midpoints for full‑year revenue, EBITDA, and FCF, sharpening the story of a turn toward sustainable profitability. That fundamental re‑rating is being layered on top of an AI/auto‑software narrative (including use of Nvidia AI frameworks) that investors are increasingly willing to pay for, so the better‑than‑feared quarter unlocked a bid in an under‑owned, previously discounted name. With volume running multiple times normal and momentum screens lighting up, technical buying and likely some short‑covering are amplifying the fundamentally driven move rather than a new single customer or regulatory headline.", "current_price": 11.15, "matched_themes": ["earnings-beat-and-raise", "margin-and-growth-inflection", "story-driven-momentum"], "momentum_score": 76.92, "verdict_claude": "WATCH", "vol_vs_10d_avg": 3.66, "composite_score": 70.77, "last_event_date": "2026-05-22", "last_event_type": "volume_spike", "news_theme_tags": ["earnings-beat-and-raise", "margin-and-growth-inflection", "ai-software-and-productivity-tools", "story-driven-momentum"], "dropped_at_stage": null, "headline_drivers": ["Stronger‑than‑expected Q2 FY26 print and guidance: revenue $64.2M and adjusted EBITDA $7.2M both above the high end of guidance, with $14.1M operating cash flow and $13.6M free cash flow; company raised midpoints for full‑year revenue, adjusted EBITDA ($60–70M, midpoint +8%) and free cash flow ($66–76M) (earnings release, 2026-05-07)", "Improving profitability and cash‑generation narrative: Q2 net income of $1.7M and reiterated/narrowed FY26 GAAP EPS range to -$0.07 to $0.15, reinforcing a turn from loss‑making to sustainable profitability (earnings release, 2026-05-07)", "Ongoing AI/auto software positioning and Nvidia-aligned narrative: Cerence has been highlighting adoption of generative AI technologies and AI platforms for automotive virtual assistants, which, combined with the solid quarter, is feeding into an AI‑software re‑rating and momentum bid (company commentary, 2025–2026; echoed in recent investor media coverage)", "Technical and positioning elements amplifying the move: high volume (≈3.7x 10‑day average), elevated RSI (93rd percentile), and the stock appearing on top 1‑day and volume‑spike screens, consistent with momentum/short‑covering behavior after a better‑than‑feared quarter in a previously beaten‑up name"], "momentum_factors": {"rsi_mktcap": {"points": 15, "reason": "rsi_mktcap=93"}, "vol_vs_avg": {"points": 10, "reason": "vol 3.66x"}, "dr5_atr_norm": {"points": 4.92, "reason": "dr5=13.9% atr=8.4% norm=0.74"}, "multi_screen": {"points": 12.0, "reason": "2 screens"}, "recent_event": {"points": 20, "reason": "event 1d ago"}, "trend_alignment": {"points": 15, "reason": "both up (20d=27.6, 60d=41.7)"}}, "news_materiality": 2, "theme_fit_reason": "The setup fits the trader’s preference for earnings-driven inflection stories where improving margins and cash generation can reset the valuation. It is only tangential to the trader’s core AI infrastructure/optical themes because Cerence is more AI software in automotive than picks-and-shovels AI buildout.", "has_material_news": true, "verdict_rationale": "Strong momentum is being backed by a legit beat-and-raise, so this is not just a chart chase. But it doesn’t hit the trader’s highest-conviction themes like hyperscaler AI deals or AI infrastructure, so it lands as a solid watch rather than a must-buy PICK.", "last_event_magnitude": 4.29, "latest_material_date": "2026-05-07", "news_materiality_reason": "This is a material beat-and-raise quarter: Cerence beat revenue and adjusted EBITDA, generated strong operating/free cash flow, and raised full-year revenue, EBITDA, and FCF guidance. It’s a real fundamental catalyst, but not a transformative named-customer or contract event."}, {"dr_1": 2.97, "dr_5": 23.82, "atr20": 14.45, "dr_20": 158.53, "dr_60": 204.87, "sector": "Communication Services", "ticker": "EVC", "sources": ["main_view", "top_20d", "volspike_gapper"], "verdict": "WATCH", "industry": "Broadcasting", "theme_fit": 2, "market_cap": 905665531, "rsi_mktcap": 99, "drop_reason": null, "company_name": "Entravision Communications Corporation", "news_summary": "The market is reacting primarily to a sharp fundamental inflection in Entravision’s ad-tech business: Q1 2026 revenue more than doubled YoY, ATS revenue tripled (~+204%), and the company flipped from a sizable loss to roughly $12M of net income, with ATS operating profit jumping to ~$34M. That earnings surprise, plus a fresh $0.05 quarterly dividend and debt paydown, triggered a violent re‑rating in a thinly traded small-cap that had been priced as a structurally challenged media asset. Since then the move has been amplified by momentum/trend-following flows: EVC has broken out to multi‑year highs, shows extremely strong relative strength (RSI ~99th percentile), and is now trading more as a growth-and-margin-inflection/momentum story than on incremental new news beyond the early-May earnings print.", "current_price": 9.695, "matched_themes": ["earnings-beat-and-raise", "margin-and-growth-inflection", "story-driven-momentum", "breakout-and-new-highs", "special-situations-with-optional-downside"], "momentum_score": 74.91, "verdict_claude": "WATCH", "vol_vs_10d_avg": 0.34, "composite_score": 69.96, "last_event_date": "2026-05-21", "last_event_type": "gapper", "news_theme_tags": ["margin-and-growth-inflection", "earnings-beat-and-raise", "story-driven-momentum", "breakout-and-new-highs", "special-situations-with-optional-downside"], "dropped_at_stage": null, "headline_drivers": ["Blowout Q1 2026 results with a major revenue and earnings inflection: consolidated net revenue ~ $196–197M, up ~114% YoY, driven by Advertising Technology & Services (ATS) net revenue up ~204% YoY to ~ $155M (company release and follow‑up coverage, 2026-05-05 to 2026-05-08).", "Return to profitability at both company and key segment level: net income attributable to common shareholders swung to roughly +$12M in Q1 2026 from a large loss a year ago; ATS segment operating profit jumped to ~$34.3M from ~$6.5M even as the legacy Media segment stayed loss‑making (Q1 2026 report, 2026-05-05).", "Capital return and balance sheet signals: Board declared a $0.05 per‑share quarterly dividend (payable 2026-06-30, record date 2026-06-16) and repaid $5M on its bank term loan, reinforcing confidence that the earnings inflection is durable (Q1 2026 report, 2026-05-05).", "Momentum and technical re‑rating in a small‑cap, previously loss‑making name: the stock has more than tripled over 12 months and ~doubled in the last month, screens as overbought (RSI in the 99th percentile), and is now a trend-following/momentum trade with relatively weak “Quality and Growth” scores per third‑party quant analysis (Benzinga/Quiver/Benzinga Edge commentary, 2026-05-06 to 2026-05-08)."], "momentum_factors": {"rsi_mktcap": {"points": 15, "reason": "rsi_mktcap=99"}, "vol_vs_avg": {"points": 0.0, "reason": "vol 0.34x"}, "dr5_atr_norm": {"points": 4.91, "reason": "dr5=23.8% atr=14.4% norm=0.74"}, "multi_screen": {"points": 20, "reason": "3 screens"}, "recent_event": {"points": 20, "reason": "event 2d ago"}, "trend_alignment": {"points": 15, "reason": "both up (20d=158.5, 60d=204.9)"}}, "news_materiality": 2, "theme_fit_reason": "This fits the trader’s appetite for earnings inflection, margin recovery, and breakout momentum with a visible catalyst. It’s not an AI-infrastructure name, but it does match the broader preference for a sharp fundamental turn translating into a re-rating.", "has_material_news": true, "verdict_rationale": "Strong momentum and a real earnings inflection make this worth tracking, but it’s outside the trader’s core AI/infrastructure theme set and there’s no marquee contract or hyperscaler-style adoption story here. The setup is more of a classic turnaround/momentum re-rate than a top-tier PICK for this trader.", "last_event_magnitude": 0.1645, "latest_material_date": "2026-05-08", "news_materiality_reason": "The Q1 2026 print is clearly material: revenue more than doubled YoY, ATS revenue tripled, the company swung back to profitability, and management added a quarterly dividend plus debt paydown. It’s a real beat/inflection, but not a game-changing named-customer or multi-year contract catalyst."}, {"dr_1": 0.3, "dr_5": 12.3, "atr20": 17.93, "dr_20": 123.41, "dr_60": 245.23, "sector": "Technology", "ticker": "WOLF", "sources": ["main_view", "top_20d", "volspike_gapper"], "verdict": "WATCH", "industry": "Semiconductors", "theme_fit": 2, "market_cap": 3373584393, "rsi_mktcap": 100, "drop_reason": null, "company_name": "Wolfspeed, Inc.", "news_summary": "A second-pass, name-by-name check over the last ~60–90 days does NOT surface a specific marquee-customer adoption or design-win headline (Apple, NVIDIA, TSMC, Tesla, major hyperscalers, defense primes, etc.) that would cleanly explain Wolfspeed’s 200%+ move. There are also no recent, widely cited supply-chain notes from the listed analysts/brokkers (Ming-Chi Kuo, DigiTimes, Wedbush, Loop Capital, Needham, Morgan Stanley) explicitly tying Wolfspeed to a flagship platform or ramp.\\n\\nInstead, the fundamental driver is a capital-structure and risk-perception reset: Wolfspeed has emerged from Chapter 11 with roughly 70% less debt and over $1.1B in cash/short-term investments, aided by nearly $700M in Advanced Manufacturing Investment Credit cash refunds and additional investment tax-credit receivables. The 10-Q/Fresh Start Accounting disclosures (for the quarter ended 2026‑03‑29) show $150.2M in revenue and a still sizable net loss, but a transformed balance sheet (positive equity of about $1.0B) and lower leverage. This materially reduces the probability of near-term insolvency or catastrophic dilution versus where equity holders stood pre-restructuring.\\n\\nOn top of that, Wolfspeed’s FQ3 2026 results came in around the midpoint of prior guidance (~$150M revenue), with non-GAAP gross margin still negative largely due to under-utilization of expanded SiC capacity. Management guided FQ4 2026 revenue to $140–160M. While this is not a growth inflection, trading commentary framed the guide as ‘stabilizing’ in a context where many feared further collapse, and emphasized management’s comments that AI data-center and industrial & energy demand should offset current EV softness. Articles like Semiconductor Today’s 2026‑05‑10 recap and StocksToTrade’s 2026‑05‑06 trading piece echo this angle, presenting Wolfspeed as a key SiC supplier levered to AI power and industrial electrification, even if customer names are not spelled out.\\n\\nThe announcement of senior hires — particularly Asia-Pacific regional president Yasuhisa Harita (based in Tokyo to drive SiC growth across Japan, Korea, and ASEAN) plus new global legal and communications leadership — reinforces a narrative that the company is preparing to scale commercial relationships and partnerships globally post-restructuring. However, no specific OEM or hyperscaler contract or qualification milestone is publicly tied to these hires yet.\\n\\nPutting this together, the move appears to be a combination of: (1) removal of existential balance-sheet risk and clarification of large government support; (2) confirmation that core revenue is holding roughly in guidance range while the company positions for AI and industrial power demand; and (3) technical dynamics from high short interest and momentum trading. After searching specifically for marquee design wins, supply-chain leaks, patent links, or named roadmap tie-ins, none emerges as the smoking-gun catalyst. The re-rating is primarily about survival, optionality on SiC demand (especially for AI-related power infrastructure), and a violent unwind of a heavily shorted, previously distressed equity rather than a newly disclosed anchor customer.", "current_price": 69.77, "matched_themes": ["special-situations-with-optional-downside", "ai-infrastructure-enablers", "power-semiconductors"], "momentum_score": 72.05, "verdict_claude": "WATCH", "vol_vs_10d_avg": 0.8, "composite_score": 68.82, "last_event_date": "2026-05-21", "last_event_type": "gapper", "news_theme_tags": ["power-semiconductors", "sic-materials-and-devices", "restructuring-and-balance-sheet", "ai-data-center-infrastructure", "short-squeeze-and-momentum", "special-situations-with-optional-downside", "risk-on-meme-and-retail-speculation", "power-and-wide-bandgap", "ai-infrastructure-enablers", "short-covering-in-underowned-cyclicals"], "dropped_at_stage": null, "headline_drivers": ["Balance-sheet reset and exit from Chapter 11 with ~70% debt reduction, $1.16–1.2B cash and large U.S. manufacturing tax-credit refunds, eliminating near‑term solvency/dilution overhang (10‑Q / fresh-start accounting disclosure and related coverage, filed for quarter ended 2026‑03‑29; StockTitan summary posted early May 2026)", "Fiscal Q3 2026 print roughly in-line with guidance (~$150.2M revenue vs $140–160M guided) and Q4 2026 revenue guidance of $140–160M framed positively by traders as ‘stable to improving’ amid sector AI/data‑center strength (company release and investor materials; Semiconductor Today recap 2026‑05‑10; StocksToTrade note 2026‑05‑06)", "Management commentary and sell‑side/retail framing that AI data centers and industrial & energy (I&E) demand will offset softness in EVs, positioning Wolfspeed’s SiC capacity as a levered play on AI power infrastructure (Semiconductor Today article summarizing Q3 2026 call, 2026‑05‑10; secondary trading commentary early May 2026)", "New senior leadership hires seen as reinforcing a ‘go‑to‑market and global scaling’ narrative — appointment of Yasuhisa Harita as Asia‑Pacific regional president plus additions of Brad Kohn (EVP, Chief Legal and Global Affairs Officer) and Sonja Burfeind (VP Communications) to support expansion in Japan/Korea/ASEAN and global partnerships (company announcement echoed in StocksToTrade recap, 2026‑05‑06)", "Massive re‑rating from terminal/bankruptcy risk to ‘cleaner balance sheet + AI/SiC option value’ interacting with prior extreme short interest, driving a sustained squeeze and momentum chase rather than a single discrete customer win (visible in +200%+ 60‑day move; narrative in restructuring and trading commentary through April–May 2026)", "Balance-sheet and liquidity developments tied to U.S. incentives and tax credits: company disclosures and follow‑up coverage highlighted ~$1.3B projected cash by end of FY Q3 2025, including ~$192M of Section 48D advanced manufacturing tax-credit refunds already received and a path toward ~$1B total credits plus potential CHIPS Act funding (company update 2025‑03‑28; reiterated in earnings/tax-credit commentary through spring 2025).", "Capital-structure overhang and bankruptcy fears: a late‑May 2025 company update on refinancing 2026 convertible notes and the possibility of Chapter 11 drove a one‑day 52% collapse (to ~$2.6), making WOLF the most shorted stock in the U.S. and setting up an extreme short‑interest situation (WSJ report and company commentary, late May 2025).", "Speculative squeeze and restructuring narrative: subsequent headlines and analysis framed Wolfspeed as a special-situation restructuring story rather than a near‑term terminal risk, with management working on convert restructuring and emphasizing ample liquidity and federal support; that shift in perception, plus ultra‑high short interest, triggered repeated short‑covering spikes and meme‑style flows (spring–summer 2025).", "CFO transition and formal restructuring plan: announcement of Gregor van Issum as incoming CFO and a preplanned Chapter 11 restructuring to wipe out ~$4.6B of debt and cut interest expense ~60% reframed the story as a de‑leveraging rather than a liquidation, sending the stock up ~95% in one session and another 25–40% pre‑market the next day (CFO announcement and restructuring outline, 2025‑07‑08)."], "momentum_factors": {"rsi_mktcap": {"points": 15, "reason": "rsi_mktcap=100"}, "vol_vs_avg": {"points": 0.0, "reason": "vol 0.80x"}, "dr5_atr_norm": {"points": 2.05, "reason": "dr5=12.3% atr=17.9% norm=0.31"}, "multi_screen": {"points": 20, "reason": "3 screens"}, "recent_event": {"points": 20, "reason": "event 2d ago"}, "trend_alignment": {"points": 15, "reason": "both up (20d=123.4, 60d=245.2)"}}, "news_materiality": 2, "theme_fit_reason": "This fits the trader’s special-situation and AI infrastructure interests because Wolfspeed is being re-rated as a SiC power-play on AI data-center and industrial energy demand. It is thematic, but more as a distressed-to-surviving optionality story than the trader’s preferred named hyperscaler-deal or optical-style inflection.", "has_material_news": true, "verdict_rationale": "Strong momentum is there, but the news is still mainly a balance-sheet reset plus short-squeeze dynamics, not the kind of named customer, backlog, or earnings inflection this trader typically upgrades to a PICK. Consistent with the trader’s taste, the AI/power-infrastructure angle helps, but the lack of a concrete adoption or contract catalyst keeps it below a true buyable theme-confirmation.", "last_event_magnitude": 0.2099, "latest_material_date": "2026-05-10", "news_materiality_reason": "The Chapter 11 exit, ~70% debt reduction, and >$1B liquidity/tax-credit support are a real capital-structure reset, and the Q3 print/guidance confirms the business is still operating rather than collapsing. It is material, but it is not a new marquee customer win or transformational product adoption story."}, {"dr_1": 12.04, "dr_5": 2.78, "atr20": 5.95, "dr_20": 14.57, "dr_60": 18.2, "sector": "Consumer Defensive", "ticker": "UTI", "sources": ["top_1d", "volspike_gapper"], "verdict": "WATCH", "industry": "Education & Training Services", "theme_fit": 2, "market_cap": 2205758923, "rsi_mktcap": 84, "drop_reason": null, "company_name": "Universal Technical Institute, Inc.", "news_summary": "The move in UTI is being driven less by a single new headline and more by a re‑rating of the growth story after a strong earnings print and higher forward expectations. Recent results beat expectations and management’s guidance points to solid revenue growth and expanding EBITDA into 2025, leading analysts to lift fair‑value targets into the high‑30s to low‑40s (e.g., ~$42.50) and frame >20% upside from earlier prices. As that fundamental narrative has spread, the stock has broken out on a large volume spike (over 3x normal), which has pulled in momentum and technical buyers and amplified the 1‑day and multi‑week gains. There does not appear to be a specific new contract or regulatory win; the move is a combination of earnings/guidance‑driven re‑rating plus technical follow‑through.", "current_price": 40.66, "matched_themes": ["earnings-beat-and-raise", "margin-and-growth-inflection", "story-driven-momentum", "breakout-and-new-highs"], "momentum_score": 68.89, "verdict_claude": "WATCH", "vol_vs_10d_avg": 3.08, "composite_score": 67.56, "last_event_date": "2026-05-22", "last_event_type": "volume_spike", "news_theme_tags": ["margin-and-growth-inflection", "earnings-beat-and-raise", "story-driven-momentum", "breakout-and-new-highs"], "dropped_at_stage": null, "headline_drivers": ["Street research has turned more bullish post-earnings, with at least one detailed note lifting its 12‑month price target from the high‑30s to about $42.50 and others emphasizing >20% upside from recent levels based on higher revenue, margin, and P/E assumptions (mid‑May 2026, various broker/independent notes summarized on SimplyWallSt).", "Management’s latest guidance and commentary imply strong multi‑year growth: revenue guided to roughly $830–$835M and adjusted EBITDA to about $124M for 2025, reinforcing a margin and growth inflection story in a still‑underpenetrated career/technical education market (guidance reiterated/updated with recent earnings).", "Recent earnings came in ahead of expectations and were accompanied by an upbeat 2024 outlook, which has prompted valuation re‑rating arguments (e.g., target prices in the mid‑ to high‑$30s implying >20% upside from pre‑move levels).", "Despite some risk flags (lower YoY profit margin, recent insider selling), the stock has been picked up by momentum screens after the volume spike and strong 1‑day move, pulling in technical/momentum capital as it breaks out near new highs on 3x normal volume (volume spike event 2026‑05‑22)."], "momentum_factors": {"rsi_mktcap": {"points": 10.5, "reason": "rsi_mktcap=84"}, "vol_vs_avg": {"points": 10, "reason": "vol 3.08x"}, "dr5_atr_norm": {"points": 1.39, "reason": "dr5=2.8% atr=6.0% norm=0.21"}, "multi_screen": {"points": 12.0, "reason": "2 screens"}, "recent_event": {"points": 20, "reason": "event 1d ago"}, "trend_alignment": {"points": 15, "reason": "both up (20d=14.6, 60d=18.2)"}}, "news_materiality": 2, "theme_fit_reason": "UTI fits the trader’s earnings-beat-and-raise and margin-and-growth-inflection preferences because the move is being driven by a real fundamental re-rating, not just chart action. It is only a moderate thematic fit rather than a perfect one, since this is education/services rather than one of the trader’s core