AXTI	26	Need PULLBACK\n---\n\n## ✅ PROS\n\n**1. Pure-play AI infrastructure story with real revenue proof**\nThe core thesis is straightforward: AI data centers need faster optical connections, and AXTI makes the indium phosphide (InP) substrates those links run on. Management reports InP demand at record levels, with a backlog above $100M — massive for a company doing ~$27M/quarter in revenue.\n\n**2. Earnings inflection is real**\nQ1 2026 revenue hit $26.9M, up from $19.4M a year ago, with non-GAAP gross margins surging to 29.9% from 21.5% the prior quarter — a sharp recovery from negative margins just a year ago. InP alone contributed $13.6M.\n\n**3. Guidance shocked the Street to the upside**\nAXTI guided Q2 EPS to $0.06–$0.08 while analysts were modeling a small loss — not just a beat, but a narrative flip from "money-losing materials supplier" to "early-stage profitable AI enabler."\n\n**4. $632.5M war chest to scale capacity**\nThe $632.5M raise is aimed at ramping Beijing Tongmei's InP capacity and pushing next-gen 6-inch wafer technology — exactly what AXTI needs if AI data center demand keeps surging.\n\n**5. Analyst support with upside targets**\nWedbush targets $80, Northland $90, B. Riley $72 — all citing the AI infrastructure theme. *(Note: stock has since blown past all of these, trading ~$124.)*\n\n---\n\n## ❌ CONS\n\n**1. Valuation is extreme**\nAXTI trades at a price-to-sales multiple above 70x and price-to-book above 25x, while the company is still loss-making at the net income level with a Q1 net loss of ~$1.5M.\n\n**2. Massive dilution from the equity raise**\nAXTI raised ~$632.5M through a common-stock offering at $64.25 — sparking sharp, dilution-driven selloffs, and the stock now trades nearly 2x that offering price, meaning early buyers in the deal have already exited with big gains.\n\n**3. Heavy China exposure / geopolitical risk**\nAXTI faces significant risks from heavy China exposure, including export permit delays and regulatory uncertainties, which have already impacted recent quarterly results and could persist. Manufacturing is concentrated at Beijing Tongmei.\n\n**4. Insider selling flagged**\nA Seeking Alpha bear note (written at $70) cited insider selling as a concern alongside the unsustainable valuation. The stock has since nearly doubled from there.\n\n**5. Momentum-driven, high volatility**\nAXTI ripped from the high-$70s on April 30 to ~$123–124 by May 15 — explosive moves in both directions are the norm. The stock previously dropped 12% in a single session tied to a valuation-driven analyst downgrade — a reminder that momentum names unwind fast.\n\n**6. Still pre-profitability at net level**\nThe stock trades at ~140x 2027 earnings estimates with a multi-billion dollar market cap on sub-$200M projected 2027 revenues.\n\n---\n\n## Bottom Line\n\nAXTI is a legitimate AI infrastructure story with improving fundamentals and a massive demand backlog — but at ~$124 it's priced for perfection after a **+600%+ YTD move**. The risk/reward is asymmetric: any stumble on China export permits, a miss on Q2 guidance, or broader AI multiple compression could cut the stock sharply. Best suited for risk-tolerant investors with a high conviction view on InP demand and tolerance for violent swings. Not a value play by any definition.\n\n*Not financial advice — always do your own due diligence.*	2026-05-16 11:22:19.601919-04	2026-05-17 14:36:19.780795-04
AGL	27	AVOID  - value based care earnings surprise\n\n## Agilon Health (NYSE: AGL) — What It Does & Why It's Exploding\n\n### What the Company Does\nAgilon Health transforms how doctors care for seniors by shifting financial incentives from volume to outcomes. It provides a platform that helps primary care physicians transition to value-based care models for Medicare patients through long-term partnerships and global capitation arrangements. Essentially, instead of doctors getting paid per visit, they get paid to keep patients *healthy* — Agilon handles the financial and operational infrastructure to make that work.\n\n---\n\n### Why the Stock Is Surging (~+150% in a Week)\n\nIt's a rare "perfect storm" of catalysts all hitting at once:\n\n**1. Massive Earnings Beat (Q1 2026)**\nRevenue came in at $1.42B vs. estimates of $1.38B, EPS of $1.80 crushed the consensus of $0.83, and Adjusted EBITDA of $53.84M beat estimates of $36.15M by nearly 49%.\n\n**2. Guidance Raised Significantly**\nThe company raised its 2026 Adjusted EBITDA guidance to $10–$40M (from the prior ($15)–$15M range), while also lifting medical margin guidance to $350–$400M.\n\n**3. Improving Margins**\nMedical margin — the key metric for Agilon — increased +16% to $149M, up from $128M a year ago, and gross profit rose +28% to $65M. Net income was $49M, a substantial turnaround from $12M in Q1 2025.\n\n**4. Analyst Upgrades Piled On**\nDeutsche Bank upgraded AGL to Buy (raising its price target to $49), and Jefferies also upgraded to Buy. The combination of a historic earnings beat, a sweeping guidance raise, multiple analyst upgrades, and the commencement of new CEO leadership on the same day created a rare confluence of positive catalysts.\n\n**5. New CEO + Growth Mode**\nTim O'Rourke stepped in as CEO, with Ronald A. Williams remaining Chairman, marking a shift from turnaround mode to a growth-focused phase in value-based care.\n\n---\n\n### A Word of Caution\nDespite the euphoric rally, InvestingPro analysis suggests the stock may be overvalued at current levels, and the company appears on InvestingPro's Most Overvalued stocks list. Its average adjusted operating margin has been negative 4% over the last five years, so profitability at scale is still unproven. This is high-momentum, high-risk territory — not a settled blue-chip story yet.	2026-05-16 23:23:45.83772-04	2026-05-16 23:23:45.83772-04
BRUN	28	**Boost Run, Inc. (Nasdaq: BRUN)** is another young "neocloud" AI infrastructure company — similar in category to SharonAI Holdings (SHAZ) that we discussed earlier — based in Northbrook, Illinois, founded September 2023.\n\n**What it does:** Boost Run rents out AI/high-performance computing infrastructure. It's an **NVIDIA Preferred Cloud Partner** that has also achieved **NVIDIA Exemplar Cloud status** on the NVIDIA Blackwell architecture (essentially a badge of approval showing NVIDIA considers Boost Run's infrastructure deployment best-in-class for its latest chips). Its platform provides:\n- **GPU compute** — dedicated physical servers with NVIDIA GPUs for training/running AI models\n- **CPU nodes**\n- **Managed Kubernetes orchestration** — automated tools for managing containerized applications at scale\n- **Shared storage**\n\nIt delivers all this through a management console and API layer, letting companies provision and scale computing resources across thousands of nodes in minutes rather than building their own data centers. In plain terms: Boost Run rents powerful, purpose-built computers to companies building AI, making expensive, specialized infrastructure accessible without massive upfront capital investment. It currently operates six U.S. data centers and is building five more, targeting total accessible capacity of over 125 MW.\n\n**How it went public:** Boost Run took the SPAC route — it merged with a blank-check company (Willow Lane Acquisition, led by Luke Weil) on May 8, 2026, generating $114.1 million in net proceeds, and began trading on Nasdaq May 11, 2026.\n\n**Why the stock is up huge:**\n\n1. **A strong debut on genuinely large contracted revenue:**\nBoost Run began trading with $940 million in long-term contracted customer revenue already secured, with average contract terms of about three years and a significant portion already in production — unusually strong committed backlog for a brand-new public company, which gave investors real visibility into future revenue rather than just a growth story.\n\n2. **Major customer deals reinforced the AI infrastructure narrative:**\nIn May 2026, Boost Run announced a multi-year GPU service agreement with **Thinking Machines Lab Inc.** worth approximately $471.7 million over an initial 36-month term, providing 5,000 NVIDIA B300 GPUs and related cloud infrastructure. This single deal reportedly added 50% to the company's backlog and drove a 41.8% single-day stock surge on its own.\n\n3. **A $1.44 billion purchase agreement with Dell Technologies** to expand capacity, further validating the scale of Boost Run's build-out plans to meet enterprise AI demand.\n\n4. **A strong first earnings report as a public company (mid-August 2026):**\nFor the quarter ended June 30, 2026, Boost Run reported total revenue of $31.1 million, up 270% year-over-year, with total long-term contracted revenue (TCV) reaching $1.9 billion — more than $1 billion of that signed in new contracts during just this one quarter. Shares jumped nearly 16–18% on this news.\n\n5. **New infrastructure expansion announced alongside earnings:**\nBoost Run finalized a long-term lease with 10X Infrastructure Partners for GPU infrastructure across multiple sites — an initial 20 MW at an existing site (delivering Q4 2026), with plans to expand to 111 MW across two more sites during 2027. Using an already-energized existing facility should let it deploy GPUs faster than building new data centers from scratch.\n\n6. **Capital structure cleanup:**\nBoost Run also received over $45 million in gross proceeds from public warrant exercises, reducing warrant overhang and simplifying its capital structure — a housekeeping move that removes some dilution uncertainty and signals management is actively managing the post-SPAC structure well.\n\n7. **Bullish analyst and full-year guidance backdrop:**\n2026 revenue guidance sits at $170–180 million, underpinned by substantial prepaid contracts and customer deposits — giving more confidence than a typical early-stage growth story, since much of that revenue is already contracted rather than hoped-for.\n\n**What the market is excited about, in one line:** Boost Run is riding the same AI infrastructure buildout wave as names like SharonAI and CoreWeave, but with an unusually strong differentiator for such a young company — nearly $2 billion in contracted, multi-year revenue locked in with credible customers (including a major AI lab), which gives investors real visibility into future cash flows instead of just speculative growth potential.\n\n**Important caution flags:**\n- This is a very young, small-cap, unprofitable company — operating loss was $2.67 million for the quarter ended March 31, 2026, a reversal from a small profit a year earlier, reflecting heavy investment ahead of revenue ramping.\n- Valuation is rich relative to current revenue — Simply Wall St's analysis specifically flags this, noting that further upside depends on Boost Run extending growth well beyond its 2026 targets.\n- Customer concentration is a real risk — with deals like the Thinking Machines Lab contract making up such a large share of backlog, any issue with a single major customer could have an outsized impact.\n- As a recent SPAC/de-SPAC name in the hottest possible sector (AI infrastructure), BRUN carries the same kind of extreme volatility and "story stock" dynamics you've seen in SHAZ and other neocloud names — worth treating with the same caution around position sizing given how much of the valuation already prices in very optimistic future execution.	2026-06-14 15:57:59.868906-04	2026-08-16 21:50:55.074968-04
SWMR	29	**Swarmer, Inc (NASDAQ: SWMR)** is a fascinating and very speculative story. Here's the rundown:\n\n---\n\n**What does it do?**\n\nSwarmer is a provider of autonomous drone swarm software and AI solutions, specializing in vendor-agnostic technologies that address critical operational challenges faced by modern military forces. Based in Austin, Texas and only incorporated in 2023, it has three main products:\n\n- **STYX** — an AI command & control system for operators to manage drone fleets in real time\n- **MINAS** — autonomy and collaboration AI that coordinates swarms of drones from multiple different manufacturers simultaneously\n- **TRIDENT** — an embedded drone operating system providing mesh networking, military-grade encryption, and a standardized software layer so any drone can plug into the Swarmer ecosystem\n\nThe key word is **vendor-agnostic** — they don't make drones, they make the software "brain" that controls any drone.\n\n---\n\n**What's the story?**\n\nSwarmer has supported more than 100,000 real-world combat missions in Ukraine — that's a real-world proving ground that most defense startups can only dream of. Swarmer was selected to lead development of a deployable drone interceptor system, and the Trump administration is pursuing funding deals with a group of drone companies as part of its effort to increase domestic production and lower costs of the increasingly vital weapons.\n\n---\n\n**The stock itself**\n\nThis is a very young, very volatile, very speculative stock. It IPO'd at $5.00 per share in March 2026, raising approximately $14.7 million. It hit an all-time low of $11.25 on March 17, 2026 and an all-time high of $68.97 on April 2, 2026. That's an enormous move in a matter of weeks.\n\nThe financials are razor thin — revenue for the last quarter was only about $20,000, and the company is losing money. This is essentially a bet on the future of autonomous drone warfare and US defense spending, not on current earnings.\n\n---\n\n**Bottom line:** It's a pure momentum/speculative play riding the drone warfare boom. The real-world Ukraine deployment gives it credibility most tiny defense startups lack, but with $20K in quarterly revenue and a market cap in the hundreds of millions, you're paying almost entirely for the story and the potential — not the fundamentals.	2026-06-14 16:38:45.618281-04	2026-06-14 16:38:45.618281-04
