HTFL	43	**HeartFlow (Nasdaq: HTFL)** is an AI-powered medical diagnostics company based in San Francisco, focused on coronary artery disease (the leading cause of death worldwide). It went public in August 2025.\n\n**What it does:** HeartFlow's platform takes a standard non-invasive heart scan (a coronary CT angiography, or CCTA) and uses AI to transform it into a personalized 3D model of a patient's coronary arteries. Doctors use this to diagnose and plan treatment for coronary artery disease without needing more invasive procedures. Its core products:\n- **Roadmap Analysis** — reads the CCTA scan and helps with early detection of coronary artery disease\n- **FFRct Analysis** — assesses blood flow through the arteries to determine whether a patient would benefit from revascularization (like a stent or bypass), without an invasive catheter-based test\n- **Plaque Analysis** — quantifies and characterizes arterial plaque to help assess a patient's risk and guide disease management\n- Newer tools: **PCI Navigator** (helps plan stent procedures) and an upcoming **Plaque Tracker** (due 2027)\n\nEssentially, HeartFlow replaces or supplements invasive diagnostic procedures with AI-driven image analysis — a big deal in cardiology because it can reduce unnecessary invasive testing while improving diagnostic accuracy. A notable reimbursement win: UnitedHealthcare agreed to cover Plaque Analysis across all its lines of business (commercial, Medicare, etc.), which matters enormously for adoption since insurance coverage is often the gating factor for new medical technology.\n\n**Why the stock is up huge — another genuine beat-and-raise earnings story, its second in a row:**\n\n1. **A strong Q2 2026 earnings beat (reported August 13, 2026):**\nHeartFlow posted an adjusted loss of 7 cents per share, handily beating Wall Street's expected loss of 13 cents — a 63% beat. Total revenue reached $64.1 million, up 48% year-over-year, driven by strong adoption in the U.S. market, where revenue grew 51% to $59.6 million on higher case volumes in FFRCT and Plaque diagnostics.\n\n2. **Margins expanded sharply on AI-driven efficiency:**\nGross margin expanded to 83.0% on a GAAP basis (83.3% non-GAAP), up 770 basis points, driven by AI-powered operational efficiency and increased procedure volume — the Plaque business specifically hit $7.8 million in revenue, $4 million above expectations, with record physician orders.\n\n3. **Guidance raised meaningfully — this was the real catalyst:**\nManagement raised full-year 2026 revenue guidance to $246–250 million (40–42% growth), a big step up from the prior forecast of 29–32% growth, and lifted the adjusted gross margin target to 82% from 81%. The CEO's framing — "record gross margin and improving operating leverage demonstrate the increasing scalability of our model" — is exactly the kind of statement that gets a growth-stock market excited, since it signals the business isn't just growing revenue, it's proving it can scale profitably.\n\n4. **This is the second consecutive quarter of beat-and-raise:**\nQ1 2026 (reported in May) also beat expectations with 41% revenue growth and raised guidance — so this pattern of consistently beating and raising has materially strengthened investor conviction that the growth is durable, not a one-off.\n\n5. **The stock move was dramatic and sustained:**\nShares jumped roughly 31–36% intraday, breaking out from a range in the mid-$20s to high-$20s up into the $38–41 zone, briefly touching new highs within its 52-week range of $20.13–$41.22.\n\n6. **Broad analyst support:**\n8 analysts rate it "Buy" on average (some sources note a "Strong Buy" consensus), with Baird initiating coverage with an Outperform rating, though a couple of analysts like Canaccord actually trimmed price targets slightly (to $37 from $43) even while maintaining Buy ratings — a sign some are being cautious about how much of the good news is now priced in after such a big move.\n\n**What the market is excited about, in one line:** HeartFlow just proved — for the second straight quarter — that its AI-driven cardiac diagnostics platform is scaling fast, profitably, and with real clinical/payer validation (the UnitedHealthcare coverage win being a key proof point), turning a story about "promising but unprofitable medtech" into "AI + healthcare + provable operating leverage," which is one of the most reliable multiple-expansion combinations in this market right now.\n\n**Worth noting:** HeartFlow is still deeply unprofitable (operating expenses of about $71.1 million against $64.1 million in revenue that quarter) and this is a genuinely volatile, high-beta name — the stock saw wide intraday swings even on the big up-day (opening around $39, dropping to about $36, then ripping back to close near $40.60), and there's an active patent infringement lawsuit against a competitor, Cleerly, in the same AI-cardiac-imaging space, adding some legal/competitive uncertainty to the long-term picture. A CEO stock sale on August 10 (about 22,562 shares under a pre-scheduled 10b5-1 plan) was reported but appears to be routine rather than a red flag.	2026-08-16 21:22:11.869723-04	2026-08-16 21:22:11.869723-04
