FSLY	45	**Fastly (Nasdaq: FSLY)** is an edge cloud computing company based in San Francisco, founded in 2011.\n\n**What it does:** Fastly runs a global "edge cloud" network — a distributed set of servers positioned close to end users around the world — that helps websites and apps load faster, handle security threats, and process data in real time. Its unified platform integrates three main things:\n- **Content delivery network (CDN)** — caches and delivers website/app content from servers close to users, reducing latency\n- **Edge computing** — lets developers run code at the network edge (close to users) rather than in a centralized data center, useful for real-time processing\n- **Web security services** — protects websites/apps from bot attacks, DDoS attacks, and other threats at the network edge\n\nIt competes directly with **Cloudflare (NET)** and, in the CDN space, with legacy players like Akamai. Its customers are typically large enterprises with high-traffic websites and apps — media companies, e-commerce platforms, gaming companies — that need speed, reliability, and security at scale.\n\n**Why the stock is up huge — this is a genuine, delayed earnings-reaction rally with an interesting mechanic:**\n\n1. **A solid Q2 2026 earnings report (August 5, 2026) that the market initially shrugged off:**\nRevenue rose to $183.32 million (up 23.3% year-over-year), and net loss narrowed to $15.59 million. Management raised full-year 2026 revenue guidance to $732–746 million (midpoint ~$739 million) — but notably, when this was first reported, Fastly stock initially traded roughly flat.\n\n2. **The real move came days later, as a "delayed reaction":**\nOn August 10, 2026, Fastly stock surged 19–21% in a single session, one of its largest single-day rallies of the year, pushing shares to their highest levels since early 2024. This wasn't triggered by new company-specific news — it was described as "the market catching up," helped by fresh analyst target hikes and a broad rally across AI-cloud names.\n\n3. **Analyst target hikes reinforced the move:**\nKeyBanc raised its target to $30 from $27, RBC Capital raised to $25 from $18, Piper Sandler raised to $28 from $27, and Evercore ISI reiterated an Outperform rating with a $32 target.\n\n4. **A valuation-gap narrative is fueling bullishness:**\nCoverage specifically highlighted that Fastly trades at roughly 6x price-to-sales versus Cloudflare's roughly 49x — a huge valuation gap for two companies in a similar space — and bulls are betting that gap narrows as Fastly's growth and margin story improves. In a market where AI-driven demand for edge compute and real-time data processing is a hot theme, a "cheap" alternative to the category leader is an easy pitch.\n\n5. **Broader sector tailwind — a sympathy rally across "AI-cloud" names:**\nThe move happened alongside a broader bounce in AI-infrastructure-adjacent cloud stocks that day, including Datadog rebounding 9% after its own post-earnings selloff. So part of Fastly's pop reflects generalized risk-on sentiment returning to this group of stocks, not purely Fastly-specific news.\n\n6. **New financing flexibility:**\nFastly also filed a new universal shelf registration covering debt, preferred stock, common stock, and warrants — giving it flexibility to raise capital in the future if needed, which some read as a sign of management's confidence in funding continued growth investment.\n\n**What the market is excited about, in one line:** Fastly is being framed as an underappreciated, deeply discounted way to play the same "edge compute + AI-driven real-time data processing" theme that's driven Cloudflare's much richer valuation — narrowing revenue losses, accelerating growth, and raised guidance gave the market a concrete reason to finally re-rate a stock that had been trading far below its larger rival on a sales-multiple basis.\n\n**Worth flagging — some genuine caution signs:**\n- Fastly remains unprofitable, with negative operating and net margins, and multiple valuation models (GF Value) flag the stock as significantly overvalued relative to fundamentals even after accounting for the growth story — one estimate puts it at roughly 150–175% overvalued on that particular model.\n- There's been notable insider selling: a director's stock sale drew attention around August 10–11, and insiders sold about $5.8 million worth of shares in the trailing three months with no offsetting insider buying.\n- Not every analyst is chasing the rally — Piper Sandler actually lowered its target to $27 from $30 even while staying constructive, and Craig-Hallum and Oppenheimer both maintained Hold ratings through the move — a reminder that this rally has real skeptics even as momentum has been strong.	2026-08-16 21:26:43.503495-04	2026-08-16 21:26:43.503495-04
AEHR	46	**Aehr Test Systems (Nasdaq: AEHR)** is a small-cap semiconductor equipment maker based in Fremont, California — and it's had one of the most explosive runs on the market in 2026.\n\n**What it does:** Aehr makes specialized burn-in and test equipment used by chip manufacturers to stress-test semiconductors under extreme conditions (heat, voltage, current) before they ship, to catch defects and ensure reliability — critical for chips going into high-stakes applications where failure isn't an option. Its main product line is the **FOX-XP** system, a multi-wafer burn-in/test platform. Historically, Aehr's biggest market was **silicon carbide (SiC)** power semiconductors used in electric vehicles (SiC chips handle EV power conversion more efficiently than traditional silicon). More recently, it's expanded into **silicon photonics** — testing optical chips used for high-speed data transmission — which is exactly what's driving the current excitement.\n\n**Why the stock is up so much — a genuine, dramatic AI-infrastructure-driven earnings story:**\n\n1. **A blowout fiscal Q4 2026 earnings report (reported August 3, 2026):**\nAehr swung to a profit of $0.11 EPS versus a year-ago loss and a consensus expecting roughly breakeven-to-a-small-loss — a dramatic beat. Revenue came in at $18.8 million, up 33% year-over-year, and the company posted record quarterly bookings of $60.7 million, pushing its effective backlog to roughly $100.6 million — a huge number relative to the company's size.\n\n2. **A specific new order was the real spark — silicon photonics for AI data centers:**\nOn August 4, Aehr announced a follow-on production order from its lead silicon photonics customer for a fully automated FOX-XP multi-wafer system, expected to ship in the first half of 2027. Management framed this as evidence that silicon photonics testing is moving "from technology adoption to manufacturing scale-up" — in plain terms, a major AI/hyperscale customer isn't just trialing Aehr's equipment anymore, they're buying it for real production volume. That single order announcement alone sent shares up 57% in the days that followed.\n\n3. **Aehr has become one of the purest public plays on optical interconnects for AI:**\nAs AI data centers increasingly rely on optical I/O and high-speed interconnects (silicon photonics) instead of traditional copper, Aehr sits in a unique position — it's a small company but nearly a "pure play" way to bet on this specific, fast-growing niche of AI infrastructure, which is part of why the stock reacts so violently to single order headlines.\n\n4. **Diversification via a silicon carbide (EV) recovery:**\nOn top of the AI story, Aehr also received more than $8 million in new silicon carbide burn-in orders tied to EV programs in China and a top-two automaker — reinforcing that its older core business (EV power semiconductors) is recovering too, not just the new AI-driven photonics business.\n\n5. **Analysts scrambled to raise targets aggressively:**\nLake Street doubled its target to $110, Craig-Hallum pushed to $125 (arguing revenue could nearly triple by fiscal 2027 on AI data center demand), and Freedom Broker upgraded to Buy with a $110 target, calling fiscal 2026 the "long-awaited growth inflection." Even more recently, Jefferies initiated coverage with a Buy rating and a $175 price target on August 14, calling its own model conservative.\n\n6. **The scale of the move is extraordinary:**\nThe stock went from around $72 in mid-July to briefly topping $133 by mid-August — and year-to-date, AEHR is up roughly 426–510%, one of the biggest gainers on the entire market in 2026.\n\n**What the market is excited about, in one line:** Aehr just proved its bet on silicon photonics testing equipment for AI data centers is transitioning from early-adopter trials to real production-scale orders — turning a formerly EV-dependent, historically unprofitable niche equipment maker into a genuine, high-margin beneficiary of the AI infrastructure buildout, right as it flipped to actual GAAP profitability with real operating leverage.\n\n**Important caution flags — this is an extremely high-risk, speculative stock right now:**\n- Valuation is stretched: the stock trades at a price-to-sales ratio around 69, extremely rich even for a hot growth name.\n- Insiders have been selling into strength — director Howard T. Slayen sold 20,000 shares at $108.30 on August 4, and there's been repeated coverage of "insiders quietly cashing in" on recent gains.\n- Aehr filed an omnibus shelf registration in July, giving it flexibility to raise capital (potentially dilutive to existing shareholders down the line).\n- The company depends heavily on a small handful of large customers — its lead hyperscale AI customer plays an outsized role, meaning any change in that single relationship could hit the stock hard.\n- Photonics/optics names broadly have been extremely volatile all summer, swinging on hyperscaler capex commentary and shifting expectations — this is a stock that can move 20-30%+ in a day in either direction on a single headline.\n\nThis is squarely in "high-risk momentum/story stock" territory rather than a stable value investment — worth sizing any position (if you were considering one) accordingly given how much of the current price already reflects very optimistic future growth assumptions.	2026-08-16 21:37:52.669302-04	2026-08-16 21:37:52.669302-04
BLZE	47	**Backblaze (Nasdaq: BLZE)** is a San Francisco-based cloud storage company, founded in 2007, that's been steadily riding an AI-storage narrative into an explosive rally.\n\n**What it does:** Backblaze provides cloud storage services for businesses, developers, IT professionals, and individuals to store, back up, archive, and manage data, host content, and build applications. It operates two main product lines:\n- **B2 Cloud Storage** — an object storage service (competing with Amazon S3, but positioned as a lower-cost alternative) used by developers and businesses to store large amounts of data, media, and application content — this is the fast-growing, strategically important segment\n- **Computer Backup** — its original, legacy consumer/small-business backup product — an older, structurally shrinking part of the business\n\nThe strategic bet driving the current story: Backblaze has positioned B2 as infrastructure for AI workloads, with a newer offering called **B2 Overdrive** specifically aimed at large-scale AI storage needs — essentially, AI models need to store and access enormous datasets, and Backblaze wants to be the affordable storage layer underneath that boom, the same way it originally undercut larger cloud providers on price for traditional storage.\n\n**Why the stock is up huge — genuine, multi-catalyst, beat-and-raise story with an AI-specific customer win:**\n\n1. **A strong Q2 2026 earnings beat (reported August 3, 2026):**\nTotal revenue reached $42.7 million, up 18% year-over-year, driven by B2 Cloud Storage surging 34% to $26.6 million. Non-GAAP EPS came in at $0.08, four times Wall Street's $0.02 estimate, and adjusted EBITDA nearly doubled to $12.8 million.\n\n2. **Guidance raised sharply, well above consensus:**\nBackblaze raised full-year 2026 revenue guidance to $172–174 million (up from a prior forecast of about $162.5 million), and guided Q3 revenue to $44.4–44.8 million, comfortably ahead of the $41.2 million analyst consensus.\n\n3. **A specific, named AI customer deal validated the AI storage thesis:**\nBackblaze disclosed a $335 million multi-year agreement with **CoreWeave** (a major AI cloud/GPU infrastructure company) to support large-scale AI workloads — a concrete, sizable proof point that real AI infrastructure players are choosing Backblaze's storage layer, not just a story about future potential.\n\n4. **Network-level data reinforced the AI narrative:**\nQ2 2026 network stats showed sharply rising AI-related traffic and a 66% jump in CDN (content delivery network) volumes, plus improving hard-drive fleet reliability and rapid adoption of high-capacity 20TB+ drives — all supporting the idea that Backblaze's infrastructure is increasingly built around and used by AI workloads specifically.\n\n5. **A wave of aggressive analyst target hikes:**\nOppenheimer raised its target to $25 from $15, Lake Street to $21 from $14, and Citizens to $21 from $16 (having already raised it to $16 shortly before, citing the CoreWeave deal specifically) — a pattern of analysts repeatedly ratcheting up targets as more good news accumulated.\n\n6. **The stock move has been large and sustained:**\nShares are up roughly 61.76% over the trailing two weeks as of mid-August, with a 52-week range that's gone from a low of $3.26 to a high of $23.99 — an extraordinary multi-month climb, not just a single-day pop.\n\n7. **A near-term catalyst still ahead:** An Investor Day is scheduled for September 9, 2026, giving the market another concrete date to watch for further AI-strategy detail and potential guidance updates.\n\n**What the market is excited about, in one line:** Backblaze is transforming from a niche, budget-friendly backup/storage provider into a legitimate infrastructure layer for the AI boom — proving with a real, named customer contract (CoreWeave) and accelerating B2 growth that AI companies need cheap, reliable, massive-scale storage, and Backblaze can win that business away from bigger, more expensive cloud providers.\n\n**Important caution flags:**\n- Backblaze is still unprofitable — Q2 net loss was about $5.09 million, and the company carries negative return on equity (around -58.5%) and negative return on assets, reflecting heavy reinvestment and stock-based-compensation-driven dilution.\n- Customer concentration is a real, explicitly-flagged risk: with a $335 million CoreWeave deal being such a large driver of the AI narrative, over-reliance on a small number of large AI customers could create volatility if any single relationship changes.\n- The legacy Computer Backup business is structurally shrinking, meaning Backblaze's whole growth thesis depends on B2/AI storage growing fast enough to offset that decline — a "believe the new business can outrun the old business" bet.\n- Valuation is rich by traditional measures: one analyst model (Simply Wall St's more skeptical scenario) pegs fair value around $9.34, implying meaningful downside from where the stock has traded, versus the bullish case built on continued AI-driven acceleration — a reminder that even with real fundamental improvement, a lot of future growth is already priced into the current stock price after this run.	2026-08-16 22:08:08.249069-04	2026-08-16 22:08:08.249069-04
